\id ISA EN_UST en_English_ltr Thu Apr 30 2026 06:52:16 GMT-0400 (Eastern Daylight Time) tc
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Isaiah
\toc1 The Book of Isaiah
\toc2 Isaiah
\toc3 Isa
\mt1 Isaiah
\c 1
\p
\v 1 These are things that Yahweh revealed to Isaiah son of Amoz about Judah and Jerusalem. Isaiah spoke about them during the times when Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz, and Hezekiah ruled as kings of Judah.
\q1 \v 2 Isaiah told the sky and the land{, as if they could hear,}
\q1 “It is as if the Israelites were my own children
\q2 who rebelled against me after I raised them.
\q1 \v 3 Even an ox knows who its owner is,
\q2 and a donkey knows where its master feeds it,
\q1 but the Israelites do not know me.
\q2 They do not recognize {how they ought to relate to me}.”
\b
\q1 \v 4 How terrible it will be for this nation that keeps sinning!
\q2 These people commit so many crimes!
\q1 They do evil, destructive things just as their ancestors did.
\q1 They do not consider it important to obey Yahweh,
\q2 the holy God of the Israelites.
\q2 Instead, they treat him as if he were a stranger.
\q1 \v 5 You Judeans are like a person whom someone has beaten all over his body.
\q2 This should make you realize that you need to stop rebelling {against Yahweh}.
\q1 You are like a person {whom someone has been beaten so badly that} he can no longer think clearly
\q2 and who has completely lost his courage.
\q1 \v 6 You are like a person whose whole body is not healthy anywhere.
\q2 It is as if you have many wounds, bruises, and open sores
\q2 that no one has cleaned out, rubbed with oil, or put a bandage on.
\q1 \v 7 Foreign armies have made your land desolate
\q2 and have set your cities on fire.
\q1 Right in front of you,
\q2 foreigners are eating up everything your ground produces,
\q2 and they have left it as desolate as a place that enemies have destroyed.
\q1 \v 8 Like a temporary shelter that someone would set up {at harvest time}
\q2 in a vineyard or a cucumber field,
\q1 Jerusalem is the only Judean city whose walls are still standing,
\q2 and enemies have surrounded it and are trying to destroy it.
\q1 \v 9 If Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 had not let a few of us survive,
\q1 enemies would have completely destroyed all of our cities,
\q2 just as he completely destroyed the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah.
\b
\q1 \v 10 Listen to what Yahweh our God wants to teach you,
\q2 you rulers and people who are as wicked
\q2 as the rulers and people of Sodom and Gomorrah!
\q1 \v 11 Yahweh says, “No matter how many sacrifices you offer to me,
\q2 that will not impress me.
\q1 You have sacrificed more than enough rams as burnt offerings to me,
\q2 and you have offered me more than enough fat from animals you have fed well.
\q1 The blood of bulls, lambs, and male goats
\q2 does not please me at all.
\q1 \v 12 I never asked you to come and worship me
\q2 by leading in so many animals
\q2 and trampling around with them in the courtyard of my temple!
\q1 \v 13 These offerings mean nothing to me, so stop bringing them!
\q2 The incense that you burn disgusts me.
\q1 You keep doing evil things even though you attend religious gatherings,
\q2 so I detest your New Moon festivals and Sabbath assemblies.
\q1 \v 14 I hate it when you gather for your New Moon festivals
\q2 and for the festivals that I appointed for you to observe each year.
\q1 It is as if they have become a heavy load
\q2 that I am tired of carrying.
\q1 \v 15 When you pray to me, lifting up your hands,
\q2 I do not look at you.
\q1 Even when you pray many times,
\q2 I do not listen to you.
\q1 That is because you have committed violent crimes.
\q2 It is as if you still have blood stains on your hands.
\q1 \v 16 I can see the evil things that you are doing.
\q2 Stop doing them,
\q2 as if you were thoroughly washing that blood off your hands.
\q1 \v 17 Learn to do things that are right.
\q2 Make sure that people treat each other fairly.
\q1 Give a better life to people whom others are treating cruelly.
\q2 Legally defend {helpless people such as} orphans and widows.”
\b
\q1 \v 18 Yahweh says, “Let us now settle this matter between us.
\q1 You have committed so many violent crimes
\q2 that it is as if you have blood stains on your hands
\q2 that are as bright red as scarlet cloth or crimson dye.
\q1 But I can forgive you and make you good again
\q2 so that it will be as if your hands are as white as snow or wool.
\q1 \v 19 If you obey me willingly,
\q2 you will eat good food that the land produces.
\q1 \v 20 But if you refuse to obey me and you continue to rebel against me,
\q2 enemy armies will destroy you.”
\q1 This will surely happen because Yahweh himself has said that it will happen.
\b
\q1 \v 21 The people of Jerusalem have changed so much!
\q2 They used to worship Yahweh faithfully,
\q1 but now they worship many different gods.
\q2 They are acting the way a prostitute does.
\q1 In that city, people formerly treated one another fairly and did what was right.
\q2 But now, they commit murder.
\q1 \v 22 The people of Jerusalem used to be pure, like silver,
\q2 but now they are corrupt, like silver mixed with cheaper metals.
\q1 The people of that city used to be strong, like pure wine,
\q2 but now they are weak, like wine that someone has mixed with water.
\q1 \v 23 The leaders of the city disobey Yahweh,
\q2 and they join others in stealing from people.
\q2 They accept bribes and ask for gifts {to make unfair decisions}.
\q1 They do not help orphans and widows
\q2 by considering their legal cases and deciding them fairly.
\q1 \v 24 Yahweh is the Lord, the commander of the heavenly armies.
\q2 He is the mighty God whom the Israelites should worship.
\q2 This is what he declares about these things:
\q1 “I am going to punish the people who are acting
\q2 so contrary to the way I want people to act.
\q2 This will satisfy me. But how terrible it will be for them!
\q1 \v 25 I will use my power against those people.
\q2 By punishing them, I will make Jerusalem pure again,
\q1 just as people use lye to help them melt precious metals
\q2 so they can remove impurities and base metals from them.
\q1 \v 26 Then I will give you good judges and wise advisors,
\q2 such as you had before.
\q1 After I do that, people will describe Jerusalem as
\q2 ‘the city where people do what is right’
\q2 and ‘the city whose people are faithful to Yahweh.’”
\q1 \v 27 Yahweh will rescue the people of Jerusalem who sincerely repent,
\q2 by doing what is right and fair.
\q1 \v 28 However, he will destroy people who rebel and keep sinning.
\q2 Then there will be no people in the city who have abandoned Yahweh.
\q1 \v 29 Then anyone who went to sacred oak trees or groves to worship idols
\q2 will feel great shame for having done that.
\q1 \v 30 My opponents will be like an oak tree whose leaves are drying up
\q2 or a garden that has no water{,
\q2 and when I punish them, it will be as if I am burning them up}.
\v 31 The strongest people among you will be like dry flax fiber {that burns easily},
\q2 and it will be as if the idols they have made are a spark that sets them on fire.
\q1 It will be as if they and their idols burn up together
\q2 and no one puts out the fire.
\c 2
\p
\v 1 This is the message that Yahweh revealed to Isaiah son of Amoz about Judah and Jerusalem.
\b
\q1 \v 2 In the future, the mountain where Yahweh’s temple is
\q2 will be the most important place on earth.
\q2 It will be as if it is higher than any other mountain or hill.
\q1 So many people from many nations will come there,
\q2 it will be as if they are a great river flowing there.
\q1 \v 3 People from many groups will say {to each other},
\q2 “Come on, let us go to the mountain where Yahweh is,
\q1 to the temple where we can worship the God whom Jacob worshiped.
\q1 There Yahweh will teach us how he wants us to live,
\q2 and we will obey him.”
\q1 Yes, people who go to Mount Zion in Jerusalem
\q2 will learn what Yahweh wants them to do,
\q2 and they will go from there and teach other people.
\q1 \v 4 Yahweh will settle disputes between the people of many nations.
\q1 Then those peoples will reshape their implements of war
\q2 into implements for farming.
\q1 {Armies of} nations will no longer fight against {armies of} other nations,
\q2 and people will no longer train to be soldiers.
\q1 \v 5 You descendants of Jacob, come on,
\q2 let us live in the way Yahweh has shown us we should live!
\b
\q1 \v 6 Yahweh, you have now abandoned your people, the descendants of Jacob,
\q2 because they have adopted pagan practices that they have learned from eastern nations.
\q1 They also practice divination as the Philistines do,
\q2 and they join foreigners in ritually clapping at pagan ceremonies.
\q1 \v 7 The descendants of Jacob have great quantities of silver and gold in their land.
\q2 It is as if their wealth has no limit.
\q1 They also have great quantities of horses in their land,
\q2 and it is as if they have more chariots than anyone can count.
\q1 \v 8 But they also have great quantities of idols in their land.
\q2 They bow down to things that they themselves have made.
\q1 \v 9 But Yahweh will humble the common people and their leaders
\q2 by making them desperate instead of prosperous.
\q2 Yahweh, do not restore them {until you have thoroughly corrected them}!
\q1 \v 10 Yahweh will make you Judeans very afraid
\q2 when he demonstrates that he rules with great glory.
\q1 You will want to hide in rocky caves
\q2 or in holes in the ground to escape from him.
\q1 \v 11 Right now the common people and their leaders have a very proud attitude.
\q2 But Yahweh will humble those people.
\q1 After that, only Yahweh will receive honor.
\b
\q1 \v 12 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, has decided that on a certain day
\q2 he will act to humble people who are proud and who think too highly of themselves.
\q1 \v 13 He will act against people who seem to be as tall and majestic
\q2 as the cedars of Lebanon and the oaks of Bashan.
\q1 \v 14 He will act against people who are very proud,
\q2 as if they were as high as tall mountains or lofty hills.
\q1 \v 15 He will act against people who believe they are safe
\q2 because their cities have strong walls and high defensive towers.
\q1 \v 16 He will act against people who are proud of their ships that can sail long distances
\q2 and of their expensive boats.
\q1 \v 17 Right now the common people and their leaders are very proud,
\q2 but Yahweh will humble those people.
\q1 After that, only Yahweh will receive honor.
\q1 \v 18 People will get rid of all of their idols.
\q1 \v 19 People will hide in rocky caves
\q2 and in holes in the ground
\q1 because Yahweh will make them very afraid
\q2 when he demonstrates that he rules with great glory.
\q2 What he does will terrify the people who live on the earth.
\q1 \v 20 When that happens, people will throw away their golden and silver idols
\q2 that people crafted so that they could worship them.
\q1 They will throw them into holes in the ground where moles live
\q2 and into caves where bats live.
\q1 \v 21 Then they will crawl into narrow spaces among rocks
\q2 because Yahweh will make them very afraid
\q1 when he demonstrates that he rules with great glory.
\q2 What he does will terrify the people who live on the earth.
\q1 \v 22 Stop trusting in human beings.
\q2 They are only alive because God breathed life into them.
\q2 They are not of great value.
\c 3
\q1 \v 1 Pay attention! Yahweh the Lord, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 is about to take away the food and drink
\q2 that the people of Jerusalem and Judah depend on.
\q1 \v 2 He will also take away {the people they depend on,} their heroes and soldiers,
\q2 their judges and prophets,
\q2 their fortunetellers and elders,
\q1 \v 3 their military officers and important officials,
\q2 their advisors, skilled craftsmen,
\q2 and clever magicians.
\q1 \v 4 Yahweh says, “I will make mere youths their leaders,
\q2 and immature people will make foolish decisions about them.”
\q1 \v 5 People will treat each other cruelly;
\q2 they will fight with one another, even with their neighbors.
\q1 Young people will treat older people disrespectfully,
\q2 and insignificant people will mock those they should honor.
\q1 \v 6 At that time, people will go to one of their relatives and say,
\q2 “You have a coat{, so you must have some resources}.
\q1 You be our leader!
\q2 We will give you the authority to govern our community,
\q2 which is in a very bad situation.”
\q1 \v 7 But that relative will protest and say,
\q2 “No, I cannot fix this situation.
\q1 I do not have any food or a coat in my house.
\q2 So do not make me your leader!”
\b
\q1 \v 8 The people of Jerusalem and Judah are saying and doing things
\q2 that offend Yahweh right in his glorious presence.
\q1 As a result, their society has increasingly worse problems.
\q1 \v 9 The look on their faces shows that they are guilty
\q2 of sins as bad as the ones that the people of Sodom committed.
\q2 But they sin openly, without trying to hide what they are doing.
\q1 How terrible it will be for them!
\q2 God will punish them as they deserve.
\q1 \v 10 Tell righteous people that things will go well for them.
\q2 They will experience the good results of what they have done.
\q1 \v 11 But how terrible it will be for wicked people!
\q2 It will be terrible because God will punish them as they deserve for the evil things they have done.
\q1 \v 12 My people now have immature and inexperienced leaders
\q2 who are oppressing them.
\q1 Those leaders of my people are telling them to do the wrong things.
\q2 They are not providing good direction for them.
\q1 \v 13 Yahweh is ready to judge his people,
\q2 as if he had taken his place as a prosecutor in a courtroom.
\q1 \v 14 Yahweh brings charges against the elders and the rulers of his people.
\q1 He says, “Your houses are full of things that you have stolen from poor people.
\q2 It is as if my people were a vineyard and you had stolen all the grapes it produced.
\q1 \v 15 How dare you oppress my people who are poor
\q2 as if you were grinding their faces into the dirt!”
\q1 This is what Yahweh the Lord, the commander of the heavenly armies, declares.
\b
\q1 \v 16 Yahweh also says,
\q2 “{I am going to punish} the women of Jerusalem because they are vain.
\q1 They hold their heads high
\q2 and flirt with their eyes.
\q1 They walk with short, dainty steps
\q2 and jingle the bracelets on their ankles.
\q1 \v 17 To punish them, I, the Lord will make sores appear on their scalps,
\q2 and I, Yahweh, will make them go bald.”
\p
\v 18 When Yahweh does that, he will also strip away all the things the women of Jerusalem use to make themselves beautiful. He will take away their ankle ornaments, their headbands, their crescent-shaped necklaces,
\v 19 their earrings, bracelets, and veils,
\v 20 their scarves, ankle chains, sashes, perfume containers, and charms,
\v 21 their signet rings and nose rings,
\v 22 their fine robes, capes, cloaks, and purses,
\v 23 and their mirrors, linen garments, turbans, and shawls.
\q1 \v 24 Instead of smelling sweet like perfume, they will stink.
\q2 Instead of wearing sashes, they will have ropes around their waists.
\q1 Instead of having fancy hair, they will have bald heads.
\q2 Instead of wearing fine robes, they will wear rough sackcloth.
\q2 Instead of being beautiful, they will have scars from branding {as captives}.
\q1 \v 25 The strong men of Jerusalem will die in battle.
\q1 \v 26 People at the gates of the city will mourn and cry.
\q2 Enemies will plunder the city.
\q1 It will be as if Jerusalem is a desolate woman
\q2 sitting alone on the ground.
\c 4
\p
\v 1 When that happens, {because few men will be alive,} seven women will grab one man and say to him, “Allow us all to marry you! We will provide our own food and our own clothing. All we want is not to have disgrace for not having husbands.”
\p
\v 2 A time will come when Yahweh causes beautiful and glorious crops to grow to feed the Israelites who have survived. They will be proud of the good things that their land produces, and people will admire them for those things.
\v 3 Yahweh will have written in his book the names of the people who would survive and keep living in Jerusalem. So people will recognize that all those who remain in that city on Mount Zion are holy.
\v 4 They will be holy because the Lord will have judged the people and purified them, as if he had burned away their impurities. He will make the women who live in Jerusalem good again, as if he had washed dirt off them. He will make Jerusalem a peaceful place again instead of a violent one, as if he were washing away the blood stains of the people whom others murdered there.
\v 5 Yahweh will create a cloud of smoke during the day to cover every place on Mount Zion and all the people who gather there. At night, he will cover them with a blazing fire. He will put a covering over that entire glorious place {to protect it}.
\v 6 This covering will protect the people from the heat of the sun during the day, and it will shelter them when there are rain storms.
\c 5
\q1 \v 1 Now I am going to sing a song for my dear friend,
\q2 about him and his vineyard.
\q2 He had a vineyard on a very fertile hillside.
\q1 \v 2 My friend plowed the ground and cleared away the stones.
\q2 Then he planted very good grapevines on that ground.
\q1 In the middle of the vineyard, he built a watchtower,
\q2 and he dug a pit for squeezing the grapes.
\q1 He expected to harvest good grapes,
\q2 but the vines produced only sour grapes.
\q1 \v 3 Now this is what Yahweh says:
\q2 “You people who live in Jerusalem and other places in Judah,
\q1 I am like the friend, and you are like his vineyard;
\q2 now judge which of us has done what is right.
\q1 \v 4 What more could I have done for you
\q2 than what I have already done?
\q1 I expected you to do good deeds,
\q2 so you should not have done evil deeds,
\q2 like the vineyard that produced only sour grapes!
\q1 \v 5 So I am telling you now that I will no longer protect Judah,
\q2 the place that is like my vineyard.
\q1 It will be as if I am cutting down and burning up
\q2 the hedge around a vineyard {that kept out small animals}.
\q1 It will be as if I am scattering the stones from the wall around a vineyard
\q2 {that kept out large animals} so that they can walk over the stones and eat the fruit.
\q1 \v 6 It will be as if I make that vineyard a place where no useful crops grow,
\q2 where no one prunes the vines or hoes the soil,
\q1 where weeds grow wildly,
\q2 and on which rain does not fall from the clouds.”
\q1 \v 7 The Israelites are like a vineyard that belongs to Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies.
\q2 The people of Judah are like a garden that he has carefully tended.
\q1 He expected them to act justly,
\q2 but instead, they murdered others.
\q1 He expected them to do what was right,
\q2 but instead, he heard people crying for help because others were attacking them violently.
\b
\q1 \v 8 How terrible it will be for people who acquire the houses and lands around them
\q1 so that others have no place to live
\q2 and they themselves live alone in the land.
\p
\v 9 I heard Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, solemnly swear,
\q1 “Some day, many of those huge houses will be empty;
\q2 no one will be living in those beautiful mansions.
\q1 \v 10 The vines on ten acres of land will produce only about 22 liters of juice,
\q2 and someone who plants 220 liters of seed will harvest only 22 liters of grain.”
\b
\q1 \v 11 How terrible it will be for people who get up early each morning
\q2 to start drinking beer,
\q1 and who stay awake into the night
\q2 drinking wine until they are completely drunk.
\q1 \v 12 They hold big parties and serve plenty of wine.
\q2 At those parties, people play harps and lyres and tambourines and flutes.
\q1 But they do not recognize or understand
\q2 what Yahweh is doing.
\q1 \v 13 Because the Israelites do not know him,
\q2 enemies will carry them away as captives to another land.
\q1 Both the leaders and the common people
\q2 will be very hungry and thirsty.
\q1 \v 14 It is as if the place where dead people go is making more room for them
\q2 and is eager to have as many of them as possible.
\q1 Both the leaders and the common people will go down into that place,
\q2 all those people who liked to have fun carousing.
\q1 \v 15 Both the common people and their leaders will no longer be so arrogant.
\q2 People who now are proud will have a humbler attitude.
\q1 \v 16 But people will praise Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, because he acts justly.
\q2 God is holy, and when he does what is right, people will recognize that he is holy.
\q1 \v 17 Then lambs will eat grass as they usually do,
\q2 and wandering animals will graze on land that is now desolate
\q2 but on which people formerly fattened their animals.
\b
\q1 \v 18 How terrible it will be for people who work hard to keep sinning,
\q2 as if their sin were a heavy cart that they were pulling with ropes
\q1 and as if the lies they tell to maintain their sin were those ropes.
\q1 \v 19 Those people say about the holy God of the Israelites,
\q2 “If he is going to punish us, let him do that right away.
\q1 Let him do what he has decided to do.
\q2 We would like to know what this punishment is actually going to be.”
\b
\q1 \v 20 How terrible it will be for people who say
\q2 that evil actions are good actions and that good actions are evil actions.
\q1 It is as if they are saying that darkness is light and that light is darkness
\q2 or that bitter things are sweet and sweet things are bitter.
\b
\q1 \v 21 How terrible it will be for people who think that they are wise
\q2 and consider themselves to be clever.
\b
\q1 \v 22 How terrible it will be for people who think that they are heroes
\q2 because they are able to drink much wine
\q2 and who boast about being able to mix things into beer to make it stronger.
\q1 \v 23 Those people accept bribes to let guilty people go free,
\q2 and they punish innocent people instead.
\b
\q1 \v 24 Therefore, just as flames of fire burn up stubble
\q2 and dry grass shrivels up in flames,
\q1 it will be as though those people are plants whose roots have rotted
\q2 and whose flowers have withered.
\q1 This will happen because they rejected the law of Yahweh,
\q2 the commander of the heavenly armies.
\q1 The holy God of the Israelites
\q2 told them what he wanted them to do, but they ignored him.
\q1 \v 25 As a result, Yahweh is extremely angry with his people.
\q2 He has already used his power to punish them.
\q2 He punished them so severely that it was as if the mountains shook.
\q1 So many people died that others could not bury them all
\q2 and their bodies remained in the streets as if they were garbage.
\q1 Even so, Yahweh continues to be very angry,
\q2 and he is ready to use his power to punish his people further.
\b
\q1 \v 26 Yahweh will summon the armies of distant nations
\q2 as if he were raising a battle flag to gather them
\q1 or as if he were whistling for them so loudly
\q2 that they could hear him even though they were very far away.
\q1 A foreign army will come very rapidly {to attack Jerusalem}.
\q1 \v 27 All its soldiers will be strong and capable.
\q2 They will not need to sleep very much.
\q1 All of their equipment will be in good condition{,
\q2 {so they will be ready to fight in battles}.
\q1 \v 28 They will have sharpened their arrows,
\q2 and they will have their bows ready to shoot those arrows.
\q1 The horses in this army have strong hooves that do not break,
\q2 as if they were stone,
\q1 and it has many fast chariots whose wheels stir up great clouds of dust,
\q2 as if there were a whirlwind.
\q1 \v 29 The sound of this army approaching will be like the roar of a great lion.
\q2 A lion growls and then pounces on an animal and carries it away,
\q2 and no one can rescue that animal.
\q1 \v 30 In the same way, this army will make a great sound as it approaches to attack,
\q2 like the loud sound that the waves of the sea make when they crash onto the land.
\q1 When this army arrives, if someone looks across the land,
\q2 he will see the army darkening it like the shadow of a cloud,
\q2 and he will see the people suffering great distress.
\c 6
\p
\v 1 During the year when King Uzziah died, I had a vision of the Lord. He was sitting on a very high throne. He was wearing a robe, and the bottom of it spread out and covered the floor of the temple.
\v 2 Shining creatures were standing above the Lord. Each one had six wings. Each creature covered his face with two wings, covered his body with two wings, and flew with the other two wings.
\v 3 They shouted to each other and said,
\q1 “Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, is absolutely holy!
\q2 His glory fills the whole earth!”
\p
\v 4 When they shouted, the whole building shook, so that the heavy doors swayed in their sockets. Smoke filled the temple.
\p
\v 5 Then I cried out, “Something terrible is going to happen to me! I am going to die! Now that I have seen Yahweh, the great King, the commander of the heavenly armies, I realize that I do not speak purely, and I am part of a community of people who do not speak purely.”
\v 6 Then one of the shining creatures picked up a burning coal from the altar with a pair of tongs. He flew to me with it.
\v 7 He touched the coal to my lips and said, “Listen, this coal from the altar has touched your lips, so God has forgiven all of your sins.”
\p
\v 8 Then I heard the Lord ask, “Whom should I send {as a messenger to my people}? Who will go and speak for all of us?” I answered, “I will go! Send me!”
\p
\v 9 Then he said, “Go and say this to these people,
\q1 ‘You will listen carefully to God’s message, but you will not understand it.
\q2 You will consider carefully what God is doing, but you will not know why he is doing it.’
\q1 \v 10 Speak my message to these people even though, in response,
\q2 they will become less willing to obey me
\q2 and less able to understand what I want them to do.
\q1 If they did respond differently, they would understand what I wanted them to do,
\q2 they would be willing to obey me, and they would repent.
\q2 Then I would help them.”
\p
\v 11 Then I asked, “How long must I continue to do that, Lord?”
\q1 He replied, “You must do it until enemies destroy their cities
\q2 and no one lives in the houses in them anymore.
\q2 Do that until enemies have also completely ruined all the farmland.
\q1 \v 12 Do that until I allow enemies to take the people away to other places
\q2 and large areas in the land of Israel are empty.
\p
\v 13 Even if one tenth of the people survive, enemies will come back and destroy them. They will do that in the same way that people burn the stump of a large tree that they have cut down. But I will set apart a small group of survivors from whom the nation can start growing again.”
\c 7
\p
\v 1 When Ahaz son of Jotham and grandson of Uzziah was the king of Judah, King Rezin of Aram and King Pekah son of Remaliah of Israel marched with their armies to attack Jerusalem. However, they were not able to conquer it.
\v 2 Someone reported to David’s descendant Ahaz that the army of Aram had joined forces with the army of Israel. This terrified Ahaz and his people. They were trembling the way trees shake when a wind blows through a forest.
\p
\v 3 Then Yahweh said to Isaiah, “Take your son Shear-Jashub and go out to meet Ahaz. You will find him on the road that leads to the field where people wash their clothes. He will be at the end of the channel that brings water to the upper pool.
\v 4 Tell him, ‘Stay in control of yourself and do not panic. Do not be afraid, and do not lose your resolve. Rezin, the Aramean king, and Pekah son of Remaliah, the Israelite king, are attacking you violently. But they will not be able to harm you, any more than the ends of two logs that had mostly burned up and were just smoking could start a fire.
\v 5 It is true that the king of Aram has made a plan with the king of Israel to attack you. They have agreed,
\v 6 “Let us go attack the people of Judah and terrify them. We will break through their defenses, and we will make the son of Tabeel their king.”
\p
\v 7 But this is what I, Yahweh, the Lord, say: “Their plan will not succeed. What they are saying will not happen.
\v 8-9 That is because the kingdom of Aram is no stronger than its capital city, Damascus. The city of Damascus is no stronger than its king, Rezin{, and he is a mere human being}. The kingdom of Israel is no stronger than its capital city, Samaria. The city of Samaria is no stronger than its king, Pekah son of Remaliah {, and he is a mere human being}. Within 65 years, enemies will completely destroy Israel so that it no longer exists as a nation. But you people must trust me if you want your kingdom to survive.”’”
\p
\v 10 Yahweh spoke again to Ahaz {through Isaiah} and said,
\v 11 “Ask Yahweh your God to do something to show you that what he is saying is true. You may ask him to do something anywhere in his entire creation.”
\v 12 Ahaz replied, “No, I will not ask for anything. I will not challenge Yahweh to prove that what he is saying is true.”
\p
\v 13 Then Isaiah said, “Listen, you descendant of David! People are already tired of you {for being afraid and not showing any resolve}. Now you are also making my God tired of you!
\v 14 Therefore, the Lord himself will do something to show all of you that what he is saying is true. Listen! A young woman will become pregnant, and she will give birth to a son. She will name him Immanuel.
\v 15 Before that child is old enough to know right from wrong, he will be eating curds and honey.
\v 16 That will happen because, before that boy is old enough to know right from wrong, no one will live anymore in the lands of the two kings whom you fear so much.
\p
\v 17 Yahweh is going to make a disaster happen to you, your family, and the whole nation that will be worse than anything that has happened since the time when Israel separated from Judah. The king of Assyria is going to attack you.”
\p
\v 18 At that time, Yahweh will cause the army of Egypt to come and invade this land. The Egyptian leaders will be from the Upper Nile region. He will also cause the army of Assyria to come from its land. Those armies will be like swarms of flies and bees.
\v 19 They will come and occupy every part of this land, as if those flies and bees had settled in the steep valleys and in the cracks of the rocks, on all the thorn bushes, and at all the places where people get water.
\p
\v 20 At that time, the Lord will allow the Assyrian army to come from the other side of the Euphrates River. They will take away your crops and valuable possessions. They will leave the land empty, as if someone had used a razor to shave off someone’s beard and remove the hair from his entire body.
\p
\v 21 After that, a person will only be able to keep alive a young cow and two sheep.
\v 22 However, those animals will produce enough milk that their owner will have curds to eat. In fact, everyone who remains in the land will eat curds and honey.
\p
\v 23 At that time, in the places where people once had a thousand grape vines worth a thousand pieces of silver, only briars and thorn bushes will grow.
\v 24 Briars and thorn bushes will cover the whole land, so people will go to those places {not to grow crops but} to hunt with bows and arrows.
\v 25 Briars and thorn bushes will grow on the hillsides that farmers once cultivated with hoes. So people will not go there, because they will be afraid that the briars and thorns will cut them. Instead, those hills will become places where people let their cattle roam and where sheep trample the ground.
\c 8
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told me, “Get a large tablet and write on it in big letters, ‘For Maher Shalal Hash Baz.’”
\v 2 I asked two trustworthy men to serve as witnesses {that I had written on the tablet at this time}, Uriah the high priest and Zechariah son of Jeberechiah.
\p
\v 3 Then I has sexual relations with my wife, the prophetess, and she became pregnant and gave birth to a son. Then Yahweh told me, “Name him Maher-Shalal-Hash-Baz,
\v 4 because before this boy is old enough to say ‘papa’ or ‘mama,’ the king of Assyria will come and take away all the valuable things in the cities of Damascus and Samaria.”
\p
\v 5 Yahweh spoke to me again and said,
\v 6 “These people should have trusted me, even though I work in ways that are hard to recognize, like the water that flows quietly through the Shiloah canal into Jerusalem. Instead, many of them are glad that King Rezin of Aram and King Pekah of Israel have invaded Judah {to try to force the Judeans to join them in opposing Assyria}. So I am going to do something in response.
\v 7 Because they have not trusted me, I, the Lord, will allow the army of the king of Assyria to invade Judah. That army is large and powerful, like the waters of the Euphrates River, So many soldiers will come that it will be as if that river had flooded and overflowed all of its banks.
\v 8 It will be as if that flood had swept into Judah and covered the land so that only people’s heads were above water. Just as an eagle spreads out its wings, this army will spread out over the country in which Immanuel will be living.”
\q1 \v 9 Listen, all you people in distant countries!
\q2 Go ahead and prepare for battle,
\q1 but Yahweh will enable the Assyrians to destroy you!
\q1 \v 10 Go ahead and make your plans to attack,
\q2 but God will make those plans fail!
\q1 Say what you want to say,
\q2 but nothing you plan will succeed,
\q1 because God will help us!
\p
\v 11 I say this because Yahweh spoke to me very strongly and warned me not to act like other people in Judah. He said this to me:
\q1 \v 12 “Do not believe that secret plots are happening just because people say they are.
\q2 Do not be afraid at all of the things that those people are so afraid of.
\q1 \v 13 Instead, you should have a special regard for me,
\q2 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies.
\q2 You should be most concerned about respecting me and not offending me.
\q1 \v 14 I will keep you safe.
\q1 However, for the people of the kingdoms of Israel and Judah,
\q2 including the people who live in Jerusalem,
\q2 I will be like a stone that they trip over
\q2 and like a trap that catches them.
\q1 \v 15 It will be as if many of those people trip over that stone
\q2 and fall and never get up again.
\q1 It will be as if that trap catches them
\q2 when their enemies capture them and take them away.”
\q1 \v 16 So I, Isaiah, want someone to keep a record of what God has said to me.
\q2 Preserve his instructions and give them to my disciples.
\q1 \v 17 I will wait to see what Yahweh will do.
\q1 Even though he has stopped favoring the descendants of Jacob,
\q2 I will trust him confidently.
\q1 \v 18 Listen, I and the children whom Yahweh has given me are warning signs for the people of Israel.
\q2 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, who is present in his temple on Mount Zion, is using us for that purpose.
\p
\v 19 Some people say that we should try to contact people who have died or talk to spirits {to find out what to do}. But they just mumble things in squeaky voices. Yahweh is the God of the Judeans, and they should ask him what to do. It makes no sense for people who are alive to ask dead people to tell them what to do!
\v 20 Pay attention to the instructions that God has given by speaking to me! If people urge us to do something different, they cannot expect good things to happen for them.
\v 21 Those people will wander through the land, destitute and hungry. When they become very hungry, they will become very angry. They will curse their king and their God. They will look {all around,} up at the sky
\v 22 and around on the earth, but they will see only distressing, gloomy things that make them feel hopeless. Finally, their enemies will take them away to a place where they will have great trouble.
\c 9
\p
\v 1 However, the people who have been suffering will not continue to suffer. Previously, Yahweh made the people experience great difficulties who lived in {the kingdom of Israel, such as in the territories of} Zebulun and Naphtali. But in the future, he will make the situation much better for the people {of that kingdom, such as those} who live along the road to the sea and in the Galilee region east of the Jordan River, where many foreigners live.
\q1 \v 2 Yahweh will make life much better for those people who were experiencing such distress.
\q2 Yes, he will make life better for those who were living in a land where there was much trouble.
\q1 \v 3 Yahweh, you will cause the nation to grow larger,
\q2 and you will cause its people to be very happy.
\q1 They will rejoice in your presence as people do when they harvest their crops,
\q2 or as soldiers celebrate when they divide among themselves the things that they have captured in battle.
\q1 \v 4 The people will be happy because you will free them from carrying heavy loads,
\q2 and because you will free them from the power of those who have been oppressing them,
\q2 just as you did when you destroyed the army of Midian.
\q1 \v 5 Then people will burn as fuel for their cooking fires
\q2 all the boots that the soldiers were wearing as they marched along
\q2 and all of their garments that got blood stains on them.
\d
\q1 \v 6 We will also rejoice because God will give us a special child.
\q2 A woman will give birth to a son who will be our ruler.
\q1 People will call him “Wonderful Counselor,” “Mighty God,”
\q2 “Everlasting Father,” and “Prince of Peace.”
\q1 \v 7 He will continually rule over more territory, and he will make things go well for his people.
\q2 He will rule as King David did over his kingdom.
\q1 He will make it strong and lasting
\q2 by continually doing what is fair and right.
\q1 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 will make this happen because he very much wants it to happen.
\q1 \v 8 The Lord sent a warning to the descendants of Jacob,
\q2 and those Israelites heard it.
\q1 \v 9-10 The people of the northern kingdom of Israel,
\q2 including those who lived in the city of Samaria,
\q1 knew what he said.
\q2 But they were very proud and arrogant.
\q1 They said, “If our enemies knock down our brick buildings,
\q2 we will build new buildings from stone blocks.
\q1 If they destroy the houses we built from sycamore wood,
\q2 we will build new ones from cedar wood.”
\q1 \v 11 Yahweh influenced the enemies of Rezin to attack {his ally,} Israel.
\q2 Yahweh influenced other foreign kingdoms to attack Israel as well.
\q1 \v 12 In fact, the army of Aram, Israel’s former ally, attacked from the east,
\q2 and the army of Philistia attacked from the west.
\q2 They conquered Israel as if they had been an animal that was gobbling up its prey.
\b
\q1 Even so, Yahweh continues to be very angry,
\q2 and he is ready to use his power to punish his people further.
\b
\q1 \v 13 Even though Yahweh punished the people like that, they still would not worship him.
\q2 They still did not ask Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, to help them.
\q1 \v 14 Therefore, all at once Yahweh will destroy people in Israel of every kind,
\q2 from the important people who are like stately palm trees
\q2 to less important people who are like marsh reeds that sway in the wind.
\q1 \v 15 The important ones are the elders and others whom people honor.
\q2 The least important ones are the false prophets.
\q1 \v 16 The leaders of the people are telling them to do the wrong things.
\q2 As a result, the people they are leading are suffering greatly.
\q1 \v 17 The people do not respect God, and so they do wrong things,
\q2 and they say it is acceptable to do those things.
\q1 Because of that, the Lord does not help the Israelite soldiers win battles,
\q2 and he does not even act mercifully toward widows and orphans.
\b
\q1 Even so, Yahweh continues to be very angry,
\q2 and he is ready to use his power to punish his people further.
\b
\q1 \v 18 The wicked behavior of the people is spreading as if it were a wildfire,
\q2 as if it were burning up briars and thorns and then starting a great fire in the forests,
\q2 from which a great cloud of smoke was rising.
\q1 \v 19 Because Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, is extremely angry,
\q2 he is allowing enemies to devastate the land as if they were burning it up.
\q1 The people are like fuel for a great fire.
\q2 It is as if no one is trying to rescue even his own brother from that fire.
\q1 \v 20 Foreign armies have invaded the country from the east and from the west,
\q2 and no matter how many things they destroy, they keep destroying more things.
\q2 Israelites are even attacking and killing their own kinsmen.
\q1 \v 21 People from Manasseh attack people from Ephraim,
\q2 and people from Ephraim attack people from Manasseh.
\q2 Meanwhile, soldiers from both tribes are attacking the people of Judah.
\b
\q1 Even so, Yahweh continues to be very angry,
\q2 and he is ready to use his power to punish his people further.
\c 10
\q1 \v 1 How terrible it will be for judges who make unjust laws
\q2 and for people who make decrees that cause people to suffer.
\q1 \v 2 You do not give poor people fair judgments;
\q2 no, you deny justice to needy people.
\q2 You allow people to seize what belongs to widows,
\q2 and you rob orphans of their possessions.
\q1 \v 3 You will not be able to do anything {to save yourselves}
\q2 when I send an army from far away to punish you.
\q2 You will not be able to run to anyone for help,
\q2 and you will have nowhere to keep your valuable possessions safe.
\q1 \v 4 You will only be able to stumble along with other prisoners,
\q2 or your dead bodies will fall among those whom others have killed.
\b
\q1 Even so, Yahweh continues to be very angry,
\q2 and he is ready to use his power to punish his people further.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Yahweh says, “How terrible it will be for the Assyrians!
\q2 I use them like a rod and a club to punish other nations
\q2 because I am angry with those nations.
\q1 \v 6 I send them to attack nations that make me angry
\q2 because they do not honor me.
\q2 I send them to take away the valuable possessions that people have
\q2 and to subdue them as if they were walking on mud in the streets.
\q1 \v 7 However, the king of Assyria does not understand
\q2 that he is only a weapon that I am using.
\q1 He does not plan to act that way.
\q2 Instead, he wants to destroy many nations completely.
\q1 \v 8 He boasts and says, ‘I have conquered many kings,
\q2 and now they all lead their soldiers as commanders in my army.
\q1 \v 9 We conquered Calno just as we conquered Carchemish.
\q2 We conquered Hamath just as we conquered Arpad.
\q2 We conquered Samaria just as we conquered Damascus.
\q1 \v 10 The kingdoms I conquered each worshiped their own gods,
\q2 and their idols were more impressive than the ones in Jerusalem and Samaria.
\q1 \v 11 I defeated Samaria and its gods,
\q2 and I will certainly do the same thing to Jerusalem and its gods!’
\p
\v 12 However, when the Lord finishes everything he plans to do to discipline the people who live in Jerusalem on Mount Zion {and elsewhere in Judah}, he will punish the king of Assyria, because that king is very proud and looks at everyone with arrogant contempt.
\q1 \v 13 That king has said, ‘I have done all this by my own great power.
\q2 I have accomplished this because I am so wise and intelligent.
\q1 I have made the territories of nations my own territory.
\q2 I have captured the treasures that belonged to those nations.
\q1 Because I am so powerful, I have defeated kings,
\q2 so that they no longer sit on their thrones.
\q1 \v 14 I took the wealth of other kingdoms
\q2 as easily as someone reaches into a bird’s nest.
\q2 I gathered treasures from throughout the world
\q2 as easily as someone gathers eggs that a bird has abandoned,
\q2 as if no bird flapped its wings or called out or chirped to stop me.’
\q1 \v 15 An axe cannot boast that it is stronger than the person who chops with it!
\q2 A saw cannot claim to be greater than the person who moves it back and forth!
\q2 That would be like a rod waving around the person who picks it up,
\q2 or like a wooden club lifting up the person who carries it!
\q1 \v 16 As a result, Yahweh, the Lord, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 will cause the strong warriors of Assyria to die from a disease that makes them very thin.
\q1 He will destroy the things that they are so proud of
\q2 as if he were burning them up in a fire.
\q1 \v 17 Yahweh, the holy God who is like a light for the people of Israel,
\q2 will destroy the Assyrians in a single day.
\q2 He will do that as if they were thorns and briars
\q2 and he was a great fire.
\q1 \v 18 Yahweh will completely destroy the splendid forests and fertile farmlands of Assyria.
\q2 The soil will be bare, with nothing on it,
\q2 just as a disease can make it so that there is almost no flesh on a person’s bones.
\q1 \v 19 So few trees will remain in the forests of Assyria
\q2 that even a child could count them.”
\b
\q1 \v 20 When that happens, the few people who remain alive in Israel,
\q2 the survivors among Jacob’s descendants,
\q1 will no longer rely on the Assyrians, who tried to destroy them.
\q2 Instead, they will genuinely trust in Yahweh,
\q2 the holy God of the Israelites.
\q1 \v 21 The survivors among Jacob’s descendants will once again worship Yahweh.
\q2 He is the only God who truly has power.
\q1 \v 22 Even though the people of Israel now seem to be as numerous
\q2 as the grains of sand on the seashore,
\q2 only a small number of them will return to their land after being captives.
\q1 Yahweh has determined to destroy most of them,
\q2 and he will do that to punish them for doing so many wrong things.
\q1 \v 23 Yes, Yahweh, the Lord, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 has determined to destroy people throughout the world.
\q1 \v 24 As a result, Yahweh, the Lord, the commander of the heavenly armies, says,
\q1 “My people who live in {Jerusalem on Mount} Zion {and in other parts of Judah},
\q2 do not be afraid of the Assyrians
\q1 when they lift up their rods and staffs to hit you
\q2 just as the Egyptians hit your ancestors when they were slaves.
\q1 \v 25 Very soon I will no longer be angry with you,
\q2 and then I will destroy them because I am angry with them.”
\q1 \v 26 Then Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 will defeat the Assyrians as if he were striking them with a whip.
\q2 He will defeat them just as he helped Gideon defeat the Midianites at the rock of Oreb.
\q1 He will defeat them just as he destroyed the Egyptian army
\q2 when he had Moses lift up his staff over the sea.
\q1 \v 27 When that happens, you will no longer be slaves to the Assyrians
\q2 and have to work hard and carry heavy loads for them.
\q2 You will be healthy and strong and free.
\b
\q1 \v 28 The invading Assyrian army has attacked Aiath.
\q2 They have marched through Migron.
\q2 They have stored their equipment at Michmash.
\q1 \v 29 They have crossed through the mountain pass
\q2 and set up their camp at Geba.
\q2 The people of Ramah are trembling with fear.
\q2 The people of Gibeah, King Saul’s hometown, have run away.
\q1 \v 30 The people of Gallim should cry out for help!
\q2 The people of Laishah should listen to them!
\q2 The people of Anathoth are in great danger!
\q1 \v 31 The people of Madmenah are running away,
\q2 and the inhabitants of Gebim are trying to hide.
\q1 \v 32 This very day the invading army will halt at Nob.
\q2 The king who is commanding them will defiantly shake his fist
\q2 to threaten the people who live in Jerusalem on Mount Zion.
\q1 \v 33 Listen! Yahweh, the Lord, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 will defeat the Assyrians with his terrifying power,
\q1 as if he were cutting off the branches of a great tree.
\q2 The Assyrians have great, proud soldiers and commanders,
\q2 but Yahweh will destroy them as if they were tall trees he was cutting down.
\q1 \v 34 They will be like a thick forest that he was cutting down with an iron axe.
\q2 He will destroy them with his great power,
\q2 as if he were chopping down great trees in Lebanon.
\c 11
\q1 \v 1 The dynasty of David son of Jesse
\q2 now seems like the stump of a tree that someone has cut down.
\q1 But one of his descendants will become king again.
\q2 It will be as if a branch had grown from the roots of that tree and produced fruit.
\q1 \v 2 The Spirit of Yahweh will always remain with that king.
\q2 The Spirit will make him wise and understanding.
\q2 The Spirit will enable him to plan well and act powerfully.
\q2 The Spirit will enable him to know Yahweh and to revere him greatly.
\q1 \v 3 He will be very happy to obey Yahweh.
\q2 He will not make decisions based on how people appear
\q2 or on what people tell him.
\q1 \v 4 Instead, he will judge the cases of poor people fairly.
\q2 Yes, he will make good decisions on behalf of needy people throughout his land.
\q1 But as for wicked people, he will give powerful commands to punish them.
\q2 He will even execute them for the harm they have done to other people.
\q1 \v 5 His desire to do what is just and honest
\q2 will equip him to rule well.
\q1 \v 6 {When he becomes king, conditions will be so peaceful that even}
\q2 wolves will live peacefully with lambs.
\q2 Leopards will lie down with young goats.
\q1 Calves and young lions and fat calves will stay together,
\q2 and a small child will lead them.
\q1 \v 7 Cows and bears will eat together;
\q2 their young will lie down beside each other.
\q2 Lions will eat straw the way cattle do.
\q1 \v 8 Babies will play safely near the holes where serpents live.
\q2 Small children will put their hands into the nests of poisonous snakes without getting hurt.
\q1 \v 9 People will no longer do harmful or destructive things
\q2 anywhere on the mountain that Yahweh chose as the place where people would worship him.
\q1 That is because people throughout the earth will know Yahweh,
\q2 just as water completely fills the sea.
\p
\v 10 At that future time, the place where this descendant of Jesse lives will be famous. People of other nations will recognize that they can come to him to get good advice about what to do. They will come to him the way soldiers gather around a signal flag.
\p
\v 11 At that time, the Lord will use his power to bring the survivors of his people back to their land, just as he brought them there before {from Egypt}. He will bring them back from Assyria, Egypt, Pathros, Cush, Elam, Shinar, Hamath, and from lands along the coast of the sea.
\q1 \v 12 He will signal to the nations {that he is restoring his people}.
\q2 He will gather together the Israelites whom enemies carried away into exile.
\q1 He will bring back the people of Judah whom enemies scattered
\q2 to different places throughout the earth.
\q1 \v 13 People in Israel will stop envying the people of Judah,
\q2 and people in Judah will stop being hostile to the people of Israel.
\q1 \v 14 Together, their armies will sweep down the hills to the west and conquer the Philistines.
\q2 They will also defeat the people who live to the east and take their valuable possessions.
\q1 They will conquer Edom and Moab,
\q2 and the Ammonites will obey them.
\q1 \v 15 Yahweh will turn the gulf of the Sea of Egypt into dry land,
\q2 and he will use his power to change the Euphrates River.
\q2 He will make a strong, hot wind blow over it
\q1 so that it splits it into seven shallow streams
\q2 that people can walk across wearing sandals.
\q1 \v 16 Those of his people who still remain in Assyria
\q2 will have an easy road on which to return home.
\q1 It will be like when Yahweh made a path through the Sea of Reeds for the Israelites
\q2 when they left the land of Egypt long before.
\c 12
\q1 \v 1 At that time you will say,
\q1 “Yahweh, I thank you! Previously you were angry with me,
\q2 but now you are no longer angry, and you have comforted me.
\q1 \v 2 Listen, God is the one who saves me!
\q1 Because Yahweh makes me strong and has saved me,
\q1 I sing about him.
\q2 I trust in him, and I am not afraid.
\q1 \v 3 You{, his people,} will joyfully experience what he has done to save you,
\q2 just as people joyfully draw water from a well.
\q1 \v 4 At that time you will tell others,
\q1 “Thank Yahweh! Pray to him!
\q2 Tell the people of other nations what he has done!
\q2 Remind them how he has earned a wonderful reputation!
\q1 \v 5 Sing praises to Yahweh for the glorious things he has done.
\q2 People throughout the world know about them!
\q1 \v 6 You people who live in {the city of Jerusalem on Mount} Zion,
\q2 shout loudly and joyfully {to praise him},
\q1 since the holy God of the Israelites
\q2 lives among you and powerfully helps you!”
\c 13
\p
\v 1 Isaiah son of Amoz received this message from Yahweh about Babylon:
\q1 \v 2 Lift up a flag on the top of a bare hill
\q2 {to signal that an army should come to attack Babylon}!
\q1 Shout to them and wave your hand
\q2 to signal them to advance until they break through the gates
\q2 that the important people use to enter or leave the city.
\q1 \v 3 Yahweh says, “I have set apart some warriors who are glad to help me triumph.
\q2 I am angry with the Babylonians,
\q2 so I have commanded those warriors to attack them.”
\q1 \v 4 Listen! There is a noise on the mountains
\q2 that sounds like a huge army!
\q1 That noise is the sound of the soldiers
\q2 whom Yahweh is gathering from many kingdoms.
\q1 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 is assembling an army for battle.
\q1 \v 5 Those soldiers are coming from distant lands,
\q2 from the farthest places under the sky.
\q1 They are the weapons that Yahweh will use
\q2 to destroy the whole land of Babylonia
\q2 because he is angry with its king and people.
\b
\q1 \v 6 The Babylonians should wail, because Yahweh will act very soon.
\q2 He, Shaddai{, the Almighty God}, will destroy them very powerfully.
\q1 \v 7 Because of this, the Babylonians will lose their courage,
\q2 and they will become weak with fear.
\q1 \v 8 They will feel such terror that
\q2 they will have great anguish constantly.
\q1 They will move about in pain
\q2 as a woman does when she gives birth to a baby.
\q1 They will look at each other in shock,
\q2 and their faces will turn red {with shame and anguish}.
\q1 \v 9 Pay attention! The time when Yahweh acts will be soon.
\q2 He will punish the Babylonians fiercely because he is so angry.
\q1 He will make the land of Babylonia desolate,
\q2 and he will destroy everyone in it who has done wrong.
\q1 \v 10 When that happens, it will be as if
\q2 no stars or constellations shine in the sky at night.
\q2 It will be as if it stays dark even after the sun rises
\q1 and as if the moon does not give any light at night.
\q1 \v 11 Yahweh says, “I will punish the people of the world for the evil things they have done.
\q2 I will punish wicked people for their sins.
\q1 I will stop arrogant people from boasting.
\q2 I will humble proud people who cruelly oppress others.
\q1 \v 12 So many people will die that it will be harder to find a living person
\q2 than to find valuable gold such as gold from the land of Ophir.
\q1 \v 13 To do this, I will shake the sky and the earth
\q2 so that they move from their places.
\q1 That will happen at the time when I, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 {punish the Babylonians} because I am so angry {with them}.”
\q1 \v 14 Then the foreigners living in Babylon will run away,
\q2 as if they were a gazelle that hunters were chasing
\q2 or as if they were a flock of sheep that had no shepherd to control them.
\q1 Each of them will say that he is not Babylonian,
\q2 he belongs to a different group of people,
\q2 and each of them will flee back to his homeland.
\q1 \v 15 The enemy soldiers will not let the Babylonian soldiers escape.
\q2 They will pursue them, catch them, and kill them.
\q1 \v 16 Those enemy soldiers will kill babies
\q2 by hurling them to the ground while their parents watch.
\q1 They will steal the valuable possessions from the houses in Babylon,
\q2 and they will rape the women there.
\b
\q1 \v 17 Yahweh says, “Pay attention! I am going to influence the Medes to attack the Babylonians.
\q2 The Medes will not accept gold or silver as payment to stop them from attacking.
\q1 \v 18 From their powerful bows, they will shoot arrows
\q2 that will smash through the armor of the Babylonian soldiers and kill them.
\q2 They will not spare children or even babies.”
\q1 \v 19 Babylon has been a very glorious kingdom.
\q2 Its people, the Chaldeans, have been proud of how magnificent it is.
\q1 However, God will destroy Babylon completely,
\q2 just as he destroyed the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah.
\q1 \v 20 No one will ever live in Babylon again.
\q2 No, in all future generations, no one will settle there.
\q1 Nomads will not set up their tents there,
\q2 and shepherds will not have their flocks rest there.
\q1 \v 21 Instead, wild desert animals will lie down in its ruins,
\q2 and owls will make their nests throughout its abandoned houses.
\q1 Ostriches will live there,
\q2 and wild goats will leap around among the ruins.
\q1 \v 22 Hyenas will howl in its ruined towers,
\q2 and jackals will make their dens in palaces that used to be beautiful.
\q1 These things will happen soon.
\q2 God will not let Babylon last much longer.
\c 14
\q1 \v 1 This will happen because Yahweh will act mercifully toward the descendants of Jacob.
\q2 He will choose the Israelites to be his people again,
\q1 and he will let them return and live in their land.
\q2 Then foreigners will unite with the Israelite people.
\q1 \v 2 People from other nations will help the Israelites return to their land,
\q2 and the Israelites will make those foreigners serve them on the land that Yahweh gave them.
\q1 The Israelites will capture those who once captured them,
\q2 and they will rule over those who once oppressed them.
\q1 \v 3 Someday Yahweh will relieve you Israelite people from the suffering, distress, and harsh slavery that others forced on you.
\v 4 When that happens, you will mock the king of Babylon by saying,
\q1 “See how he is no longer oppressing us!
\q2 He will not attack us again!
\q1 \v 5 Yahweh has taken away the power of that wicked ruler.
\q1 \v 6 He used to harm other people furiously and continuously.
\q2 He ruled other nations viciously,
\q2 and he did not restrain himself when he persecuted them.
\q1 \v 7 But now people throughout the world are resting peacefully.
\q2 They are singing happy songs.
\q1 \v 8 It is as if even the cypress and cedar trees in Lebanon
\q2 are celebrating the defeat of the king of Babylon.
\q1 They are singing {as if he could hear},
\q2 ‘Now that you are gone, no one comes to cut us down.’
\q1 \v 9 It is as if the place underground where dead people are is excited to meet you when you arrive.
\q2 It is as if that place is waking up the spirits of people who were leaders on earth so they can meet you.
\q2 It is as if it is making those who once ruled over the nations as kings
\q1 stand up from their thrones to greet you.
\q1 \v 10 When you arrive, they will say to you,
\q2 ‘Now you have become as weak as we are!
\q2 You have become just like us!’
\q1 \v 11 You used to be so arrogant, and you lived in luxury,
\q2 but now you have come down here to the place where dead people are.
\q1 Maggots and worms are devouring your corpse.
\q2 They completely surround your body as if they were its bed and blanket.
\b
\q1 \v 12 {King of Babylon,} it is as if you have fallen from the sky!
\q2 You are like the planet Venus{, which rises brightly high into the morning sky
\q2 but then disappears when the sun rises}.
\q1 You once defeated many nations,
\q2 but now it is as if you are a tree that someone has cut down that is lying on the ground.
\q1 \v 13 You said to yourself, ‘I will go up into the sky
\q2 and set up my throne higher than the stars that God made.
\q1 The divine beings gather on a mountain in the far north,
\q2 and I will preside over their gathering.
\q1 \v 14 I will rise above the tops of the clouds
\q2 and make myself equal to the Most High.’
\q1 \v 15 Instead, you have descended to the place where dead people are.
\q2 You are in the deepest part of that underground place.
\q1 \v 16 The dead people there stare at you and look closely at you.
\q1 They say, ‘It is hard to believe that this is the man
\q2 who terrified the people who live on the earth
\q2 and who conquered so many kingdoms!
\q1 \v 17 He devastated the world and tore down cities,
\q1 and he never let prisoners go home{, but look at him now}!’
\q1 \v 18 When the king who rules a nation dies,
\q2 his people bury him honorably in a tomb near his palace.
\q1 \v 19 But no one has buried your body in a grave.
\q2 Instead, people have discarded it,
\q2 the same way they discard a branch that is useless.
\q1 It is as if you died in battle and soldiers first trampled your body {while fighting}
\q2 and then threw it into a pit with a lining of stones
\q2 along with the bodies of many other soldiers who died in battle.
\q1 \v 20 It is your fault that enemies have destroyed your land
\q2 and killed so many of your people.
\q1 So no one will bury you the way people have buried other kings.
\q2 Because you have been so evil, you will not have descendants,
\q2 so people will never remember you.
\q1 \v 21 Because the king of Babylon has been so evil,
\q2 kill his descendants!
\q1 Otherwise, they might become powerful and conquer other lands
\q2 and build cities for themselves throughout the world!”
\q1 \v 22 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, declares,
\q2 “I myself will take action against the people of Babylon.
\q1 I will make sure that they have no survivors or any descendants
\q2 and that no one remembers them.”
\q2 That is what Yahweh declares.
\q1 \v 23 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, declares,
\q2 “I will make Babylon a swampy marshland where herons live.
\q1 I will destroy it so completely
\q2 that it will be as if I had swept it away with a broom.
\b
\q1 \v 24 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, has made this solemn oath:
\q2 “What I have planned will certainly happen,
\q2 and no one will change what I have decided:
\v 25 I will destroy the Assyrians in my land and crush them on my mountains.
\q2 Then my people will no longer be slaves to the Assyrians
\q2 and have to work hard and carry heavy loads for them.
\q1 \v 26 This is the plan that Yahweh has made regarding the whole earth.
\q2 This is how he has decided to use his power against all the foreign kingdoms.
\v 27 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, has decided to do this,
\q2 so no one can stop him.
\q1 He has decided to use his power,
\q2 so no one can resist him.
\p
\v 28 Yahweh gave this message during the year when King Ahaz died:
\q1 \v 29 Do not celebrate, all you Philistines,
\q2 because the king who used to oppress you has died.
\q1 Other kings will succeed him, and they will oppress you even worse,
\q2 as if that king had been a serpent and as if a viper and then a flying serpent
\q2 came from him the way a branch grows from a stump.
\q1 \v 30 The poorest of my people will have food to eat,
\q2 and those who are needy will have safe places to settle.
\q1 However, I will use famine to destroy the population of you Philistines
\q2 so that none of you survive.
\q1 \v 31 If your cities and their gates could cry out in fear, they should,
\q2 and all of you Philistines should be very afraid.
\q1 That is because a great army is coming from the north,
\q2 and it will darken the land like a thick cloud of smoke.
\q2 None of its soldiers leave its ranks and fall behind.
\q1 \v 32 The nation of that army is likely to send messengers
\q2 {to Jerusalem to demand surrender or tribute}.
\q1 When it does, we should tell them that Yahweh has made
\q2 {Jerusalem on Mount} Zion a strong city,
\q2 and his people who live in the countryside can go there and be safe.
\c 15
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave this message about Moab:
\q1 Enemies will destroy the cities of Ar and Kir in Moab in a single night
\q2 and leave them in ruins.
\q1 \v 2 The Moabites will go up to their temple,
\q2 and the people of the city of Dimon will go up to their hilltop shrines to weep.
\q1 The people of Moab will wail about what happens to the towns of Nebo and Medeba.
\q2 They will shave their heads and cut off their beards {to show that they are grieving}.
\q1 \v 3 They will wear sackcloth when they go out in public.
\q2 They will wail on their rooftops and in their city squares.
\q1 People will be weeping as they come down
\q2 into the cities from the temple and the shrines.
\q1 \v 4 The people of the cities of Heshbon and Elealeh will cry out;
\q2 people as far away as Jahaz will hear them wailing.
\q1 The Moabite soldiers will shout in distress,
\q2 and they will tremble with fear.
\q1 \v 5 I feel very sorry for the people of Moab.
\q1 Its refugees will flee as far as Zoar and Eglath Shelishiyah.
\q1 They will weep as they go up on the road to Luhith.
\q2 All along the road to Horonaim, they will cry out because enemies have destroyed their land.
\q1 \v 6 The water in the Nimrim brook will dry up.
\q2 The grass will wither, the plants will die,
\q2 and nothing green will remain.
\q1 \v 7 So the people will take their valuable possessions and the food they have saved
\q2 and carry those things across the Wadi of the Willows.
\q1 \v 8 People will cry out throughout the whole territory of Moab.
\q2 There will be wailing from Eglaim {in the south}
\q2 all the way to Beer Elim {in the north}.
\q1 \v 9 The stream near Dimon will be red with the blood of those who have died,
\q2 but Yahweh will bring even more trouble on Dimon.
\q1 He will send lions to attack those who try to escape from Moab
\q2 and those who remain in that land.
\c 16
\q1 \v 1 {The leaders of Moab will say to each other,}
\q1 “We must send lambs {as gifts} to the ruler of Judah.
\q2 We must send them from the city of Sela through the wilderness
\q2 to the city of Jerusalem on Mount Zion.
\q1 \v 2 At that time, the women of Moab will look like fluttering birds that someone has chased from their nests
\q2 as they try to wade {to safety} across the shallow places of the Arnon River.
\q1 \v 3 {The leaders of Moab will say to the ruler of Judah:} Decide that it is right to help us.
\q2 Protect us, just as the shadow of a large rock
\q2 provides a dark, cool shelter from the heat of the sun at midday.
\q1 Hide us who are fleeing from our enemies,
\q2 and do not tell them where we are.
\q1 \v 4 Allow us who are fleeing from Moab to live among you.
\q2 Please protect us from those who want to destroy us!
\q1 {We do not need to settle among you permanently, because}
\q2 someday no one will be oppressing us or destroying our land anymore.
\q2 Those who have been doing that will be gone from our land.
\q1 \v 5 {If you help us,} your reign will become more secure
\q2 because you will be acting mercifully toward people in need.
\q1 You will be a genuine successor of David as king,
\q2 making fair decisions and quickly doing what is right.”
\b
\q1 \v 6 We {people of Judah} have heard how very proud the Moabites are.
\q2 We have heard that they very arrogantly make great claims about themselves.
\q2 But their boasts are not true.
\q1 \v 7 As a result, people throughout Moab will wail about what is happening to them.
\q2 They will feel very badly and moan
\q2 that they can no longer have raisin cakes from the city of Kir Hareseth.
\q1 \v 8 The crops in the fields of the city of Heshbon
\q2 and the grapevines at the town of Sibmah have withered.
\q1 Armies that rulers of other nations command
\q2 have destroyed Sibmah’s finest vines.
\q1 {Moab is no longer prosperous as it was when it was like a vine}
\q2 whose shoots spread out north to the city of Jazer,
\q2 extended east into the wilderness,
\q1 and went all the way to the sea in the west.
\q1 \v 9 So I will weep as the people of Jazer are weeping
\q2 for the grapevines of Sibmah.
\q1 If I could, I would water the fields and vineyards
\q2 around the cities of Heshbon and Elealeh with my tears.
\q1 I am sad because no one is shouting joyfully anymore
\q2 about collecting summer fruit or harvesting grain.
\q1 \v 10 No one is celebrating happily anymore
\q2 in the fields they have cultivated.
\q1 No one is singing or shouting joyfully in the vineyards.
\q2 No one is crushing grapes in the winepresses to make wine.
\q1 {Yahweh says,} “I have stopped people from shouting joyfully.”
\q1 \v 11 So I grieve deeply for Moab.
\q2 My moaning is like the mournful sound of a harp.
\q1 I am very sad for Kir Heres.
\q1 \v 12 When it becomes evident that the Moabites have worn themselves out
\q2 praying at their hilltop shrines,
\q1 they will go to their temples to pray,
\q2 but they will accomplish nothing by praying.
\p
\v 13 That was the message that Yahweh spoke about Moab previously.
\v 14 However, Yahweh now says, “In no more than three years, counted as precisely as a hired worker counts the years of his contract, people will consider Moab insignificant, even though it was once glorious. Even though there are now a large number of Moabites, only very few of them will remain, and they will be weak.”
\c 17
\q1 \v 1 Yahweh gave this message about {the Aramean kingdom whose capital was} Damascus:
\q1 “Listen! I am going to destroy Damascus, so that it will no longer be a city.
\q2 It will be only a pile of ruins!
\q1 \v 2 People will abandon the towns around the city of Aroer.
\q2 Flocks of sheep will eat grass and lie down there,
\q2 and no one will chase them away.
\q1 \v 3 The northern kingdom of Israel will also lose its fortified cities
\q2 when enemies destroy the kingdom whose capital is Damascus.
\q2 The few people who remain in Aram
\q1 will lose the things that made them great, just as the Israelites will.”
\q2 That is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, declares.
\q1 \v 4 “The kingdom of Israel has been glorious,
\q2 but at that time, it will become insignificant,
\q2 as if it were a fat person who became very thin.
\q1 \v 5 The land of Israel will be as bare as a field where harvesters have cut down the grain stalks
\q2 and collected the heads of grain.
\q1 It will be like the fields in the Valley of the Raphaites
\q2 after poor people have come along and picked up the grain that the harvesters left behind.
\q1 \v 6 Only a few Israelites will remain,
\q2 like the few olives that remain on a tree after workers have shaken it,
\q2 which poor people then come and pick.
\q1 The few Israelites will be like the two or three olives that remain on the top branches,
\q2 or like the four or five olives that remain on the branches that produce the most fruit.”
\q1 That is what Yahweh, the God whom Israelites should worship, declares.
\q1 \v 7 At that time, the people will seek help from the God who made them.
\q2 They will depend on the holy God of the Israelites.
\q1 \v 8 They will no longer seek help from {the gods whom they worship at} altars
\q2 that they have made themselves.
\q1 They will not worship idols they have made
\q2 that represent the goddess Asherah or the sun.
\q1 \v 9 When the Israelites invaded the land of Canaan,
\q2 the former inhabitants abandoned their cities,
\q2 and trees and shrubs grew there.
\q1 In the same way, when God punishes the Israelites,
\q2 they will abandon their cities that have walls around them;
\q2 no one will live in them.
\q1 \v 10 That will happen because they no longer worship the God who saved them.
\q2 He protected them as if he had been a huge rock they could climb onto and be safe.
\q1 They may plant fine plants
\q2 and even vine cuttings from foreign lands.
\q2 But since they no longer worship Yahweh,
\q1 \v 11 even if the plants sprout and produce blossoms right away,
\q2 at harvest time, {they will not have produced any crops}.
\q2 People will just cut them down and throw them into a heap.
\q1 This will happen at the time when enemies afflict you
\q2 and you experience suffering that no one can relieve.
\q1 \v 12 How terrible it will be for the huge army
\q2 that a king will assemble from many peoples!
\q1 It will make a loud sound, as the sea does.
\q2 Yes, that great army will make a loud roar, as a waterfall does.
\q1 \v 13 But even though it will make a load roar as a waterfall does,
\q2 Yahweh will defeat it, and its soldiers will run away back to their distant homes.
\q1 Israelite soldiers will chase them,
\q2 and they will scatter like the chaff that the wind scatters on the hills
\q2 or like the tumbleweed that storms blow away.
\q1 \v 14 It will be as if the enemies who terrify us so much
\q2 disappear in a single night.
\q1 That is what will happen to those who invade our land
\q2 and try to take our possessions.
\c 18
\q1 \v 1 How terrible it will be for the people who live in the land
\q2 where insects’ wings buzz loudly!
\q1 That is the land of Cush,
\q2 through which rivers flow.
\q1 \v 2 The king of that land sends ambassadors down the river in papyrus boats,
\q2 sailing over the surface of the water.
\q1 But his messengers who travel so quickly can go back to their nation
\q2 {because Judah does not need to make an alliance with them}.
\q1 The people of that nation are tall and have smooth skin.
\q2 Others throughout the world fear that nation
\q1 because it conquers other countries and takes their territories.
\q2 Great rivers flow through its land.
\q1 \v 3 People who live throughout the world
\q2 should pay attention to the signs that Yahweh himself
\q2 is going to take action to defeat the enemies of Judah.
\q1 \v 4 Yahweh told me this:
\q2 “I am watching quietly from heaven where I live{, but I will act soon}.
\q1 It is as if it is late summer
\q2 and the heat seems to shimmer during the day
\q1 and a heavy mist forms overnight
\q2 {but thunderstorms form suddenly}.”
\q1 \v 5 {When Yahweh defeats Judah’s’ enemies, it will be as if}
\q2 grapes were ready for people to harvest.
\q1 It will be as if the blossoms had fallen off those vines
\q2 and grapes had grown where the flowers were.
\q1 It will be as if Yahweh uses pruning hooks
\q2 to cut off the sprigs that hold the grape bunches
\q2 and as if he also cuts off the branches that have grown from the vine.
\q1 \v 6 The bodies of the dead enemy soldiers will remain on the battlefield.
\q2 There are birds that live in the mountains that eat flesh.
\q1 Those birds will eat those bodies during the summer.
\q2 Wild animals will eat those bodies during the winter.
\q1 \v 7 When Yahweh defeats that enemy army,
\q2 ambassadors will return from that nation
\q2 whose people are tall and have smooth skin.
\q1 Others throughout the world fear that nation
\q2 because it conquers other countries and takes their territories.
\q2 Great rivers flow through its land.
\q1 But those ambassadors will be bringing gifts to Yahweh,
\q2 the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 at Mount Zion, the place where he has chosen to be present.
\c 19
\q1 \v 1 Yahweh gave this message about Egypt.
\q1 Pay attention! Yahweh is coming to Egypt,
\q2 riding on a fast-moving cloud.
\q1 It will be as if the idols of Egypt tremble when he arrives.
\q2 The Egyptians will not be brave anymore.
\q1 \v 2 Yahweh says, “I will influence the Egyptians to fight against each other.
\q2 People will fight against their own relatives and neighbors.
\q1 The army of one city will fight against the army of another city.
\q2 The army of one kingdom will fight against the army of another kingdom.
\q1 \v 3 I will prevent the Egyptians from doing what they plan,
\q2 and this will greatly discourage them.
\q1 They will try to find out what to do by asking {the gods that} their idols {represent}.
\q2 They will ask fortunetellers and those who try to contact people who have died or talk to spirits.
\q1 \v 4 Then I will give a cruel person the power to become the master of the Egyptians.
\q2 He will rule over them as a fierce king.”
\q2 This is what the Lord, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, declares.
\q1 \v 5 The water in the great Nile River will dry up
\q2 so that its riverbed will become dry ground.
\q1 \v 6 The water in the irrigation channels will {become stagnant and} stink.
\q1 The streams that the Nile divides into at its delta will steadily dry up.
\q2 The reeds and rushes in the river will wither.
\q1 \v 7 The vegetation along the Nile at its mouth
\q2 and the crops that people plant near the Nile will dry up.
\q2 The wind will blow them away, and nothing will remain.
\q1 \v 8 The people who used to catch fish in the Nile
\q2 will be very sad because they cannot do that work anymore,
\q1 whether they caught fish by casting hooks into the river
\q2 or by throwing nets into the water.
\q1 \v 9 Workers used to helped to produce linen
\q2 Some of them combed the flax fibers.
\q2 Others wove the fibers into cloth.
\q1 These workers will also be sad
\q2 because they have no work
\q2 {because there is no water to grow flax anymore}.
\q1 \v 10 The work that provided the basis of the Egyptian economy will end.
\q2 The people who worked for wages will feel great distress.
\b
\q1 \v 11 The officials of the city of Zoan are certainly fools.
\q2 The wisest advisors of Pharaoh give foolish advice.
\q1 None of them should brag to Pharaoh,
\q2 “I am one of the wise people you ought to consult.
\q1 ` My ancestors were kings, and they passed their wisdom
\q2 down through the generations to me.”
\q1 \v 12 If there really are wise people in Egypt,
\q2 they should recognize what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q1 is planning to do to Egypt.
\q2 Then they should tell the Egyptians.
\q1 \v 13 But the officials of Zoan have become foolish.
\q2 The officials of Noph are only deceiving themselves {when they think they are wise}.
\q1 The most important leaders of its tribes have led Egypt astray.
\q1 \v 14 Yahweh has confused the Egyptians
\q2 by making these leaders tell them to do the wrong things.
\q1 It is as if the Egyptians are stumbling about
\q2 the way a very drunk person vomits and then steps in his vomit.
\q1 \v 15 No one in Egypt is able to do anything to change the situation.
\q2 It does not matter whether he is a leader or a common person.
\q2 It does not matter whether he is important or unimportant.
\p
\v 16 At that time, the Egyptians will not be strong warriors. They will tremble with fear because of what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, is about to do to them by his power.
\v 17 The Egyptians will be very afraid of the people who live in the land of Judah. Suppose someone mentions Judah to an Egyptian. That will make him very afraid because of what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, has decided to do to Egypt.
\p
\v 18 At that time, people in five cities in the land of Egypt will speak Hebrew. They will worship Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, as their God. People will call one of those cities “the City of Destruction.”
\p
\v 19 At that time, there will be an altar for worshiping Yahweh in the center of the land of Egypt. There will also be a stone pillar at Egypt’s border that honors Yahweh.
\v 20 This pillar will identify Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, as the God whom people worship in the land of Egypt. When enemies invade and oppress the Egyptians, they will pray to Yahweh for help. He will send a powerful person to rescue them from their enemies.
\p
\v 21 At that time, Yahweh will reveal himself to the Egyptians, and they will acknowledge him. They will worship him with sacrifices and grain offerings. The Egyptians will make vows to Yahweh, and will do what they have promised.
\v 22 Yahweh will punish the Egyptians when they do wrong, and this will cause them to repent. They will pray to Yahweh, and he will restore them.
\p
\v 23 At that time, there will be a highway from Egypt to Assyria. Assyrians will travel to Egypt, and Egyptians will travel to Assyria. The people of both countries will worship Yahweh together.
\p
\v 24 At that time, Yahweh will use the Israelites together with the Egyptians and Assyrians to make good things happen for people throughout the earth.
\v 25 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, will bless them. He will say, “I bless Egypt, my people, and Assyria, the nation I have made, and Israel, my special possession.”
\c 20
\q1 \v 1 One year, King Sargon of Assyria sent the Tartan{, his army commander,} to attack the city of Ashdod. The Tartan and his soldiers fought against Ashdod and conquered it.
\v 2 At that time, Isaiah son of Amoz received a message from Yahweh. Yahweh told him, “Take off the rough cloth that you have been wearing. Also take off the sandals you are wearing.” So Isaiah did what Yahweh told him to do. He went around naked and barefoot.
\p
\v 3 Then Yahweh said, “My servant Isaiah has been going around naked and barefoot for three years. He has been doing that as a sign to warn about what is going to happen to the people of Egypt and Cush.
\v 4 The king of Assyria is going to capture the people of Egypt and Cush and lead them away. He will force people of all ages to walk naked and barefoot, just as Isaiah has done, with their buttocks uncovered. This will cause the people of Egypt to feel great shame.
\v 5 Then the people who boasted that they could expect the people of Cush and Egypt to help them will feel shock and shame.
\v 6 At that time, the people who live in this coastal area will say, ‘Look what happened to the nations we were hoping would help us! We expected them to protect us if the king of Assyria attacked us. Now we cannot escape from him!’”
\c 21
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave this message about {Babylon,} the land near a great river that will become a desert.
\q1 The powerful army of a nation that terrifies others
\q2 will come swiftly and destructively from the desert.
\q2 It will sweep along like the storm winds that blow across the Negev.
\q1 \v 2 God showed me a distressing vision:
\q2 In a surprise attack, this army will destroy Babylon.
\q1 Yahweh has commanded the Elamites and Medes
\q2 to go and besiege that city.
\q1 Babylon made many people suffer,
\q2 but Yahweh will keep them from suffering anymore.
\q1 \v 3 Because of what I saw, I feel anguish constantly throughout my body
\q2 like the pains that a woman feels when she gives birth.
\q1 I have to bend over {to support myself},
\q2 and this keeps me from hearing anything further.
\q2 I feel such dismay that I am not able to see anything further.
\q1 \v 4 When I try to think, I get confused.
\q2 I am very afraid of what will happen.
\q2 I was hoping that things would be peaceful.
\q2 But what God has shown me has made me afraid about the future.
\q1 \v 5 {In my vision, I saw} the Babylonians preparing a great feast.
\q2 They spread out carpets, and they ate and drank.
\q1 But then someone shouted, “Get up, you commanders!
\q2 Get your weapons ready to fight a battle!”
\q1 \v 6 The Lord said to me,
\q2 “Have a watchman stand on the wall.
\q2 Have him report whatever he sees.
\q1 \v 7 If he sees a chariot coming along with two soldiers riding horses,
\q2 or if he sees someone coming who is riding on a donkey or camel,
\q2 he should pay very careful attention to what he sees.”
\q1 \v 8 Later the watchman called out loudly {to me},
\q2 “My lord, I have stood on this watchtower
\q2 continually during the day,
\q2 and I have stayed here at my guard post
\q2 throughout the night.
\q1 \v 9 And now I am finally seeing something.
\q2 Here comes a man in a chariot!
\q2 A pair of horsemen are traveling with him.”
\q2 One of those men shouted out this news:
\q2 “Enemies have completely destroyed Babylon!
\q2 They have taken the idols that represented its gods
\q2 and smashed them on the ground!”
\q1 \v 10 My people, your captors have been oppressing you
\q2 as if they were crushing grain on a threshing floor.
\q1 But now I am telling you what Yahweh has revealed to me about them.
\q2 He is the commander of the heavenly armies and the God whom Israelites should worship.
\p
\v 11 Yahweh gave this message about {Edom, which I will call} Dumah.
\q1 Someone called out to me from Mount Seir,
\q2 “Watchman, how much longer will our suffering last?
\q2 It feels as if it has been a long, dark night”
\q1 \v 12 {I} the watchman replied,
\q2 “You will briefly have some relief, which will feel like morning,
\q1 but then you will be suffering again,
\q2 and it will feel like another night.
\q2 If you really want to understand what God is doing,
\q2 repent of your sin and ask him to help you.”
\p
\v 13 Yahweh gave this message about Arabia:
\q1 “You people who lead caravans from Dedan,
\q2 you will have to camp in the thickets of the Arabian desert.
\q1 \v 14 The people who live in the land of Tema
\q2 should bring water to these thirsty people.
\q2 They should bring food to feed these refugees.
\q1 \v 15 They are fleeing to escape from intense fighting.
\q2 They do not want the soldiers of hostile armies
\q1 to stab them with their swords
\q2 or shoot their arrows at them.”
\p
\v 16 The Lord said to me, “A hired worker counts the years of his contract precisely, and in one year that is just as precise, the people of Kedar will no longer be great.
\v 17 The warriors of Kedar are famous for being good archers, but only a few of them will survive. This will happen because I, Yahweh, the God whom Israelites should worship, have said it will happen.”
\c 22
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave this message about {Jerusalem, which is up on a mountain, but which seemed like} a valley in the vision he gave me.
\q1 It is strange that so many people have gone up onto their flat rooftops.
\q1 \v 2 People in the city are having a very noisy celebration.
\q2 Many other people have died,
\q1 but enemies did not kill them with swords.
\q2 Instead, they died of hunger or disease.
\q1 \v 3 The leaders of the city have fled
\q2 and surrendered to the enemy without fighting.
\q1 Other people tried to flee far from the city,
\q2 but the enemy found them and captured them.
\q1 \v 4 Because of this, I have told the people around me,
\q2 “Leave me alone, because I want to weep bitterly.
\q1 Do not try to comfort me about how the enemy is destroying
\q2 Jerusalem, our capital city.”
\q1 \v 5 I am so sad because Yahweh the Lord, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 has allowed this to be a time of great trouble for Jerusalem.
\q1 Enemy soldiers will try to batter down the city walls.
\q2 Then the people will cry out for help to Yahweh,
\q2 whose temple is on Mount Zion.
\q1 \v 6 Armies from Elam and Kir are ready to attack.
\q2 They have arrived in chariots and on horseback.
\q1 They have brought quivers full of arrows,
\q2 and they are removing the protective covers of their shields
\q2 {to get ready to fight a battle}.
\q1 \v 7 Enemy chariots have overrun the fertile valleys around the city.
\q2 Enemy soldiers have come on horseback
\q2 and positioned themselves right outside the city gates.
\q1 \v 8 Those enemies destroyed the defenses of Judah.
\q2 When that happened, the people of Jerusalem hoped they could protect themselves
\q2 with the supply of weapons in the House of the Forest.
\q1 \v 9 The people realized that there were many breaks in the walls of Jerusalem.
\q2 They also realized that they needed to collect water and store it safely within the city.
\q1 \v 10 So they surveyed the houses in Jerusalem
\q2 and tore some of them down to use their stones to repair the city wall.
\q1 \v 11 They also built a reservoir{, the Pool of Siloam,} between the walls of the city
\q2 to store water that they channeled down from the catchment area of the Gihon Spring.
\q1 But they did not ask Yahweh for help,
\q2 even though he was the one who originally created the water.
\q1 \v 12 At that time, Yahweh the Lord, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 told the people to weep and mourn
\q1 and shave their heads and wear rough cloth
\q2 {to show that they were sorry for the sins they had committed}.
\q1 \v 13 But instead of doing that, they had a celebration!
\q1 They slaughtered cattle and sheep
\q2 to have meat for a feast where they also drank wine.
\q1 They said, “We should eat and drink as much as we want,
\q2 because we might die tomorrow!”
\p
\v 14 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, revealed this to me: “I swear that I will not forgive these people for this sin for as long as they live!” That is what Yahweh the Lord, the commander of the heavenly armies, said.
\p
\v 15 Yahweh the Lord, the commander of the heavenly armies, also said this to me:
\q1 “Go and give this message from me to Shebna,
\q2 the official who is in charge of the royal palace:
\q1 \v 16 ‘You should not be having workers chisel out a tomb for you
\q2 in that rocky cliff above the city of Jerusalem!
\q2 You have no ancestors who have tombs there.
\q1 \v 17 You think that you are a great man,
\q2 but I am about to take away your official position.
\q1 It will be as if I grab you powerfully and throw you away.
\q1 \v 18 It will be as if I roll you up into a ball
\q2 and hurl you into a large, open country.
\q1 You will die there.
\q2 You are very proud of your military power,
\q2 but this will be the end of it.
\q1 {I am going to punish you in this way because}
\q2 you have disgraced the household of your master, the king.
\q1 \v 19 I will remove you from your important position
\q2 so that you have to stop working in the palace.
\q1 \v 20 Then I will summon my servant Eliakim son of Hilkiah to replace you.
\q1 \v 21 I will give him your robe to wear,
\q2 and I will fasten your sash around him.
\q2 This will show that I have given him the authority that you had.
\q1 He will protect the people who live in Jerusalem and other places in Judah,
\q2 just as a father protects his family.
\v 22 I will give him authority over the royal household of Judah.
\q1 When he says that something should happen,
\q2 no one will stop it from happening.
\q1 When he says that something should not happen,
\q2 no one will make it happen.
\q1 \v 23 I will establish him firmly in his position
\q2 as if he were a peg that someone had hammered firmly into a wall.
\q1 He will bring great honor to his family,
\q2 as if they had a special place to sit on important occasions.
\q1 \v 24 Many members of the younger generation of his extended family
\q2 will receive important positions because of him.
\q1 It will be as if he is a peg on which people hang
\q2 all of their small household containers, such as bowls and jars.
\p
\v 25 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, declares, “There will be a time when Eliakim no longer has his position. It will be as if a peg that someone firmly fastened had come loose and fallen down and everything that was hanging on it had also crashed down.” This will surely happen because Yahweh has said that it will happen.
\c 23
\p
\v 1 Yahweh gave this message about the city of Tyre.
\q1 Sailors on ships that can sail long distances should weep
\q2 because enemies have destroyed {the port city of} Tyre!
\q2 There are no buildings left, and it is not safe for their ships to enter the harbor.
\q1 They first heard this news when they stopped {at the island of Cyprus}
\q2 where the Kittim people live.
\q1 \v 2 Merchants from the city of Sidon
\q2 made Tyre wealthy by using its harbor to send and receive goods
\q2 that they transported across the sea in ships.
\q1 People who lived along the coast near Tyre also became wealthy.
\q2 Now those merchants and people should be silent {with grief}.
\q1 \v 3 Tyre became wealthy by trading the grain
\q2 that people grew along the Nile River.
\q1 Tyre was a place where people from many nations
\q2 bought and sold goods.
\q1 \v 4 The people of the city of Sidon should feel shame.
\q2 {The city of Tyre, its ally,} was once a strong city by the sea.
\q1 Now it is as if the sea is speaking to the people of Sidon
\q2 {on behalf of Tyre} and saying,
\q1 “I have no population left.
\q2 It is as if I never had labor pains and gave birth to children.
\q2 It is as if I never raised young men or brought up young women.”
\q1 \v 5 When the Egyptians hear what happened to Tyre,
\q2 they will tremble with grief.
\q1 \v 6 The ships that once docked at Tyre
\q2 should now go to other ports.
\q2 The people who live along the part of the coast
\q2 near where Tyre used to be should weep {with grief}.
\q1 \v 7 Tyre was a city for a very long time.
\q2 The people who lived along the coast were very proud of it.
\q2 Its people went out and settled in many other lands.
\q1 \v 8 The leaders of Tyre appointed leaders for its colonies.
\q2 Its merchants were like princes.
\q1 People throughout the world treated its traders with great respect.
\q2 So it is important to consider who decided to destroy it{, and why}.
\q1 \v 9 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, decided to destroy it.
\q2 He did that to show that people should not have considered Tyre
\q2 to be so great that it was almost a god.
\q1 He did that to show that the leaders of Tyre, whom people honored so greatly,
\q2 were only human beings.
\q1 \v 10 The people who formerly made their living from the sea trade
\q2 should now spread out over the land and farm it,
\q2 just as the Nile floods over its banks.
\q1 They should do this because they no longer have a reason
\q2 to keep living by the sea.
\q1 \v 11 Yahweh used his power to destroy this great city by the sea,
\q2 and that has made the people of other kingdoms afraid.
\q1 He decided that enemies would destroy the fortresses
\q2 of this great merchant city.
\q1 \v 12 Yahweh said to the people of {Tyre, who came from} Sidon,
\q1 “Now that enemies have destroyed your city,
\q2 you will no longer be happy.
\q1 Even if you leave and resettle {on the island of Cyprus,}
\q2 where the Kittim people live,
\q2 you will not have a peaceful life there either.”
\q1 \v 13 Consider what happened to the land of the Chaldeans!
\q2 It is as if those people are not even a nation anymore.
\q1 The Assyrians turned their land into a place where wild desert animals live.
\q1 They set up siege towers {to conquer their capital city of Babylon}.
\q2 {When they captured it,} they took away the valuable things from its palaces,
\q2 and then they destroyed it completely.
\q1 \v 14 Sailors on ships that can sail long distances should weep
\q2 because enemies have destroyed {the city of Tyre,}
\q2 the place that was once a secure harbor for them.
\q1 \v 15 Now that enemies have destroyed it,
\q2 people will not use Tyre as a trading port
\q2 for 70 years, about as long as a king lives.
\q1 By the end of those 70 years,
\q2 Tyre will be like the prostitute in this song:
\q1 \v 16 “You prostitute whom people have forgotten,
\q2 pick up your harp and walk through the city.
\q1 Play skillfully and sing many songs
\q2 so that people will remember you.”
\q1 \v 17 After those 70 years end,
\q2 Yahweh will restore Tyre.
\q1 Then Tyre will once again earn money
\q2 by trading with kingdoms throughout the world,
\q1 just as a prostitute earns money from her clients.
\q1 \v 18 However, the people of Tyre will dedicate what they earn to Yahweh.
\q2 They will not store it up or save it for themselves.
\q1 Instead, their trading profits will belong to those who worship Yahweh,
\q2 so that they can have the food they need
\q2 and buy good clothing.
\c 24
\q1 \v 1 Pay attention! Yahweh is about to destroy everything on the earth
\q2 and make it a wasteland.
\q1 He will ruin the surface of the land
\q2 and scatter the people who live on it.
\q1 \v 2 He will treat everyone the same way:
\q2 ordinary people and priests,
\q2 male servants and their masters,
\q2 female servants and their mistresses,
\q2 buyers and sellers,
\q2 those who lend money and those who borrow it,
\q2 creditors and those who owe them debts.
\q1 \v 3 Yahweh will completely empty the earth
\q2 and remove everything valuable on it.
\q1 This will surely happen because Yahweh has said that it will happen.
\q1 \v 4 The plants on the earth will completely wither.
\q2 The plants that grow in the ground will not be able to produce any fruit.
\q2 The most important people on earth will become weak.
\q1 \v 5 The people who live on the earth have made it
\q2 a place that Yahweh considers unclean.
\q2 That is because they have disobeyed his instructions,
\q2 disregarded his laws,
\q2 and broken the permanent agreement that he made with them.
\q1 \v 6 Because of that, God has cursed the earth and is destroying it.
\q2 The people of the earth are suffering for what they have done wrong.
\q1 As a result, most of the people of the earth are dying.
\q2 Only a few people remain alive.
\q1 \v 7 The grapevines are producing few grapes,
\q2 so there is little new wine.
\q2 The people who used to feel happy are now groaning.
\q1 \v 8 People no longer play happy music on tambourines or harps.
\q2 They no longer celebrate loudly.
\q1 \v 9 People no longer sing songs as they drink wine.
\q2 People no longer enjoy drinking beer.
\q1 \v 10 There is so much damage in the city
\q2 that it is no longer a busy place.
\q2 People do not allow others to enter their homes.
\q1 \v 11 People shout in the streets because they have no more wine.
\q2 People who used to be happy are not happy anymore.
\q2 Instead, they feel gloomy.
\q1 \v 12 Enemies have battered down the gates of the city
\q2 and come in and destroyed its buildings.
\q1 \v 13 This is what it will be like throughout the earth
\q2 and among its peoples:
\q1 it will be like when workers beat the last few olives off a tree,
\q2 or like the few grapes that remain after the grape harvest ends.
\q1 \v 14 But those who survive will shout loudly and joyfully.
\q2 From the western coastlands, they will proclaim how majestic Yahweh is.
\q1 \v 15 So people in eastern regions should also praise Yahweh.
\q2 Those who live in the coastlands by the sea
\q1 should celebrate the fame of Yahweh,
\q2 the God whom Israelites worship.
\q1 \v 16 We know that people in faraway places are singing,
\q2 “Glorify Yahweh, who is righteous!”
\q1 However, I say, “I am wasting away! I am wasting away!
\q2 How terrible it is for me!”
\q1 Dishonest people continue to betray others.
\q2 They betray others repeatedly and very dishonestly.
\q1 \v 17 You people who live on the earth,
\q2 you will experience great fear
\q2 and difficulties you cannot overcome,
\q2 as if they were deep pits or traps from which you could not escape.
\q1 \v 18 If someone tries to flee from something that scares him,
\q2 it will be as if he falls into a deep pit.
\q1 If he overcomes one difficulty, as if he climbed out of that pit,
\q2 he will have another difficulty, as if a trap catches him.
\q1 God will cause torrential rainstorms
\q2 and great earthquakes.
\q1 \v 19 God will shatter the earth completely.
\q2 He will split it open
\q2 and make it shake violently.
\q1 \v 20 The earth will move back and forth the way a drunk person staggers.
\q2 It will sway as a hut does {in a windstorm}.
\q1 The sins of the people who live on the earth
\q2 are like a weight that is too heavy for it to carry.
\q1 It will be as if the earth collapses under that weight
\q2 and is not able to get up again.
\q1 \v 21 At that time, Yahweh will punish the powerful beings in the heavens above
\q2 and the kings on the earth below.
\q1 \v 22 Yahweh will gather them all together and put them in a dungeon like prisoners.
\q2 He will lock them up in that prison,
\q1 and after a long time, he will punish them.
\q1 \v 23 Then the moon and the sun will not seem so bright
\q2 because Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, will reign
\q1 on Mount Zion and in Jerusalem,
\q2 and the elders of his people will see how glorious he is.
\c 25
\q1 \v 1 Yahweh, you are my God.
\q2 I will praise and worship you for who you are.
\q1 You have done wonderful things.
\q2 You planned them long ago, and you have accomplished them with complete faithfulness.
\q1 \v 2 You turn cities into piles of rubble.
\q2 You even destroy cities that have strong walls around them.
\q1 Even when foreigners build fortresses,
\q2 you destroy them so completely
\q2 that no one will ever rebuild them.
\q1 \v 3 Therefore, people from powerful nations will declare that you are very great.
\q2 and people will be in awe of you
\q2 in the cities of nations that terrify others.
\q1 \v 4 Yahweh, you protect poor people just as a strong fortress protects those inside it.
\q2 You protect needy people when they are suffering
\q1 just as a shelter protects people from rain
\q2 and a shade protects people from the heat of the sun.
\q1 Cruel people attack others the way a rainstorm batters a wall
\q2 \v 5 and the way scorching heat makes a desert unbearable.
\q1 Foreign armies may cause trouble, but you defeat them
\q2 just as a cloud cools the land with its shade.
\q2 You keep cruel people from singing to celebrate their victories.
\b
\q1 \v 6 Here on Mount Zion, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 will prepare a wonderful feast for all peoples.
\q2 It will be a banquet with rich food and fine wine.
\q1 \v 7 The people of all nations are gloomy,
\q2 as if a thick shroud were covering them.
\q1 But here on Mount Zion, Yahweh will make them happy again,
\q2 as if he were taking away that shroud.
\q1 \v 8 He will do that by making it so that no one dies anymore.
\q2 Yahweh, the Lord, will make it so that no one weeps anymore.
\q1 His people were in shameful situations in many places in the world,
\q2 but he will restore them to honorable situations.
\q1 This will surely happen because Yahweh has said that it will happen.
\p
\v 9 At that time, people will proclaim,
\q1 “Pay attention! Our God has done this!
\q2 We waited confidently for him to help us,
\q2 and he has rescued us!
\q1 Our God is Yahweh. We waited confidently for him to help us.
\q2 Now he has rescued us, so we should be happy and celebrate!”
\b
\q1 \v 10 Yahweh will use his power to protect Mount Zion,
\q2 but he will defeat the people of Moab in their own land
\q2 just as someone tramples straw into a pile of manure.
\q1 \v 11 The people of Moab will try to escape
\q2 as if they were in a giant pool of manure
\q2 and trying to swim out of it.
\q1 But no matter how cleverly they try to escape,
\q2 Yahweh will defeat them and humble them.
\q1 \v 12 Even though there are strong, high walls around Moab’s cities,
\q2 Yahweh will demolish them
\q2 so that they are only stones lying on the dust of the ground.
\c 26
\q1 \v 1 At that time, people in the land of Judah will sing this song:
\q1 “We have a strong city!
\q2 God protects us as though he had built walls and fortifications around us to save us.
\q1 \v 2 Open the gates so that the people who do what is right may enter.
\q2 Those people remain faithful to Yahweh.
\q1 \v 3 Yahweh, you keep those who firmly commit themselves to you
\q2 completely secure, because they trust in you.
\q1 \v 4 So always trust Yahweh,
\q2 because Yahweh will always protect us
\q2 as if he were a huge rock we could climb onto and be safe.
\q1 \v 5 He has humbled those who lived in a city
\q2 that they thought was safe because it was on a high place.
\q1 He has destroyed it so completely that all that remains
\q2 are stones lying on the dust of the ground.
\q1 \v 6 Poor and needy people walk over its ruins.”
\b
\q1 \v 7 Yahweh, you are righteous,
\q2 and when people want to do what is right,
\q2 you make it easier for them to do that.
\q1 \v 8 Yes, Yahweh, we want to do what you have decided is right,
\q2 and we are waiting for you to help us do that.
\q1 We earnestly want people to know
\q2 what you are like and the great things you have done.
\q1 \v 9 During the night I long for you,
\q2 and the first thing I want to do in the morning
\q2 is to meet with you.
\q1 When you judge people on the earth,
\q2 those who live in the world learn to do what is right.
\q1 \v 10 Even if rulers show mercy to wicked people
\q2 {and do not punish them right away},
\q2 they do not learn to do what is right.
\q1 Even in a land where people do what is right, they do wrong things,
\q2 and they do not recognize how great Yahweh is.
\q1 \v 11 Yahweh, you are about to use your power to punish them,
\q2 but they do not recognize this.
\q1 Show them how eager you are to help your people,
\q2 and let that shame them.
\q1 Give them the punishment you have prepared for your enemies,
\q2 and may that destroy them.
\b
\q1 \v 12 Yahweh, you have really made happen
\q2 everything it seems that we have accomplished.
\q2 So please make good things happen for us,
\q1 \v 13 Yahweh our God, other masters have governed us,
\q2 but you are the only one whose authority we acknowledge.
\q1 \v 14 Those rulers are dead, and they will not live again.
\q2 They are spirits of dead people who will not come back to life.
\q1 That is because you punished them and destroyed them.
\q2 Now no one remembers them anymore.
\q1 \v 15 Yahweh, you have greatly increased the population of our nation,
\q2 and people honor you for doing that.
\q2 You have also enlarged our territory.
\b
\q1 \v 16 Yahweh, when your people were suffering,
\q2 they called out to you to help them.
\q1 When you were punishing them to correct them,
\q2 they whispered prayers to you.
\q1 \v 17 A pregnant woman writhes in pain and cries out
\q2 when she is about to give birth.
\q2 In the same way, we suffered because of your discipline, Yahweh.
\q1 \v 18 We suffered as if we were having birth pangs,
\q2 but it was as if we did not give birth to anything.
\q1 We could not do anything to make our land safe,
\q2 and no new people came into the world.
\b
\q1 \v 19 But Yahweh, those of your people who have died will live again;
\q2 their bodies will become alive!
\q2 Those who now lie in the ground should come back to life and sing joyfully!
\q1 You are like dew, Yahweh, which makes plants live {even when there is not rain},
\q2 but you are like spiritual dew that makes dead people live again.
\q1 When the spirits of dead people come back to life,
\q2 it will be as if the earth is giving birth to people.
\b
\q1 \v 20 So now, my people, go into your rooms
\q2 and shut your doors behind you.
\q1 Hide there for just a little while
\q2 until Yahweh is no longer angry.
\q1 \v 21 Listen! Yahweh is about to come from heaven
\q2 to punish the people on earth for the evil things they have done.
\q1 It will become evident whom others have murdered,
\q2 even if they buried their bodies or their blood soaked into the ground.
\c 27
\q1 \v 1 At that time, Yahweh will {destroy the enemy nations that have oppressed the Israelites.}
\q2 {It will be as if he} uses his big, strong, dangerous sword
\q1 to kill a sea monster that glides swiftly through the water
\q2 and moves so elusively that no one else can capture or kill it.
\q1 \v 2 At that time, Yahweh will sing this song:
\q2 “{You people of Israel,} sing {with me} about the vineyard that delights me{, which represents you}!
\q2 \v 3 I, Yahweh, guard that vineyard.
\q2 I water the plants frequently.
\q1 I guard it all the time
\q2 so that no one steals the fruit or harms the plants.
\q1 \v 4 I am no longer angry {with the vineyard for producing sour grapes,}
\q2 so I will no longer allow weeds to grow wildly in it.
\q1 If I see any weeds, I will {pull them out and} burn them up,
\q2 as if I were attacking an enemy in battle.
\q1 \v 5 Those weeds {represent enemies who} can make peace with me
\q2 and depend on me for protection
\q2 so that I do not destroy them.”
\q1 \v 6 In the future, the descendants of Jacob will be like a plant with deep roots.
\q2 Those Israelites will flourish and prosper.
\q2 They will provide good things for people throughout the world.
\q1 \v 7 Yahweh has not punished the Israelite people
\q2 as severely as he punished the nations that attacked them.
\q1 He has not killed Israelites the way he killed the enemies who killed them.
\q1 \v 8 Yahweh disciplined his people just enough to correct them.
\q2 He did that by sending them away into exile.
\q2 He drove them out of their land the way a harsh east wind blows things away.
\q1 \v 9 The descendants of Jacob will break down all of their pagan altars
\q2 and crush their stones to pieces as if they were chalk.
\q1 They will destroy the idols that they made
\q2 to represent the goddess Asherah and the sun.
\q1 When they do that, it will show that Yahweh has made them stop committing those sins
\q2 and has forgiven them.
\q1 \v 10 Cities that once had strong walls around them are now empty.
\q2 People do not live in them anymore, just as they do not live in the desert.
\q1 Calves eat grass and lie down where the cities once were.
\q2 They chew the leaves off the branches of trees.
\q1 \v 11 Those cities are like a tree {that has died and} whose branches have dried up.
\q2 Women come and break branches like that and use them to start fires.
\q1 Those people do not understand how Yahweh wants them to live.
\q2 So even though he created them,
\q2 he will not be merciful to them or treat them kindly.
\p
\v 12 However, at that future time, Yahweh will gather his people one by one.
\q2 He will separate them from the people of other nations
\q2 the way a farmer separates grain from chaff.
\q1 He will collect them all the way from the Euphrates River, which flows so strongly,
\q2 to the brook at the boundary of Egypt.
\q1 \v 13 At that time, someone will blow a great ram’s horn.
\q2 Then those Israelites who are about to die in Assyria
\q2 and those whom their enemies have taken to Egypt
\q1 will come back to Jerusalem.
\q2 They will worship Yahweh there on his holy mountain, Mount Zion.
\c 28
\q1 \v 1 How terrible it will be for the people who live in the city of Samaria!
\q2 They are proud that their city is very beautiful.
\q1 It is on a hilltop above a rich valley.
\q2 It looks to them like a crown on a king’s head.
\q1 But it will stop being beautiful, just as a flower fades.
\q2 The people who live there drink so much wine that they become drunk.
\q1 \v 2 Listen! The Lord is going to use someone who is very powerful {to punish them}.
\q2 That person will come like a very destructive hailstorm
\q2 and like a flood of mighty water that sweeps over the land.
\q1 He will use his power to destroy Samaria completely.
\q1 \v 3 Those drunkards in Samaria are proud of their city on top of a hill,
\q2 but enemy soldiers will trample it under their feet.
\q1 \v 4 That very beautiful city
\q2 on a hilltop above a rich valley
\q2 will stop being beautiful, just as a flower fades.
\q1 {Enemies will take the valuable things from Samaria}
\q1 just as someone quickly picks and eats a fig he sees that has ripened before summer.
\b
\q1 \v 5 When that happens, some people will survive
\q2 who belong to Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies.
\q2 He will rule them in a glorious way.
\q1 \v 6 He will enable judges to judge cases fairly,
\q2 and he will make soldiers who defend cities strong enough
\q2 to keep enemy armies from conquering them.
\b
\q1 \v 7 However, the leaders of Judah also drink too much wine and beer.
\q2 This makes them stumble about drunkenly.
\q1 The priests and prophets become drunk
\q2 from drinking wine and beer.
\q1 As a result, the prophets do not understand messages from Yahweh,
\q2 and the priests do not correctly decide the cases the people bring to them.
\q1 \v 8 {They drink so much that} they vomit on the tables where they are eating
\q2 and there are no clean places on those tables.
\b
\q1 \v 9 {The Judeans mock God and say,} “He speaks to us as if we were people
\q2 who are difficult to teach and need to have someone explain to us what he is doing.
\q1 He speaks to us as if we were babies
\q2 who have just stopped nursing.
\q1 \v 10 We say this because he only tells us one thing at a time, and he repeats it.
\q2 He explains each part of it repeatedly.
\q2 He talks only for a little while and then waits before saying anything more.”
\b
\q1 \v 11 Because these people refuse to listen, Yahweh will speak to them
\q2 through foreigners who speak a language they do not understand.
\q1 \v 12 Yahweh told his people earlier,
\q2 “I am offering rest and refreshment to anyone who is weary.” But they refused to listen.
\v 13 So now Yahweh really will only tell them one thing at a time and repeat it.
\q2 He will explain each part of it repeatedly.
\q2 He will talk only for a little while and then wait before saying anything more.
\q1 As a result, they will {not succeed at what they attempt,}
\q2 like babies who try to walk but fall into a sitting position.
\q2 God will let their enemies defeat them, trap them, and capture them.
\b
\q1 \v 14 So listen to what Yahweh says, you rulers of the people in Jerusalem,
\q2 who have been speaking so mockingly!
\q1 \v 15 You say boastfully,
\q1 “We are confident that no one will kill us.
\q2 It is as if the place where dead people are has promised us that we will not go there.
\q1 The Assyrian army is very destructive.
\q2 It has overwhelmed other nations as if it were a flood.
\q1 But we will be safe from it
\q2 because we have protected ourselves by making a secret agreement {with Egypt}.”
\p
\v 16 So Yahweh, the Lord, says this:
\q1 “I am going to make Jerusalem a new kind of community,
\q2 as if I were building a new building.
\q1 I am going to make sure that the people live in the right way,
\q2 as if I were setting in place a special first stone for the building
\q1 that I had proved to be perfectly straight
\q2 so that it could guide the placement of all the other stones.
\q2 Whoever trusts me to do this will not scramble to try to save himself.
\q1 \v 17 I will make sure that people in Jerusalem treat one another fairly,
\q2 as if I were using justice to measure lengths
\q1 and using righteousness to make sure that walls were straight
\q2 in the building I was building.
\q1 But enemies will destroy those who think they are safe
\q2 because they have made a secret agreement.
\q1 It will be as if they were crops that a great hailstorm destroyed
\q2 or as if a great flood swept them away.
\q1 \v 18 Suppose that the place where dead people are
\q2 really had promised these Judean leaders that they would not go there.
\q1 Then I am cancelling that promise.
\q1 Instead, the Assyrian army, which is very destructive,
\q2 will destroy them as if a flood had washed them away.
\q2 The Assyrian soldiers will march throughout their land.
\q1 \v 19 Each day, the Assyrian army will advance steadily farther into Judah.
\q2 Its soldiers will continually capture more cities.
\q1 When the Judean leaders finally understand what Yahweh is doing,
\q2 it will terrify them.”
\q1 \v 20 {The secret agreement with Egypt will not be adequate to support or protect them,}
\q2 just like a bed that is too short for a person to stretch out on
\q2 or a blanket that is too narrow for someone to wrap around himself.
\q1 \v 21 Yahweh will take action just as he did at Mount Perazim{,
\q2 where he enabled David to defeat the Philistines,}
\q2 and in the Valley of Gibeon{, where he enabled Joshua to defeat five Canaanite kings}.
\q1 He will do something to his own people
\q2 that they would not expect him to do.
\q1 \v 22 So the Judeans should stop speaking so mockingly.
\q2 Otherwise, Yahweh will punish them even more severely.
\q1 Yahweh, the Lord, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 has told me that he has decided to destroy the entire land.
\b
\q1 \v 23 Listen carefully to what I say!
\q2 Yes, pay close attention to this message!
\q1 \v 24 When a farmer plows his field, he does not keep plowing without ever planting anything.
\q2 He does not keep breaking up the soil continually.
\q1 \v 25 No, after he makes the ground smooth,
\q2 he scatters seeds of black cumin and {common} cumin on the soil.
\q1 He plants wheat in rows,
\q2 and he plants barley in places where it will grow well.
\q2 He plants spelt along the edges of the field.
\q1 \v 26 God has enabled farmers to understand the right way to do things.
\q1 \v 27 A farmer does not use a heavy threshing sledge to thresh black cumin,
\q2 and he does not roll the wheels of a cart over cumin.
\q1 Instead, he beats black cumin with a stick,
\q2 and he beats cumin with a rod.
\q1 \v 28 A farmer does thresh grain that he wants to use to make bread,
\q2 but he certainly does not thresh it continually.
\q1 He may drive the wheels of a cart over it,
\q2 but he does not let his horses crush it with their hooves.
\q1 \v 29 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 has helped people understand how to do these things right.
\q2 He gives wonderful advice,
\q2 and he makes people truly wise.
\c 29
\q1 \v 1 How terrible it will be for the people of Jerusalem,
\q2 the city that King David made his royal capital!
\q2 Even if they keep celebrating festivals year after year,
\q1 \v 2 I will cause great trouble for the people of Jerusalem,
\q2 and they will weep and grieve.
\q1 The whole city will become like an altar where I burn sacrifices.
\q1 \v 3 I will cause enemies to come and surround the city.
\q2 They will station troops around it to keep anyone from leaving,
\q2 and they will build fortifications to help them attack the city.
\q1 \v 4 I will humble the people of Jerusalem so completely
\q2 that it will seem as if they are speaking from the ground.
\q2 It will seem that what they say is coming faintly from the dust.
\q1 They will sound like ghosts speaking from underground
\q2 who mumble things there in high-pitched voices.
\b
\q1 \v 5 However, suddenly and unexpectedly,
\q2 Yahweh will destroy the huge foreign army that is attacking Jerusalem.
\q1 He will disperse its many fierce soldiers
\q2 the way a strong wind blows away dust or chaff.
\q1 \v 6 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 will come to help the people of Jerusalem.
\q2 He will defeat their enemies with his great power,
\q2 which is like thunder and an earthquake and a very loud noise
\q2 and a violent windstorm and a raging fire.
\q1 \v 7 An army of soldiers from many nations will attack Jerusalem.
\q2 It will besiege the city and cause its people to suffer.
\q1 But suddenly it will go away,
\q2 as if it had been just a dream that someone was having during the night.
\q1 \v 8 The soldiers in that army from many nations will think
\q2 that they are going to conquer the city of Jerusalem on Mount Zion{,
\q2 but suddenly Yahweh will defeat them}.
\q1 They will be like a hungry man who dreams that he is eating
\q2 but wakes up and realizes that he is still hungry.
\q1 They will be like a thirsty man who dreams that he is drinking
\q2 but wakes up and realizes that he is weak from thirst.
\b
\q1 \v 9 Even if the people of Jerusalem try to understand what is happening, this will confuse them.
\q2 It is as if they are blind and cannot see what Yahweh is doing.
\q1 It is as if they are drunk and staggering around
\q1 because they have drunk wine and beer.
\q1 \v 10 {Because the people do not want to understand,}
\q2 Yahweh has made them unable to understand,
\q2 as if he had put them deeply to sleep.
\q1 He has prevented their prophets from understanding,
\q2 as if he had closed their eyes.
\q1 He has prevented their seers from seeing,
\q2 as if he had covered their heads.
\p
\v 11 Everything that God has revealed has become to them like the words on a scroll that someone has sealed shut. If a person gave that scroll to someone who could read and said, “Please read this,” he would say, “I can not read it, because someone has sealed it.”
\v 12 And if a person gave that scroll to someone who could not read and said, “Please read this,” he would say, “I am not able to read.”
\q1 \v 13 The Lord said,
\q1 “{I am going to do something} because these people seem to honor me by what they say,
\q2 but they have not truly devoted themselves to me.
\q1 When they worship me,
\q2 they only do things that other people have told them to do.
\q1 \v 14 Therefore, I am going to amaze these people
\q2 by doing more things that make them wonder.
\q1 I will show that the people they think are wise are not truly wise.
\q2 Yes, I will show that the people they think are intelligent are not truly intelligent.”
\b
\q1 \v 15 How terrible it will be for people who try to hide their plans from Yahweh
\q2 by making them secretly!
\q1 They do things privately and say to themselves,
\q2 “No one sees us! No one knows what we are doing!”
\q1 \v 16 You are considering things to be the opposite of what they really are.
\q2 You are acting as if you were the one who made God.
\q2 That is like a pot acting as if it made the potter.
\q1 No one should say about the one who made him,
\q2 “He did not make me.”
\q1 No one should say about the one who formed him,
\q2 “He did not understand what he was doing.”
\q1 \v 17 But very soon, Yahweh will make things the opposite of what they are now.
\q2 It will be as if the great forests of Lebanon become fertile farmland
\q2 and as if fertile farmland becomes great forests.
\q1 \v 18 At that time, deaf people will be able to hear
\q2 when someone reads words from a scroll.
\q1 Blind people will see clearly.
\q2 They will no longer see dimly or not at all.
\q1 \v 19 Poor people will be very happy again
\q2 because of what Yahweh has done for them.
\q2 He is the holy God of the Israelites.
\q1 \v 20 They will be happy because Yahweh will destroy people who terrify others
\q2 and the people who have been mocking him.
\q1 He will destroy people who eagerly look for opportunities to do wrong things.
\q1 \v 21 Those people falsely accuse others who are innocent.
\q2 They try to trick the elders who decide cases at the city gate.
\q2 They get people to tell lies so that people who deserve to win their cases lose them.
\p
\v 22 Yahweh is the one who rescued Abraham.
\q2 Because of what he is going to do,
\q2 he says this to the descendants of Jacob:
\q1 “The Israelites should no longer feel shame
\q2 that makes their faces turn pale.
\q1 \v 23 I will enable them to have many descendants,
\q2 and when they see those descendants around them,
\q2 they will be loyal only to me.
\q1 I am the holy God of their ancestor Jacob,
\q2 and the Israelites will worship me as their God.
\q1 \v 24 Then those who believed wrong things will understand the right things,
\q2 and those who were complaining will accept what I teach them.”
\c 30
\q1 \v 1 Yahweh declares, “How terrible it will be for the Israelites
\q2 who have rebelled against me even though I am like a father to them.
\q2 They have made plans without asking me what they should do.
\q1 They have made an alliance with the rulers of Egypt
\q2 even though I did not guide them to do that by my Spirit.
\q1 By doing these things, they keep committing more sins.
\q1 \v 2 They sent messengers to Egypt
\q2 without asking me if they should do that.
\q1 They wanted Pharaoh to protect them,
\q2 as if Egypt were a fortress where they could go to be safe
\q2 or a huge rock whose shadow would shelter them from the sun.
\q1 \v 3 However, it will embarrass the Israelites that they depended on Pharaoh to protect them.
\q2 It will humiliate them that they thought Egypt was like
\q2 a huge rock whose shadow would shelter them from the sun.
\q1 \v 4 Officials from Judah have gone to places throughout Egypt
\q2 as ambassadors {seeking help}. However,
\q1 \v 5 it will embarrass all of them that they did this.
\q2 That is because the Egyptians will not be helpful to them at all.
\q1 Instead, the Judeans will feel shame and disgrace
\q2 because they depended on Egypt.”
\p
\v 6 Yahweh gave this message about animals that were in the Negev.
\q1 The Judean leaders loaded valuable treasures
\q2 onto the backs of donkeys and the humps of camels.
\q1 {They had those animals carry those treasures}
\q2 through a land that is difficult and dangerous to cross.
\q2 Lions and lionesses can kill people in that land,
\q2 and vipers and flying serpents can bite them there.
\q1 They are bringing those treasures to the Egyptians {to make an alliance},
\q2 but the Egyptians will not be helpful to them.
\q2 \v 7 The Egyptians will not do anything for the Judeans that really helps them
\q2 {even though the Judeans think that they are very powerful, like a sea monster}.
\q2 That is why I call Egypt “The Sea Monster That Does Nothing.”
\b
\q1 \v 8 {Yahweh told me,} “Now go and write this message on a tablet where my people can see it.
\q2 Also write it on a scroll so that in the future,
\q2 it will always testify {that I warned them}.
\q1 \v 9 These people are rebellious.
\q2 They are like children who do not obey their parents.
\q2 They disobey the commands that I, Yahweh, gave them in my law.
\q1 \v 10 They tell the prophets who see visions,
\q2 ‘Stop seeing visions that show us the right things to do.
\q1 See visions about things that are not true,
\q2 and tell us that we are doing good things.
\q1 \v 11 Tell us that we can live in a way
\q2 that is not the way Yahweh wants people to live.
\q1 Stop confronting us with messages from
\q2 the holy God of us Israelites!’”
\p
\v 12 Because the people are saying these things,
\q2 this is what the holy God of the Israelites says:
\q1 “{I am going to punish you} because you are refusing to obey what I have told you.
\q2 Instead, you are relying on cheating and oppressing other people.
\q1 \v 13 Sometimes a crack spreads in a high wall and causes it to bulge.
\q2 When that happens, the wall collapses very suddenly.
\q2 Because you are sinning in this way, I will punish you just as suddenly.
\q1 \v 14 I will smash you the way someone smashes a clay jar
\q1 into tiny pieces without leaving any large pieces.
\q2 It will be as if there is not a single shard among the broken pieces
\q2 big enough to take coals from a fireplace
\q2 or to scoop water from a cistern.”
\p
\v 15 Yahweh the Lord, the holy God of the Israelites, also says this:
\q1 “If you repent and depend on me, I will save you.
\q2 If you quietly trust me, I will make you strong.
\q2 However, you refuse to do that.
\q1 \v 16 Instead, you said, ‘No! We will escape {from trouble}
\q2 by riding on fast horses {that the Egyptians will give us}!’
\q2 But you will have to run away to try to escape {from your enemies},
\q2 and they will be riding on fast horses as they chase you.
\q1 \v 17 A thousand of your soldiers will run away when only one enemy soldier resists them.
\q2 When five of them resist you, your army will run away.
\q1 Those who remain will be alone,
\q2 like a flagpole on a mountaintop
\q2 or a signal flag on top of a hill.”
\b
\q1 \v 18 Even so, Yahweh wants to be kind to you;
\q2 yes, he is eager to treat you mercifully.
\q1 Yahweh is a God who always does what is just.
\q2 Those who wait patiently for him to act have a truly good life.
\p
\v 19 Someday you Israelites will once again live in the city of Jerusalem on Mount Zion. You will not be sad anymore. When you call out to Yahweh for help, he will surely treat you kindly. As soon as he hears you cry out, he will help you.
\v 20 The Lord will allow you to experience suffering and trouble as though they were your food and drink. But then he, your teacher, will not hide himself from you anymore. You will see him personally.
\v 21 Whenever you are about to do something that is wrong, he will speak to you and tell you the right thing to do instead, as if he were a guide walking behind you and telling you how to stay on the right path.
\v 22 At that time, you will reject the idols you have made for yourselves and covered with silver and gold. You will throw them away as you would throw away filthy rags. It will be as if you say to each one, “Get away from us!”
\p
\v 23 Then Yahweh will send rain so that the seeds you plant in the ground will grow. The crops that the ground produces will be plentiful and healthy. Your livestock will graze in large, open pastures at that time.
\v 24 The oxen and the donkeys that plow your ground will eat grain that farmers have sorted with shovels and pitchforks and to which they have added salt {for flavor}.
\v 25 At that time, Yahweh will kill many of the enemy soldiers who are attacking you, and you will knock down their {siege} towers. Then streams of water will flow down from every high mountain and every tall hill.
\v 26 The moon will shine as brightly as the sun. The sun will shine seven times more brightly than it does now. Each day will have as much light as seven days have now. This will happen when Yahweh repairs the harm that his people have suffered.
\q1 \v 27 Listen to this! Yahweh is coming from far away,
\q2 and it is clear that he is very angry {with his enemies}.
\q2 What he is saying shows that he is furious
\q2 and that he is going to destroy his enemies as if he were a fire that would burn them up.
\q1 \v 28 His anger, which is evident from his heavy breathing,
\q2 is like a flood that rises as high as a person’s neck.
\q1 He will totally destroy his enemies,
\q2 just as only chaff and dust come through a sieve.
\q1 It will be as if they are animals on whose jaws Yahweh puts a bridle
\q2 so that he can lead them to a place where someone will slaughter them.
\q1 \v 29 However, his people will sing joyfully
\q2 as they do on the night when they celebrate a festival.
\q1 They will feel very happy,
\q2 as people do when they walk, playing flutes, up Mount Zion
\q1 to come to {the temple of} Yahweh.
\q2 He is like a rock on which the Israelites are safe.
\q1 \v 30 Yahweh will shout with his powerful voice.
\q2 He will show how he strikes with his mighty power.
\q1 His wrath will be as destructive as lightning
\q2 in a storm with heavy rain, strong winds, and hailstones.
\q1 \v 31 When they realize that Yahweh is powerfully destroying them,
\q2 the Assyrians will become very afraid.
\q1 \v 32 Yahweh will fight against the Assyrians {and destroy them}
\q2 by using his tremendous power.
\q1 His people will {sing and} play tambourines and harps
\q2 to celebrate each thing that Yahweh does to destroy the Assyrians.
\q1 \v 33 It is as if Yahweh prepared a place long ago in Tophet
\q2 to burn the body of the king of Assyria.
\q1 It is as if he made a deep, wide pile from much firewood.
\q1 It is as if Yahweh will set all that wood on fire
\q2 with his own breath, which will be like a stream of burning sulfur.
\c 31
\q1 \v 1 How terrible it will be for those who depend on the Egyptians to help them!
\q2 Such people believe that Egypt’s many horses, chariots, and horsemen will protect them.
\q1 They do not depend on Yahweh, the holy God of the Israelites,
\q2 and they do not ask him to help them.
\q1 \v 2 {They believe the Egyptians are wise,} but Yahweh is also wise,
\q2 He has determined to allow the Judeans to experience great trouble,
\q2 and he is going to do what he has said he will do.
\q1 He will punish the people who do what is wrong,
\q2 and he will also punish anyone who would help them do wrong things.
\q1 \v 3 The Egyptians are only humans. They are not God!
\q2 Their horses are merely animals, not powerful spirits!
\q1 Yahweh will enable enemies to defeat the Egyptians,
\q2 whom the Judeans expected to help them.
\q2 Then those enemies will also defeat the Judeans,
\q2 and they and the Egyptians will perish together.
\p
\v 4 Yahweh said this to me:
\q1 “When a strong, young lion kills a sheep,
\q2 it growls as it eats it {to warn other animals away}.
\q2 The shepherd of that sheep may call many other shepherds
\q2 to come and chase that lion away.
\q1 The shepherds may shout at it and make noise,
\q2 but that does not frighten the lion. It does not run away.
\q1 In the same way, I, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 will come down to fight from the height of Mount Zion {and nothing will deter me}.
\q1 \v 5 I, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, will protect Jerusalem
\q2 the way a mother bird hovers over its nest to protect its baby birds.
\q1 I will protect the city and rescue its people from their enemies.”
\p
\v 6 You Israelites have greatly rebelled against Yahweh. Repent and obey him once more.
\v 7 When you do that, each of you will throw away the silver and gold idols that you made for yourselves. It was a sin to make and worship those idols.
\q1 \v 8 “No human army will destroy the Assyrian army.
\q2 I, Yahweh, will destroy it.
\q1 The Assyrian soldiers who survive will run away from the battlefield.
\q2 The Judeans will capture many of their young soldiers
\q2 and make them slaves who have to work hard for them.
\q1 \v 9 The Assyrian king, whose soldiers considered to be their strong leader,
\q2 will be so afraid that he runs away.
\q2 The Assyrian commanders will panic when they realize that I, Yahweh, am attacking them.”
\q1 This is what Yahweh declares.
\q2 From his temple in Jerusalem on Mount Zion,
\q2 he destroys his enemies as if he were a great fire burning them up.
\c 32
\q1 \v 1 Listen! Someday a king will rule who does what is right.
\q2 His officials will help him govern fairly.
\q1 \v 2 Each of those rulers will protect the people,
\q2 like a shelter that protects people from wind and storms.
\q2 or like the shadow of a huge rock in a hot place.
\q1 Each one will provide for them,
\q2 like a brook flowing through a dry place.
\b
\q1 \v 3 People will look carefully at what Yahweh wants to show them,
\q2 and they will listen carefully to what Yahweh wants to tell them.
\q1 \v 4 People will take the time to think carefully
\q2 so that they understand what Yahweh wants them to know.
\q2 People who now cannot speak well will speak fluently and clearly.
\q1 \v 5 People will stop saying that foolish people are noble.
\q2 People will not say that scoundrels are honorable.
\q1 \v 6 After all, foolish people say foolish things,
\q2 and they think about doing wrong things.
\q1 They say {and believe} things about Yahweh that are not true,
\q2 and so they do not live as he wants people to live.
\q1 They do not give food to hungry people,
\q2 and they do not give water to thirsty people.
\q1 \v 7 A scoundrel plans to do wicked things,
\q2 and he uses evil methods to accomplish them.
\q1 He plans to ruin poor people by telling lies about them in court,
\q2 even though those poor people deserve to win their cases.
\q1 \v 8 But an honorable person plans to do honorable things,
\q2 and he commits himself to doing what is honorable.
\b
\q1 \v 9 You women who are so proud and complacent,
\q1 pay attention and listen to what I say!
\q1 \v 10 In a little more than a year,
\q2 you who are so complacent now will tremble with fear.
\q2 That is because no one will harvest any grapes
\q1 or gather any other crops.
\q1 \v 11 You women who are so proud and complacent should shake with fear.
\q2 Take off your fancy clothes
\q1 and instead wear rough cloth {to show that you are sorry for your sins}.
\q1 \v 12 Beat your breasts to mourn for the good grainfields
\q2 and the grapevines that now produce much fruit.
\q1 \v 13 Do that because only thorns and briars will grow
\q2 on the ground that belongs to us Israelites.
\q1 In fact, they will also grow in the houses in which people now live happily
\q2 in cities where people enjoy themselves.
\q1 \v 14 That is because people will abandon their expensive homes
\q2 and crowds will no longer gather in the cities.
\q1 For a long time, animals will use empty hilltop forts and watchtowers as their dens.
\q2 Wild donkeys will roam and flocks will graze where people once lived.
\q1 \v 15 {It will be like that} until Yahweh sends his Spirit to us from heaven.
\q2 Then the deserts will become fertile farmland,
\q2 and the fertile farmland will become great forests.
\q1 \v 16 Then people will act fairly and do what is right
\q2 throughout the land,
\q1 both in places where people have settled
\q2 and in places where they do not live.
\q1 \v 17 When people do what is right, they will live peacefully.
\q2 When people live in a way that is right, they will always live quietly and safely.
\q1 \v 18 We Israelites will live peacefully in our homes,
\q2 We will have safe places where we can stay
\q2 and quiet places where we can rest.
\q1 \v 19 Yahweh will destroy the Assyrians and their cities
\q2 the way a severe hailstorm knocks down trees in a forest.
\q1 \v 20 But we Israelites will have truly good lives.
\q2 We will plant seeds wherever streams will water them,
\q2 and we will let our cattle and donkeys go wherever they want.
\c 33
\q1 \v 1 How terrible it will be for the nation that has destroyed others,
\q2 which has broken its agreements with others!
\q1 No one has destroyed it yet,
\q1 but when it stops destroying others,
\q2 others will destroy it.
\q1 No one has broken an agreement with it yet,
\q1 but when it stops breaking its agreements with others,
\q2 others will break their agreements with it.
\q1 \v 2 Yahweh, we are waiting for you to help us,
\q2 so please be kind to us,
\q1 Strengthen us each day,
\q2 and rescue us when we suffer distress.
\q1 \v 3 When our enemies hear the loud noise {of you approaching}, they run away.
\q2 When you show how powerful you are, they scatter.
\q1 \v 4 Then we will swarm like locusts over their valuable possessions
\q2 and take them all away, just as caterpillars eat all the leaves off plants.
\q1 \v 5 Yahweh, who lives in heaven, is greater than anyone else.
\q2 He will lead the people who live in Jerusalem on Mount Zion
\q2 to do what is fair and right.
\q1 \v 6 He will make them secure for as long as they live.
\q2 He will rescue them powerfully,
\q2 and he will enable them to know and understand important things.
\q1 These things will happen for the people
\q2 because they greatly revere Yahweh.
\b
\q1 \v 7 But now brave warriors are crying out loud in the streets.
\q1 The ambassadors whom the Judeans sent to make peace
\q2 are weeping very sadly {because they did not succeed}.
\q1 \v 8 People do not travel on the roads anymore.
\q2 The Assyrian king has broken the peace treaty he made,
\q2 even though he made it in the presence of witnesses.
\q2 He does not care what people think of him.
\q1 \v 9 People are not working in the fields, so they are not producing crops.
\q2 No one is harvesting the trees in the great forests of Lebanon,
\q2 so they are becoming wild again.
\q1 The fertile plain of Sharon has become like a desert.
\q2 Crops are not growing in the agricultural areas of Bashan and Carmel;
\q2 it is as if the leaves have dropped off the plants there.
\b
\q1 \v 10 Yahweh says, “Now I am going to take action.
\q2 Now I will show how very powerful I am.
\q1 \v 11 The enemy nations will not succeed in conquering Judah.
\q2 Their own hostility will destroy them, as if a fire had burned them up.
\q1 \v 12 It will be as if a fire burns those nations completely to ashes.
\q2 They will be like thornbushes that people cut down and burn up.
\q1 \v 13 People throughout the world should listen to what I am going to do
\q2 and recognize how powerful I am.”
\b
\q1 \v 14 Sinful people in Jerusalem are afraid;
\q2 godless people tremble uncontrollably with fear.
\q1 They say, “None of us will survive in the presence of Yahweh!
\q2 He is about to destroy wicked people as if he were a fire that was burning them up continually!”
\q1 \v 15-16 These are the people who will be safe,
\q2 as if they were on the top of a cliff:
\q1 people who do what is right
\q2 and speak honestly;
\q1 people who do not try to get rich by extorting others
\q2 and who refuse to accept bribes;
\q1 people who refuse to consider doing evil
\q2 such as by attacking others violently.
\q1 God will always make sure that such people
\q2 have the food and drink that they need.
\b
\q1 \v 17 You people of Judah will see your king reigning splendidly.
\q2 You will see that his kingdom is very large.
\q1 \v 18 You will think about when the enemy terrified you {and you will say},
\q2 “The Assyrian officers who gathered their soldiers are gone! The Assyrian officials who collected tribute from us are gone!
\q2 The Assyrian officers who built siege towers against us are gone!”
\q1 \v 19 You will no longer see those proud people
\q2 who spoke a language that you could not understand.
\q1 \v 20 Instead, when you look at Mount Zion,
\q2 you will see that the city of Jerusalem,
\q1 where we celebrate our annual festivals,
\q2 has become a safe place to live.
\q2 No one will ever be able to destroy it so that it is not there anymore.
\q1 \v 21 That is because Yahweh will be our mighty protector there.
\q1 He will protect Jerusalem the way a wide river protects a city,
\q2 and it will be as if no enemy warships
\q2 are able to approach the city on that river.
\q1 \v 22 It will be that way because Yahweh judges us.
\q2 Yahweh gives us our laws.
\q1 Yahweh rules us as our king.
\q2 He is the one who will save us.
\q1 \v 23 It is as if our enemies are trying to attack us on ships whose ropes hang loose
\q2 so that the masts do not stay securely in place
\q2 and the sailors can not spread the sail.
\q1 We will take many valuable possessions from our enemies
\q2 and divide them among ourselves.
\q2 Even people who can not fight as soldiers will be able to take valuable possessions.
\q1 \v 24 Yahweh will forgive the sins that the people who live in Jerusalem have committed.
\q2 As a result, those people will no longer say that they are suffering.
\c 34
\q1 \v 1 You people of other nations, come near
\q2 and listen carefully to what I am saying.
\q1 Everyone who lives on the earth should hear it.
\q1 \v 2 Yahweh is angry with the people of other nations,
\q2 and he is especially furious with their armies.
\q1 He has decided to destroy them completely,
\q2 and so he is going to allow others to kill them.
\q1 \v 3 No one will bury them,
\q2 so their dead bodies will stink.
\q1 So much blood will flow from their bodies
\q2 that it seem as if the mountains are liquid.
\q1 \v 4 All of the stars in the sky will disappear,
\q2 and the sky will also disappear,
\q2 the way a scroll disappears when someone rolls it up.
\q1 The stars will fade away
\q2 just as leaves fade and fall off grapevines
\q2 or figs shrivel up and drop from fig trees.
\b
\q1 \v 5 {Yahweh says,} “It is as if my sword drinks blood
\q2 and it has drunk all the blood it wants in the sky.
\q2 Now I will use my sword against the people of Edom.
\q2 I have decided to destroy those people completely.”
\q1 \v 6 Yahweh is going to kill many people
\q2 as if he were offering a sacrifice of many animals
\q2 in the city of Bozrah and throughout the land of Edom.
\q1 As a result, it will be as if his sword has much blood and fat on it
\q2 from slaughtering lambs, goats, and rams
\q2 and removing their fat and inner organs.
\q1 \v 7 It will even be as if Yahweh slaughters wild oxen
\q2 along with young and strong oxen from a herd.
\q1 It will be as if the land of Edom drinks blood
\q2 and has drunk all the blood it wants,
\q1 and it will be as if the ground there eats fat
\q2 and has become fat from eating so much of it.
\b
\q1 \v 8 This will happen because it will be the time when Yahweh takes revenge on Edom
\q2 and punishes them for what they did to the people of Judah.
\q1 \v 9 The rivers in Edom will flow with tar instead of water,
\q2 and the ground will be sulfur instead of soil.
\q1 Burning tar will cover the land.
\q1 \v 10 Fire will always be burning,
\q2 and the smoke from it will rise continually.
\q1 People will not live in Edom again in any future generation.
\q2 No one will ever even travel through that land.
\q1 \v 11 Pelicans, desert owls, screech owls, and ravens
\q2 will live there instead.
\q1 It will be as if Yahweh is using a measuring line
\q2 not to make sure that a building is the right size
\q2 but to make sure that no building is there.
\q1 It will be as if Yahweh is using a cord with weights on the end
\q2 not to make sure that walls are straight
\q2 but to make sure that there are no walls.
\q1 \v 12 There will be nothing in Edom that people would call a kingdom,
\q2 so it will not have any nobles or princes.
\q1 \v 13 Thorns, nettles, and thornbushes will grow up over the palaces and fortresses.
\q1 Jackals and ostriches will live in those ruins.
\q1 \v 14 Wild desert animals and hyenas will roam there,
\q2 and wild goats will call out to each other.
\q1 Animals that come out at night
\q2 will also make their dens there and live there.
\q1 \v 15 Great owls will build their nests there.
\q2 They will lay their eggs, hatch them,
\q2 and care for their young in the shade of those ruins.
\q1 Hawks will also gather there,
\q2 living near one another throughout those ruins.
\q1 \v 16 Yahweh has decided that every one of these creatures will be there.
\q2 None will be without its counterpart.
\q1 Yahweh has recorded this in a scroll,
\q1 and anyone can verify it by checking that scroll.
\q1 This will happen because Yahweh himself has commanded it,
\q2 and it will be his Spirit who gathers all those animals there.
\q1 \v 17 Yahweh has assigned each creature its own territory,
\q2 and he has measured out the land and divided it among them.
\q1 They will possess that land forever.
\q2 Their descendants will live there in every generation.”
\c 35
\q1 \v 1 Someday the wilderness and the dry desert will become fertile,
\q2 and the barren plains will produce wildflowers.
\q1 \v 2 The desert will surely produce flowers abundantly.
\q2 It will seem as if it is happily celebrating!
\q1 Yahweh will make it as beautiful as the forests of Lebanon
\q2 and as fertile as the plains of Carmel and Sharon.
\q1 There people will see how glorious Yahweh is.
\q2 Yes, they will see how magnificent our God is.
\b
\q1 \v 3 So encourage people who have become discouraged,
\q2 and strengthen people who are afraid.
\q1 \v 4 Say to those who are anxious,
\q2 “Do not be afraid but be brave
\q1 because your God is coming to punish his enemies as they deserve.
\q2 He himself will come and rescue you.”
\b
\q1 \v 5 Then Yahweh will enable blind people to see
\q2 and enable deaf people to hear.
\q1 \v 6 Then people who could not walk will jump around as deer do,
\q2 and people who could not speak will shout joyfully.
\q1 Then water will gush out from springs in the desert,
\q2 and streams will flow through the barren land.
\q1 \v 7 The ground that shimmered with heat will become a pool of water,
\q2 and springs will flow in land that once was dry.
\q1 Grass, reeds, and papyrus will grow
\q2 in places where jackals once lived.
\b
\q1 \v 8 There will be a highway through that land,
\q2 and people will call it “The Way of Holiness.”
\q1 Only people who live as God wants will travel on it.
\q2 No one who dishonors God will walk on that road,
\q2 and foolish people will not wander onto it.
\q1 \v 9 No lions will be there,
\q2 and no dangerous animals will go along that road.
\q1 Only the people whom God has rescued will walk on it.
\q2 \v 10 The people whom Yahweh has set free will return
\q2 and come to Zion, singing joyfully.
\q1 They will always be happy,
\q2 as if they are wearing joy as a crown on their heads.
\q1 It will be as if great happiness caught up with them on the road
\q2 and has stayed with them.
\q2 They will not sigh with sadness anymore.
\c 36
\p
\v 1 After Hezekiah had been ruling Judah for almost 14 years, King Sennacherib of Assyria led his army to attack all of the cities in Judah that had walls around them. They started capturing these cities one by one.
\v 2 The king of Assyria sent {one of his most important officials,} the Rabshakeh, with a large army from the city of Lachish to persuade King Hezekiah to surrender. When the army reached Jerusalem, they stopped alongside the aqueduct through which water flowed from the Upper Pool into Jerusalem. They stopped at the place where it went under the road that led to the Field of the Fuller.
\v 3 Hezekiah sent three of his officials to speak with him. He sent Eliakim son of Hilkiah, who was in charge of the palace. He also sent Shebna, his official secretary, and Joah son of Asaph, the official who reported to the people everything that Hezekiah decided they should do.
\v 4 The Rabshakeh told them to tell Hezekiah, “The great king, the king of Assyria, says that you have no reason to expect that anyone will help you fight against him.
\v 5 You claim that you have a strong enough army and good enough plans to resist him. But that is only talk. {You can not resist him alone, and} you can not depend on anyone else to help you. So you should not have rebelled against him.
\v 6 In fact, you are relying on the army of Egypt to help you. But you must realize that that is like using a broken reed as a staff for support. The pointed end of such a staff would make a hole in the hand of anyone who tried to use it! That is what Pharaoh, the king of Egypt, is like. He hurts anyone who relies on him for help.”
\v 7 {The Rabshakeh then said to Hezekiah’s officials,} “Perhaps you will tell me that you are relying on Yahweh your God to help you. But he {will not help you, because} Hezekiah has insulted him by destroying his hilltop shrines and the altars on which the Judeans were offering sacrifices to him. Hezekiah has required everyone who lives in Jerusalem and elsewhere in Judah to worship only at the altar in Jerusalem.
\v 8 {So tell Hezekiah for me that} I am offering him a deal on behalf of my master, the king of Assyria. I will give him 2,000 horses if he still has enough healthy soldiers to ride on them!
\v 9 But also tell him that I know that even if I gave him those horses, he would not be able to defeat {the squad of} one of the least important officers in our army. Tell him I know that is why he is hoping that the king of Egypt will send chariots and soldiers on horseback to help him.
\v 10 Furthermore, do not think that we have come to destroy Judah without Yahweh’s help. It is Yahweh himself who told us to come here and destroy this land!”
\p
\v 11 Then Eliakim told the Rabshakeh on behalf of himself and Shebna and Joah, “Sir, please speak to us in your own Aramaic language. We understand it. Do not keep speaking to us in our Judean language. The people who are standing on the wall can {hear and} understand you{, and you are frightening them}.”
\v 12 But the Rabshakeh told Eliakim, “You should not think that my master sent me to say these things only to your king and to you and not also to the people on the wall who are listening! {If you do not surrender, we will besiege the city, and soon} they will have nothing to eat but their own dung and nothing to drink but their own urine. The three of you will also have nothing else to eat or drink.”
\v 13 Then the Rabshakeh directly faced the people on the wall. He shouted to them in Judean, “Listen to this message from the great king, the king of Assyria.
\v 14 He says that you must not allow Hezekiah to deceive you. Hezekiah will not be able to rescue you from his army.
\v 15 The king says that you must not allow Hezekiah to persuade you to rely on Yahweh by telling you that Yahweh will certainly rescue you and not allow {the army of} the king of Assyria to capture this city!
\v 16 Do not let Hezekiah persuade you! The king of Assyria says that you should come out of the city and surrender to him. He says that if you do that, {instead of being very hungry as you are now,} each of you will be able to eat the grapes and figs you have grown and drink water from your own wells.
\v 17 He promises that you will be able to do that until his soldiers come and take you to a land that is like your land. It will be a land where you can grow grain to make bread and grow grapes to make wine.
\v 18 The king says that you must not believe Hezekiah, because he is deceiving you when he says that Yahweh will rescue you. The king reminds you that none of the gods that the people of other nations worship have been able to prevent him from conquering those nations!
\v 19 The gods of Hamath and Arpad did not come and rescue the people who lived there! The gods of Sepharvaim did not come and rescue the people who lived there! And even the gods of Samaria did not prevent me from conquering it!
\v 20 The king reminds you that none of these gods prevented him from conquering the people who worshiped them. So do not think that Yahweh will prevent him from conquering Jerusalem.”
\p
\v 21 King Hezekiah had commanded the people, “When the Rabshakeh speaks to you, do not answer him.” So the people who were listening did not say anything in reply to him.
\v 22 Then Eliakim, Shebna, and Joah tore their clothes {because they felt great distress}. They went back to Hezekiah and told him what the Rabshakeh had said.
\c 37
\p
\v 1 When King Hezekiah heard what the Rabshakeh had said, {to show what great distress he was feeling,} he tore his regular clothes and wore rough cloth instead. Then he went into {the courtyard of} Yahweh’s temple {to pray}.
\v 2 Then Hezekiah sent Eliakim, Shebna, and some of the older priests to go and speak with the prophet Isaiah son of Amoz. All those men were also wearing rough cloth {to show their distress}.
\v 3 The men told Isaiah, “King Hezekiah says that we are now in great trouble. Yahweh seems to be punishing us for our sins. Foreigners are insulting us {because we do not seem to have a God who can help us}. Our lives are in danger, like the lives of the mother and child when it is time for a woman to give birth but she does not have the strength to push her baby out.
\v 4 The Rabshakeh insulted the only true God. That was what his master, the king of Assyria, sent him to do. Perhaps Yahweh your God heard what he said. Perhaps he will punish him for saying those things. King Hezekiah asks you to pray for those of us who are still alive here in Jerusalem.”
\v 5 After the messengers from Hezekiah told Isaiah these things,
\v 6 Isaiah told them to go back to the king and tell him that Yahweh said to him, “Those soldiers of the king of Assyria insulted me {by saying that I was no better than the false gods that other nations worship}. You have learned what they said, but you should not let it make you afraid.
\v 7 I am going to send a spirit to Sennacherib {that will make him anxious}. Then he will hear a rumor that will worry him so much that he will return to his own country. There some men will assassinate him.”
\v 8 To report what had happened in Jerusalem, the Rabshakeh went to Libnah. He went there because he learned that the Assyrian army had left Lachish. The king of Assyria was then leading an attack against Libnah.
\v 9 Soon after that, King Sennacherib received a report that King Tirhakah of Cush was leading his army to attack them. {So he did not attack Jerusalem right away, but instead} he sent more messengers to King Hezekiah.
\v 10 The king told his messengers to tell Hezekiah, “Yahweh your God is promising you that my army will never capture Jerusalem. You trust Yahweh, but what he is saying is not true, so do not believe him.
\v 11 You have certainly heard what the armies of the kings of Assyria have done to all the other lands they invaded. They have completely destroyed them. So do not think that you will escape!
\v 12 The gods of other nations did not rescue them. Instead, the armies of previous kings of Assyria destroyed them. That is what happened to the people of Gozan, Haran, and Rezeph and the Edenites who lived in Tel Assar.
\v 13 The Assyrians defeated and killed the kings of Hamath and Arpad and the cities of Sepharvaim, Hena, and Ivvah.”
\p
\v 14 The messengers gave Hezekiah a letter from the Assyrian king that said the same thing they had told him. Hezekiah read the letter. Then he went to {the courtyard of} Yahweh’s temple. The Assyrian king had written the letter on a scroll, and Hezekiah unrolled the scroll so that the writing would be visible as he prayed to Yahweh.
\v 15 Then Hezekiah prayed to Yahweh,
\v 16 “Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, you are the God of us Israelites. You are present in a special way above the statues of the winged creatures that are above the sacred chest in your temple. You are the only true God. You rule all the kingdoms on this earth. You are the one who created everything on the earth and in the heavens.
\v 17 So, Yahweh, please pay attention to me as I speak, and look at what the king of Assyria has written. Listen {as I read it aloud} to what King Sennacherib has said to insult you, the only true God.
\v 18 Yahweh, it is true that the armies of the kings of Assyria have completely destroyed many people groups and the lands in which they were living.
\v 19 It is also true that they have burned up the idols of those nations. But those were not really gods. They were only wooden or stone statues. Humans made them, and that is why the Assyrians were able to destroy them so easily.
\v 20 So now, Yahweh our God, please prevent the king of Assyria from conquering us. That way the people in all the kingdoms of the world will know that you, Yahweh, are the only true God.”
\p
\v 21 Then Isaiah son of Amoz sent a message to Hezekiah. He told him, “Yahweh, the God of us Israelites, wants to tell you something in answer to your prayer to him about Sennacherib, the king of Assyria.
\v 22 This is what Yahweh says about him. The people of Jerusalem will belittle him and make fun of him. They will shake their heads to ridicule him as he runs away from the city with his army.
\v 23 Yahweh says that the Assyrian king should not have insulted and made fun of him, the holy God of the Israelites. He should not have shouted at him or proudly looked up at the sky.
\v 24 Yahweh says that the Assyrian king sent messengers to make fun of him. Through those messengers, {boasting about his military conquests,} he said that it was as if he had led his many chariots to the highest and most distant mountains in Lebanon. He said it was as if he and his soldiers had cut down the tallest cedar trees and best cypress trees in Lebanon. {Boasting that he would now conquer Jerusalem,} he said that it was as if he and his army would now go to the place where people most want to go in Lebanon. That is a place where workers have made a beautiful park in the midst of the forest.
\v 25 Yahweh says that the Assyrian king is boasting that {in order to march through desolate regions,} he and his soldiers have dug wells in other countries to get water to drink. He is also boasting that he will conquer Matsor{, which people also call Egypt,} as easily as a person can stamp out a small puddle of water.
\p
\v 26 But Yahweh says that the Assyrian king should realize that he planned long ago for those things to happen. Now he is causing them to occur. That is why the king’s army has been capturing and destroying many cities that had high walls around them.
\v 27 The people who lived in those cities were not able to resist the Assyrians. This discouraged them and shamed them. The Assyrians destroyed them as easily as someone can cut down green plants that are growing in a field. The Assyrians destroyed them just as the heat of the sun destroys grass on the roof of a house before it can grow.
\v 28 But, Yahweh says, he knows where the king of Assyria lives. He knows when he leaves home and when he returns. He knows when the king yells at him angrily.
\v 29 Yahweh says that he has heard the king yelling at him proudly and angrily. As a result, he is going to force him to return to his own country {without conquering Jerusalem} by the same route that he took to get here. It will be as if Yahweh puts a hook in his nose and a bit in his mouth in order to make him go where he wants him to go.
\p
\v 30 Here is a sign for you, Hezekiah{, to show that this message is truly from Yahweh}. This year, you and your people will eat wild grain. Next year, you will eat the grain that grows from the seeds of those wild plants. But the following year, you Israelites will be able to plant grain and harvest it. You will be able to plant vineyards and eat the grapes.
\v 31 The people in Judah who remain alive will once again prosper and have many children, like plants whose roots go deep down into the ground and that produce much fruit.
\v 32 People will survive the Assyrian invasion. Afterwards, many of them will move from Jerusalem on Mount Zion to resettle other parts of Judah. Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, will eagerly make this happen.
\v 33 Yahweh declares that the king of Assyria will not bring his army to this city. His soldiers will not shoot a single arrow at it. His soldiers will not appear outside of it carrying shields. They will not build a high mound of dirt against the city wall to try to get over it.
\v 34 Yahweh declares that the king of Assyria will not lead his army to this city. Instead, they will return to their own country by the same route that they took to get here.
\v 35 Yahweh says that he will defend this city and prevent the Assyrians from destroying it. He will do that to show that he is the one true God and because of what he promised to his servant David{, that his descendants would always rule in Jerusalem}.”
\p
\v 36 That night, an angel representing Yahweh went to the place where the Assyrian soldiers had set up their tents. He killed 185, 000 of the soldiers. When the rest of the soldiers woke up the next morning, they saw the dead bodies of all those soldiers.
\v 37 Then King Sennacherib had his soldiers take down their tents, and he led them away from Israel. They returned to Assyria, and Sennacherib remained in the city of Nineveh.
\v 38 One day King Sennacherib was worshiping in the temple of his god Nisroch. {Two of his sons,} Adrammelech and Sharezer, killed him with their swords. Then they escaped to the region of Ararat. Sennacherib’s {other} son Esarhaddon became the next king of Assyria.
\c 38
\p
\v 1 Around that time, Hezekiah became so sick that he was in danger of dying. The prophet Isaiah son of Amoz came to him and told him, “Yahweh says that you are not going to recover from this illness. Instead, you are going to die. So you should tell the people in your palace what you want them to do after you die.”
\v 2 Hezekiah turned his face toward the wall {so that he could have privacy} and prayed to Yahweh.
\v 3 He said, “Yahweh, please do not forget that I have served you loyally and I have devoted myself entirely to you. I have done things that pleased you.” Then Hezekiah started to cry loudly.
\v 4 Yahweh then gave Isaiah a message. He said,
\v 5 “Go back to Hezekiah and tell him that I, Yahweh, say this. I am the God whom his ancestor King David worshiped. I have heard what he prayed. I have seen how he has been crying. Tell him that {I will heal him and} enable him to live for 15 more years.
\v 6 Promise him that I will prevent the king of Assyria from defeating him and conquering this city. I will defend this city.
\v 7 I, Yahweh, am going to do something that will prove to him that I will do what I have promised.
\v 8 On the stairway that King Ahaz had his workers build {as a sundial}, the shadow of the sun has gone down ten steps. I am going to make it go back up those ten steps.” And the sun did go back up the ten steps that it had gone down.
\p
\v 9 When King Hezekiah was almost well again, he wrote this song:
\q1 \v 10 I said to myself, “In the middle of my life,
\q2 I am about to die and go to the place where dead people are.
\q2 Yahweh is not going to allow me to live a full lifetime.
\q1 \v 11 I said, “I will never see Yahweh again
\q2 in this world where people are alive.
\q1 I will not see my friends again
\q2 or any other people who now are alive in this world.
\q1 \v 12 It is as if my life is a tent
\q2 that a shepherd has taken down and carried away.
\q1 When a weaver has finished weaving a piece of cloth,
\q2 he cuts it off from the loom and rolls it up.
\q1 It felt as if Yahweh had cut off my life in the same way.
\q2 It felt as if the sickness he allowed was steadily killing me.
\q1 \v 13 I could not sleep, but I waited patiently for morning.
\q2 I was in such pain that I felt as if a lion were tearing me apart.
\q2 It felt as if the sickness Yahweh allowed was steadily killing me.
\q1 \v 14 I moaned painfully so that I sounded like
\q2 a swallow or a crane chirping or a dove cooing.
\q1 I looked up toward heaven for help for so long that my eyes became tired.
\q2 I called out, ‘Lord, I am suffering such distress! Please help me!’
\q1 \v 15 But there was really nothing that I could say to him,
\q2 since he had told me that he had decided to allow this illness.
\q1 I felt very sad that I was going to die,
\q2 and I knew that I should behave humbly for as long as I was alive.
\q1 \v 16 Lord, what you say and do enables all people to live,
\q2 and what you have said and done just now has enabled me to live.
\q1 Yes, you have healed me
\q2 and allowed me to continue living!
\q1 \v 17 I suffered badly, but in fact, that was good for me.
\q2 You forgave all of my sins,
\q2 and you rescued me from dying and not existing anymore.
\q1 \v 18 After all, in the place where dead people are,
\q2 no one sings songs to praise you.
\q1 Those who have gone down into that place
\q2 can not expect you to do things for them faithfully.
\q1 \v 19 Only people who are still alive thank you,
\q2 as I am doing now.
\q1 People who are alive tell their children
\q2 how you helped them because you are faithful.
\q1 \v 20 Yahweh will enable me to recover fully.
\q2 Then we will sing songs to praise him
\q1 while others play musical instruments.
\q2 We will praise Yahweh in his temple for as long as we live.”
\p
\v 21 Isaiah had told Hezekiah’s servants, “Make a paste from figs and put it on the sore place on Hezekiah’s body. Then he will recover.”
\v 22 Hezekiah had asked Isaiah {when he told him that Yahweh would heal him}, “What sign will Yahweh give me to show {that he is going to heal me and} that I will be able to go and worship him in his temple?”
\c 39
\p
\v 1 Merodach Baladan had become the king of Babylon after his father Baladan died. Around this time, he learned that King Hezekiah had been very sick, so he sent messengers to him. They brought a gift to Hezekiah, along with a letter {that said how glad Merodach Baladan was that Hezekiah had recovered}.
\v 2 When the messengers arrived, Hezekiah listened as they read the letter to him from Berodach Baladan. Then he showed them all the valuable possessions he was keeping in his palace. He showed them the silver and the gold, the spices, the special olive oil, and the weapons for his soldiers. He showed them all the valuable things that were in his storerooms. The messengers saw all the things that were in Hezekiah’s palace and in other places in his kingdom.
\v 3 Then the prophet Isaiah went to King Hezekiah and asked him, “Where did those men come from, and what did they say to you?” Hezekiah replied, “They came from a country that is far from here. They came from Babylon.”
\v 4 Isaiah asked, “What did they see in your palace?” Hezekiah replied, “I showed them everything in my palace. I showed them all the valuable things that are in my storerooms.”
\p
\v 5 Then Isaiah told him, “Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, has given me this message for you.
\v 6 “There will truly be a time when the Babylonian army comes and takes away all the valuable things in your palace. That includes the things that your ancestors have put there over the years. They will leave nothing valuable here.” This is what Yahweh is telling you.
\v 7 He is also telling you, “The Babylonian army will force some of your own descendants to go to Babylon. There they will be eunuchs in the royal palace.”
\v 8 Then Hezekiah replied to Isaiah, “I accept what Yahweh has said in the message that you have delivered.” He said that because he was thinking, “Even if that happens, the people of my kingdom will live peacefully and safely for as long as I am alive.”
\c 40
\q1 \v 1 Our God says, “Comfort my people!
\q2 Yes, comfort them!
\q1 \v 2 Speak tenderly to the people of Jerusalem
\q2 and announce to them
\q1 that their time of suffering has ended.
\q2 Tell them that I have forgiven them for what they did wrong
\q1 and that I, Yahweh, have punished them enough
\q2 for all of their sins.”
\b
\q1 \v 3 I hear someone shouting,
\q2 “Make a road for Yahweh in the wilderness!
\q2 Yes, clear a straight path through the desert for our God!
\q1 \v 4 Fill in the valleys
\q2 and flatten the mountains and hills.
\q1 Level the uneven ground
\q2 and smooth out the rough places.
\q1 \v 5 Then Yahweh will show how glorious he is,
\q2 and people throughout the world will see it at the same time.”
\q2 This will surely happen, because Yahweh himself has said it will happen.
\b
\q1 \v 6 I heard someone say, “Shout!”
\q2 I responded, “What should I shout?”
\q1 {The answer was,} “People are {temporary,} like grass.
\q2 Their loyalty is completely {unreliable,} like wildflowers {that bloom only briefly}.
\q1 \v 7 Grass dries up and flowers wilt
\q2 when Yahweh makes a hot wind blow on them.
\q2 Certainly people {live only for a short time,} like grass.
\q1 \v 8 Yes, grass dries up and flowers wilt,
\q2 but our God always keeps his promises.”
\b
\q1 \v 9 You people of Jerusalem, you have good news to tell!
\q2 Go up on a high mountain and shout it out!
\q1 Shout it loudly, and do not be afraid!
\q1 Tell the people of the other cities of Judah,
\q2 “Listen, our God is coming!”
\q1 \v 10 Listen! Yahweh the Lord is coming powerfully,
\q2 and he will use his great strength to rule.
\q1 Listen! He is bringing something good with him.
\q1 \v 11 He will take care of his people
\q2 the way a shepherd takes care of his sheep.
\q1 It will be as if some of his people are young lambs
\q2 that he gently gathers and holds close to his chest,
\q2 and it will be as if others of his people are mother sheep
\q2 that he leads carefully as they are nursing their lambs.
\b
\q1 \v 12 No human being has used the hollow of his hand
\q2 to measure the water of the oceans.
\q2 No human being has measured the sky
\q2 by using the distance between his thumb and little finger as a unit.
\q1 No human being has determined how much dust is on the earth
\q2 by putting it in large baskets.
\q1 No human being has used a scale or a balance
\q2 to weigh the mountains and hills.
\q1 \v 13 No one has ever understood how vast Yahweh’s Spirit is,
\q2 and no one has ever been his counselor and given him advice.
\q1 \v 14 Yahweh has never consulted anyone to help him understand things.
\q2 He did not need anyone to teach him how to judge fairly.
\q1 No one has ever taught him anything he did not already know.
\q2 He did not need anyone to show him how to think wisely.
\b
\q1 \v 15 Listen! From Yahweh’s perspective, the nations are as insignificant
\q2 as one drop of water is to a whole bucketful of water.
\q1 They are as insignificant as dust on a set of scales{,
\q2 which makes basically no difference in weighing}.
\q1 Listen! Yahweh could pick up all the lands by the sea
\q2 as easily as he could pick up a tiny grain of sand on the beach.
\q1 \v 16 If someone wanted to offer Yahweh the sacrifice he deserves,
\q2 the wood from all the trees in Lebanon would not make a big enough fire,
\q2 and there would not be enough animals there.
\q1 \v 17 From Yahweh’s perspective, all the nations are so insignificant
\q2 it is as if they do not even exist.
\b
\q1 \v 18 So there is no one to whom one can compare God.
\q2 There is nothing at all that resembles him.
\q1 \v 19 An idol {is certainly not like him}! A craftsman shapes an idol out of metal,
\q2 and then another worker covers it with a thin layer of gold
\q2 and makes silver chains to hang on it.
\q1 \v 20 A poor person who cannot afford gold or silver
\q2 chooses a piece of wood that will not rot.
\q1 Then he finds a capable woodworker
\q2 to carve an idol for him from it
\q2 that will not topple over.
\b
\q1 \v 21 Surely all of you know this!
\q2 Surely you have heard about it!
\q1 Someone must have told you about it long ago.
\q2 People should have understood it since God created the earth.
\q1 \v 22 God sits on his throne {in heaven} above the earth.
\q2 From there, its people look as tiny as grasshoppers to him.
\q1 {When he created it,} God stretched out the sky
\q2 the way people stretch out a curtain
\q2 or set up a tent to live in.
\q1 \v 23 He is able to take away the power of rulers
\q2 and make those who judge other people’s cases insignificant.
\q1 \v 24 They are like plants that are just beginning to grow from seeds,
\q2 whose roots are just beginning to go down into the ground.
\q1 It is as if God merely blows on them and they dry up,
\q2 and then a windstorm carries them away as if they were straw.
\b
\q1 \v 25 So the holy God says,
\q2 “There is no one to whom you can compare me!
\q2 No one is equal to me!”
\q1 \v 26 Look up at the sky and realize
\q2 that God created all those stars!
\q1 He is the one who brings every one of them out at night.
\q2 He summons each star by name.
\q1 Because his power is so very great,
\q2 not a single star ever fails to appear.
\b
\q1 \v 27 You people of Israel should not say
\q2 that Yahweh is not aware of your situation
\q2 or that your God is not treating you fairly.
\q1 \v 28 You must have heard and you must know
\q1 that Yahweh is the eternal God.
\q2 He created the entire earth.
\q1 He never gets tired or weary,
\q2 and no one can fully understand how wise he is.
\q1 \v 29 He gives strength to people who have become exhausted,
\q2 and he makes those who have lost their energy become strong again.
\q1 \v 30 Even young people get tired and become exhausted.
\q2 Strong young men can become so tired that they collapse.
\q1 \v 31 But those who trust in Yahweh will become strong again.
\q2 They will be able to do things without apparent effort,
\q2 the way eagles spread their wings and float in the sky.
\q1 They will run for a long time and not become exhausted.
\q2 They will walk long distances and not get tired.
\c 41
\q1 \v 1 {Yahweh says,} “You people throughout the world,
\q2 be silent in front of me {while I ask you some questions}!
\q1 Then be brave and come forward and speak with me.
\q2 We will meet together and decide which one of us is right.
\q1 \v 2 Who has summoned this king from the east to serve him
\q2 and enabled him to be so victorious?
\q1 Whoever has done this enables him to defeat nations
\q2 and depose their kings.
\q1 His armies use their weapons to destroy opposing armies
\q2 so that they scatter and flee
\q2 as if they were dust or straw that the wind was blowing away.
\q1 \v 3 His armies chase their enemies,
\q2 and the enemies do not harm them.
\q2 They travel so fast that it seems as if their feet are not touching the ground.
\q1 \v 4 Who has made this happen?
\q2 Who has determined what each generation would do throughout history?
\q1 I, Yahweh, am the one who has made this happen!
\q2 I was present when the first thing happened,
\q2 and I will be present when the last things happen.”
\b
\q1 \v 5 People throughout the world see what is happening,
\q2 and they tremble with fear.
\q2 So they gather together.
\q1 \v 6 They encourage one other and say to each other,
\q2 ‘Be strong!’
\q1 \v 7 The worker who made an idol encourages another worker who will cover it with gold.
\q2 That other worker flattens the gold by hitting it with a hammer on an anvil.
\q1 Then he attaches the gold to the idol,
\q2 and the first worker tells him, “That should hold together.”
\q2 Then they carefully nail down the idol so that it will not topple over!
\b
\q1 \v 8 {But Yahweh says,} “You Israelites are my servants.
\q2 I chose you descendants of Jacob to be my people.
\q2 You are descendants of Abraham, who was my friend.
\q1 \v 9 You are the ones I am bringing back from places throughout the world.
\q2 I have told you that I want you to be my servants.
\q1 I have chosen you,
\q2 and I will not reject you.
\q1 \v 10 I will be with you,
\q2 so do not be afraid.
\q1 I am your God,
\q1 so do not be anxious.
\q2 I will make you strong, and I will help you.
\q2 Indeed, I will protect you with the great power by which I always win victories.
\q1 \v 11 Listen! I am going to shame and humiliate
\q2 all the people who were angry with you.
\q2 I am going to destroy completely
\q2 those who opposed you.
\q1 \v 12 Then, even if you search for the people who had a dispute with you,
\q2 you will not find them.
\q1 Those people who attacked you
\q2 will not exist at all anymore.
\q1 \v 13 I will protect you in this way because I, Yahweh, am your God.
\q2 I am strengthening you,
\q1 and I am telling you, ‘Do not be afraid,
\q2 because I will help you.’
\q1 \v 14 Even though you Israelites, who are descendants of Jacob, seem insignificant,
\q2 do not be afraid,
\q1 because I will help you!”
\q1 This is what Yahweh declares.
\q2 He is the holy God of the Israelites, and he rescues them.
\q1 \v 15 {Yahweh says,} “Listen! I am going to enable you to defeat your enemies
\q2 as if they were grain and you were a new threshing sledge
\q2 with many sharp objects on the bottom of it.
\q1 Even if your enemies are large and powerful
\q2 like mountains and hills,
\q1 it will be as if they are grain you are crushing
\q1 to separate out the chaff.
\q1 \v 16 It will be as if you toss them up into the air
\q2 and a strong wind blows them away {as it does chaff}.
\q1 But as for you, you will be very happy about what I, Yahweh have done for you.
\q2 You will praise me, the holy God of you Israelites.
\b
\q1 \v 17 When poor people need water but have no water,
\q2 and their tongues are dry because they are so thirsty,
\q2 I, Yahweh, will come and help them.
\q1 I am the God of the Israelites, and I will never abandon them.
\q1 \v 18 I will make rivers flow for them on hills where no plants are growing.
\q2 I will make fountains flow in the valleys.
\q1 I will make springs flow in dry areas
\q2 so that there are many pools of water in those areas.
\q1 \v 19 I will make trees grow in the desert.
\q2 They will be cedar trees, acacia trees, myrtle trees, olive trees,
\q2 cypress trees, plane trees, and box trees. All those trees will be there.
\q1 \v 20 I will do this so that everyone who sees it will think about it carefully
\q2 and they will all understand
\q2 that I, Yahweh, have done this by my power.
\q1 Yes, they will know that I, the holy God of the Israelites, have created it.”
\q1 \v 21 Yahweh, the King of the descendants of Jacob, says,
\q2 “Present your case! Make your best arguments!
\q1 \v 22 Have your idols come and tell us what will happen!
\q2 Have them tell us what happened in the past
\q1 so we may think about those things
\q2 and learn whether what they predicted really happened.
\q1 Or have them tell us about the future
\q2 {so that we see whether that happens}.
\q1 \v 23 Those idols should tell us what signs
\q2 indicate what is going to happen in the future.
\q2 If they do that, then we will know that they really are gods.
\q1 Tell them to do anything at all
\q2 that will amaze and frighten all of us.
\q1 \v 24 But the gods whom those idols represent do not do anything,
\q2 so it is clear that they really do not exist at all.
\q2 So anyone who decides to worship them is doing something that I detest.
\q1 \v 25 I have empowered a man to come from the north {as a conqueror}.
\q2 He is originally from the east,
\q2 and he is loyal to me.
\q1 He defeats other rulers as easily as someone squashes mud
\q2 or a potter treads on clay.
\q1 \v 26 No idol announced this ahead of time so that we knew it would happen.
\q2 No one can say, ‘An idol predicted that, and it happened!’
\q1 Indeed, no idol ever predicted anything
\q2 No one ever heard one say anything at all.
\q1 \v 27 I was the first one to tell the people who live on Mount Zion,
\q2 ‘Listen, your exiles are coming back!’
\q2 I sent a messenger to bring this good news to Jerusalem.
\q1 \v 28 I looked among the idols, but none of them could say anything
\q2 or give any advice.
\q2 When I asked them questions, none of them answered.
\q1 \v 29 It is clear that the gods whom those idols represent
\q2 do not really exist. They can not do anything.
\q2 Those metal images represent beings that are not real at all.
\c 42
\q1 \v 1 Yahweh says, “Here is my servant. I support him.
\q2 I have chosen him, and he pleases me.
\q1 I have given him my Spirit,
\q2 and he will make the people of the nations do what is right.
\q1 \v 2 He will not yell at people,
\q2 and he will not shout in public places.
\q1 \v 3 He will not harm weak people
\q2 who are barely surviving.
\q1 Instead, he will make sure that judges decide cases in the right way.
\q2 \v 4 He will not become tired or lose courage
\q2 until he ensures that earthly rulers govern fairly.
\q2 People throughout the world will look forward to learning what he decrees.”
\b
\q1 \v 5 Yahweh our God created the sky and put it in place.
\q1 He also formed the earth and everything that is on it.
\q2 He makes the people who live on the earth alive.
\q1 This is what he says:
\q1 \v 6 “I, Yahweh, have chosen you to do what is right.
\q2 I will help you and protect you.
\q2 Through you I will fulfill my agreement with my people,
\q2 and through you I will reveal my salvation to other nations.
\q1 \v 7 You will help people recognize what is true,
\q2 and you will free people who are in bad circumstances
\q2 because others are holding them as their prisoners.
\q1 \v 8 I am Yahweh. That is who I am.
\q2 I will not allow anyone else to receive the honor that only I deserve.
\q2 I will not allow people to praise wooden idols in the way that they should praise only me.
\q1 \v 9 Listen! The events I predicted in the past have happened.
\q2 Now I am telling you what is going to happen in the future.
\q1 I am telling you about these things before they begin to happen.”
\q1 \v 10 People throughout the world should sing a new song to praise Yahweh.
\q1 This includes people who sail on the oceans to catch what is in them
\q2 and those who live anywhere on the land.
\q1 \v 11 People should also sing who live in towns in desolate areas,
\q2 such as the villages where the people of Kedar live.
\q1 It includes the people who live in the city of Sela.
\q2 They should sing joyfully and shout to praise Yahweh
\q2 from their city on top of a rocky cliff.
\q1 \v 12 People throughout the world should honor Yahweh
\q2 and tell about how great he is.
\q1 \v 13 Yahweh will go out to fight against his enemies like a warrior champion.
\q2 He will be eager to fight.
\q1 He will shout his battle cry very loudly,
\q2 and he will overpower his enemies.
\b
\q1 \v 14 {Yahweh says,} “I have been quiet for a long time;
\q2 I have stayed silent and restrained myself from acting.
\q1 {But now it is time for me to act,} as if I were a woman who was about to have a baby,
\q2 and so I will yell, and I will breathe hard and fast.
\q1 \v 15 I will destroy mountains and hills
\q2 and make the plants on them dry up.
\q1 I will cause rivers to become dry land,
\q2 and I will also dry up marshes.
\q1 \v 16 The Israelites have not understood how they should live,
\q2 as if they were blind people who could not see the path they should walk on.
\q1 But I will enable them to live properly,
\q2 as if I were enabling blind people to see what is in front of them.
\q2 It will be as if I am also making smooth the path they should walk on.
\q1 I will certainly do these things for them.
\q2 I will continually help them.
\q1 \v 17 Some people depend on the gods that wooden or metal idols represent.
\q2 They even worship them.
\q1 But those gods will do nothing to help them,
\q2 and this will greatly humiliate them.
\b
\q1 \v 18 You Israelites have been disobeying me as if you were deaf and could not hear my commands.
\q2 Now you must obey me.
\q2 You Israelites have been ignoring my instructions as if you were blind.
\q2 Now must recognize what I want you to do, and you must do it.
\q1 \v 19 The Israelites are my servants,
\q2 but it is as if they are blind.
\q1 I want to send them as my messengers,
\q2 but it is as if they are deaf.
\q1 I made a covenant with them and they are my servants,
\q2 but they do not recognize what I want them to do.
\q1 \v 20 They actually do see many things,
\q2 but they do not understand what those things mean.
\q1 They actually are able to hear,
\q2 but they do not respond as they should to what they hear.”
\q1 \v 21 Yahweh always does what is right.
\q2 So it pleased him to show that his law is great and glorious.
\q1 \v 22 That is why the Babylonians were able to conquer the Israelites
\q2 and take away their valuable possessions.
\q2 That is why they took them captive and held them as their prisoners.
\q1 No one has rescued them from captivity.
\q2 No one has demanded that the Babylonians set these captives free.
\q1 \v 23 The Israelites should listen carefully to what I am about to say.
\q2 They should consider it in order to understand what they should do in the future.
\q1 \v 24 They should recognize who allowed enemies
\q2 to conquer the Israelites, the descendants of Jacob, and make them prisoners.
\q1 It was Yahweh who did this,
\q2 because we Israelites sinned against him.
\q1 We would not live as he wanted us to live,
\q2 and we did not obey his laws.
\q1 \v 25 As a result, he became extremely angry with us,
\q2 and he allowed powerful enemies to defeat us in war.
\q1 It was as if those enemies burned up the Israelites entirely.
\q2 But they did not consider what happened
\q2 or understand why it happened.
\c 43
\q1 \v 1 Yahweh is the one who created the Israelites, the descendants of Jacob.
\q2 This is what he now says to them:
\q1 “I am rescuing you, so do not be afraid.
\q2 I am calling for you Israelites by name. You belong to me.
\q1 \v 2 When you experience difficult situations,
\q2 as if you were crossing a deep river,
\q2 I will help you, so it will be as if you do not drown.
\q1 When you experience dangerous situations,
\q2 as if you were in an area that was burning,
\q2 I will protect you, so it will be as if that fire does not burn you.
\q1 \v 3 I will do this because I am Yahweh your God.
\q2 I am the holy God of you Israelites. I will save you.
\q1 I will allow your captors to have the people of Egypt, Cush, and Seba
\q2 instead of you so that you can go free.
\q1 \v 4 You are very valuable to me.
\q2 I honor you, and I love you personally.
\q1 So I will allow your captors to have the people of other nations
\q2 instead of you so that you may live.
\q1 \v 5 I am helping you, so do not be afraid.
\q2 I will gather your descendants from throughout the world
\q2 and bring them home.
\q1 \v 6 I will command the people of nations throughout the world,
\q2 ‘Release my people and do not keep any of them as prisoners!
\q2 Allow all of them to return from distant places on the earth.
\q1 \v 7 Send back all of those people! They belong to me.
\q2 I created them, and they show that I am glorious.’
\b
\q1 \v 8-9 Suppose it were possible to gather together
\q2 the people of all nations for a trial.
\q1 Suppose it were possible to bring in the Israelites as witnesses,
\q2 even though they do not understand as they should what Yahweh is doing,
\q2 and they are not responding to it as they should.
\q1 One could then ask the people of the nations,
\q2 “Did any of your gods predict what is happening now?
\q2 Did any of them predict anything that happened in the past?
\q1 If you claim that they did, bring in witnesses to prove this.
\q2 Let people listen to them and decide whether your claim is true.”
\q1 \v 10 {No one would testify on behalf of a foreign god, but} Yahweh would declare,
\q2 “You Israelites are my witnesses
\q2 {that I have said what was going to happen and have made it happen}.
\q1 I chose you to be my servants.
\q1 I wanted you to know me and trust me
\q2 and understand that I am the only true God.
\q1 There was no other god before me,
\q2 and there will be no other god after me.
\q1 \v 11 I, Yahweh, am the only true God.
\q2 I am the only one who can save people.
\q1 \v 12 I announced {that I was going to save you}, and I did save you.
\q2 I told you that myself.
\q2 No foreign god told you that!
\q1 So you can testify for me,” Yahweh would declare,
\q2 “that I am God.
\q1 \v 13 I have always existed,
\q2 and no one can rescue people from my power.
\q2 When I act, no one can undo what I have done.”
\b
\q1 \v 14 Yahweh is the holy God of you Israelites.
\q2 He is the one who rescues you.
\q2 He says this to you:
\q1 “For your sake, I will have an army attack Babylon.
\q1 It will defeat the Babylonians, and they will run away.
\q2 They will flee in the ships of which they are so proud.
\q1 \v 15 I am Yahweh, your holy God.
\q2 I created you Israelites, and I am your true king.”
\q1 \v 16-17 Yahweh is the one who made a dry path
\q2 through the Sea of Reeds {so that the Israelites could cross it}.
\q1 He then got the Egyptian army with its great soldiers
\q2 and chariots and horses {to follow them}.
\q1 But {the waters flowed back over them and} they all drowned and died.
\q2 They were like a lamp whose light went out.
\q1 This is what Yahweh now says to you Israelites:
\q1 \v 18 “You do not need to remember these things
\q2 that happened so long ago.
\q1 \v 19 I am about to do something new {that is just as great}.
\q2 I have already begun to do it. You ought to be able to recognize it.
\q1 I am actually going to make a path through the desert,
\q2 and I will make water flow there.
\q1 \v 20 Even wild animals such as jackals and ostriches will thank me
\q2 for making this water flow in the desert.
\q1 I will do that so that my people, whom I have chosen,
\q2 will have water to drink {when they walk on that path}.
\q1 \v 21 I created those people so that they would belong to me.
\q2 They will tell why I deserve to have people praise me.
\b
\q1 \v 22 However, you descendants of Jacob have not prayed to me.
\q2 It appears that you people of Israel have become tired of worshiping me.
\q1 \v 23-24 You have not brought me your sheep as burnt offerings,
\q2 and you have not offered sacrifices to honor me.
\q1 You have not spent money to buy aromatic cane for me,
\q2 and you have not made me content with the fat from your sacrifices.
\q1 I have not burdened you by requiring grain offerings,
\q2 and I have not tired you out by demanding frankincense.
\q1 Instead, you have burdened me by the sins you have committed,
\q2 and you have wearied me by the wrong things you have done.
\b
\q1 \v 25 But for my part, I am the one who forgives your sins
\q2 for my own sake.
\q2 I will never think about your sins again.
\q1 \v 26 If you have any evidence that you did not sin against me,
\q2 I am willing to consider it.
\q1 Go ahead and try to state your case
\q2 if you think you can prove that you did not sin.
\q1 \v 27 But you people have been sinning against me from the time of your first ancestor.
\q2 Your priests and prophets have continually sinned.
\q1 \v 28 That is why I allowed enemies to destroy my temple
\q2 and the priests no longer had a special place to do their work.
\q2 That is why I allowed enemies to destroy the descendants of Jacob completely.
\q2 Now other nations are mocking the people of Israel.”
\c 44
\q1 \v 1 “I have chosen you Israelites, who are descendants of Jacob,
\q2 to be my servants. So now listen to me.”
\q1 \v 2 Yahweh is the one who originally made you. He helps you.
\q2 This is what he says.
\q1 “You descendants of Jacob who serve me, do not be afraid.
\q2 I call you Jeshurun because you are special to me.
\q1 \v 3 {Do not be afraid,} because I will make streams of water flow
\q2 onto the land that is now dry.
\q1 I will give my Spirit to your descendants,
\q2 and I will bless them.
\q1 \v 4 They will grow up as grass does in a meadow
\q2 and as willow trees do along a riverbank.
\q1 \v 5 They will proudly say that they are descendants of Jacob
\q2 and that they belong to me, Yahweh.
\q1 They will write on their hands that they belong to me, Yahweh,
\q2 and when they say their names, they will add that they are Israelites.”
\b
\q1 \v 6 Yahweh is the true king of the Israelites,
\q2 and he is rescuing them.
\q1 Yahweh is the commander of the heavenly armies.
\q2 This is what he says.
\q1 “I have always existed, and I will always exist.
\q2 I am the only God.
\q1 \v 7 No one is like me!
\q1 If anyone thinks he is, he should say so and give me evidence.
\q2 He should say what has happened since I first created my people of Israel long ago,
\q1 and he should also say what is just about to happen
\q2 and what will happen later in the future.
\q1 \v 8 You Israelites should not be afraid at all.
\q2 Long ago I certainly told you what would happen.
\q2 You can confirm that I did.
\q1 I am the only God.
\q2 Indeed, there is none other whom you can depend on.
\q2 I know that for certain!”
\b
\q1 \v 9 The people who make idols are all wasting their time.
\q2 The idols that they value can not help them at all.
\q1 The people who say that they are real gods do not recognize what is true.
\q2 This shames them.
\q1 \v 10 An idol does not help anyone,
\q2 so it does not make sense for someone to make one and say that it is a god!
\q1 \v 11 Listen! Everyone who joins in making idols will feel shame.
\q1 The craftsmen who make them are only human beings.
\q1 Suppose they all had to gather together and stand before Yahweh
\q2 {to account for why they made idols}.
\q2 They would all be very afraid and feel great shame.
\b
\q1 \v 12 {To make an idol from metal,}
\q2 a metalworker uses a tool to hold metal over hot coals to heat it.
\q1 Then he pounds the hot metal with a hammer to shape it.
\q2 This is very hard work.
\q1 So it uses up the energy that the man gets from eating.
\q2 {It is hot work, so} he becomes weary if he does not drink water.
\q1 \v 13 {To make an idol from wood,}
\q2 a woodworker stretches out a line as a guide.
\q2 Then he uses red material to mark the outline of an idol on the wood.
\q1 He uses a compass to mark where the round parts should be.
\q2 Then he uses chisels to carve the wood into the shape of a human being.
\q1 He makes it look like a beautiful human figure.
\q2 Then someone puts it in a shrine.
\q1 \v 14 To get wood to make idols, woodworkers get trees from the forest.
\q2 They may cut down cedar, cypress, or oak trees.
\q1 They may even plant pine trees
\q2 and wait for them to grow as the rain waters them.
\q1 \v 15 People take some of the wood from those trees
\q2 to make fires to warm their homes.
\q2 They also burn the wood so they can bake bread.
\q1 But from that same wood, people also make idols that represent gods,
\q2 and then people bow down to worship those idols!
\q1 \v 16 Yes, people burn part of that wood
\q2 to make fires on which they roast meat.
\q2 They eat the meat until they have had enough.
\q2 People also make fires to warm their homes.
\q2 A person says, “Ah! I can feel the heat from the fire. It is warming me up.”
\q1 \v 17 But people take the rest of the wood from those trees
\q2 and carve it into the shape of an idol that represents a god.
\q1 They bow down to it to worship it.
\q2 People also pray to it, saying,
\q2 “You are my god, so rescue me!”
\q1 \v 18 People who make and worship idols do not recognize what is true.
\q2 It is as if someone has smeared clay over their eyes to keep them from seeing.
\q2 It is as if someone is keeping them from thinking clearly with their minds.
\q1 \v 19 None of them thinks carefully about this.
\q1 None of them knows or understands enough to say to himself,
\q2 “I burned half of this wood in a fire
\q2 and baked bread over its coals.
\q2 I roasted meat and ate it.
\q1 So I should not take what remains and make something from it that Yahweh detests!
\q2 I should not bow down to a block of wood!”
\q1 \v 20 A person like that depends on something that cannot help him.
\q2 People have deceived him into thinking that idols are real gods,
\q2 and so he worships them instead of Yahweh.
\q1 He does not get himself out of that situation by realizing,
\q2 “What I am doing is certainly wrong!”
\b
\q1 \v 21 “You Israelites who are descendants of Jacob are my servants,
\q2 so remember these things.
\q1 I created you, and you are my servants.
\q2 I will never forget you Israelites.
\q1 \v 22 I have completely forgiven your sins.
\q2 They are gone, just as clouds disappear from the sky.
\q1 I have rescued you,
\q2 so worship me as your God again.”
\b
\q1 \v 23 Yahweh is rescuing the descendants of Jacob.
\q2 What he does for the Israelites will show how great he is.
\q1 So everything throughout the creation
\q2 should sing and shout because Yahweh is doing this.
\q1 {If they could,} the mountains and all the trees in the forests
\q2 should shout joyfully.
\b
\q1 \v 24 Yahweh is the one who originally made you Israelites.
\q2 He is rescuing you.
\q2 This is what he says.
\q1 “I am Yahweh. I made everything.
\q1 I alone created the sky,
\q2 and I formed the earth without help from anyone.
\q1 \v 25 Some people falsely claim that certain signs predict the future,
\q2 but I show that this is not true.
\q2 I prove that what fortunetellers say is false.
\q1 Some people claim to be wise,
\q2 but I show that they are not.
\q2 I show that what they think they know is foolish.
\q1 \v 26 However, when I send prophets as my messengers
\q2 to say that something is going to happen,
\q2 I always make it happen.
\q1 I say about Jerusalem, ‘People will live there again.’
\q2 I say about the cities of Judah, ‘People will rebuild them.
\q2 They will restore their ruins.’
\q1 \v 27 I can tell the ocean that I am going to dry it up
\q2 so that no current flows through it{, and this will happen}.
\q1 \v 28 I say about King Cyrus, ‘He is the leader I have chosen,
\q2 and he will do everything I want him to do.’
\q1 He will decree that people should rebuild Jerusalem
\q2 and that they should lay the foundation of the temple again
\q2 {so that workers can rebuild it}.
\c 45
\q1 \v 1 Yahweh has chosen Cyrus, and he has given him great power.
\q2 He is enabling him to defeat other kings when they fight against him
\q2 and to conquer the nations that they rule.
\q1 Yahweh is enabling Cyrus to conquer cities that have strong walls around them.
\q2 No one has been able to stop Cyrus from doing that.
\q1 Yahweh says this to him:
\q1 \v 2 “I myself will go ahead of you
\q2 and prepare the way for you.
\q1 Even if city walls have bronze gates and iron bars,
\q2 I will enable you to cut through those bars and smash those gates.
\q1 \v 3 I will enable you to take valuable possessions
\q2 that people are hiding in secret places.
\q1 Then you will know that I am Yahweh,
\q2 the God of Israel,
\q2 and that I have called you personally {to serve me}.
\q1 \v 4 I have chosen the Israelites, the descendants of Jacob, to be my servants.
\q2 For their sake, I have called you personally {to serve me}.
\q1 Even though you do not know me,
\q2 I have given you an honorable title.
\q1 \v 5 I am Yahweh, and no one else is like me.
\q2 I am the only true God.
\q1 Even though you do not know me,
\q2 I will enable you to win battles.
\q1 \v 6 Then people throughout the world will know
\q2 that I am the only true God.
\q2 I am Yahweh, and no one else is like me.
\q1 \v 7 I make light, and I make darkness.
\q2 I cause things to go well, and I cause things to go badly.
\q2 I, Yahweh, do all these things.”
\b
\q1 \v 8 Yahweh also says, “After rain pours down from the sky above,
\q2 the ground soaks up the water
\q2 and plants grow and produce fruit.
\q1 Just as surely, I will act to save my people and make things right for them again.
\q2 I, Yahweh, will make this happen.”
\b
\q1 \v 9 How terrible it will be for anyone who argues with the God who made him!
\q2 A person is like a piece of broken pottery among many pieces on the ground.
\q1 The clay should never say to the potter, “You should be making something else!”
\q2 It should not say, “You do not work very skillfully at all!”
\q1 \v 10 {When people question what Yahweh is doing, that is like}
\q2 someone saying to {the man who would be} his father,
\q2 “You should not have me as a child!”
\q1 That is like someone saying to the woman {who would be his mother},
\q2 “You should not give birth to me!”
\q1 How terrible it will be for people who do that!
\b
\q1 \v 11 Yahweh is the holy God of the Israelites. He made them.
\q2 This is what he says.
\q2 “No one should question me about what I am going to do
\q2 for {the Israelites, who are like} my children!
\q2 No one should tell me what to do for the people I have made!
\q1 \v 12 I am the one who made the earth
\q2 and created people to live on it.
\q1 I created the sky by my own power,
\q2 and I put all the stars in their places.
\q1 \v 13 I have empowered Cyrus to do what is right,
\q2 and I will make it possible for him to do that.
\q1 He will command people to rebuild my city of Jerusalem,
\q2 and he will return my people, whose enemies them took away as captives.
\q1 He will do this without anyone paying or bribing him.”
\q2 This is what Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies, says.
\p
\v 14 Yahweh also says this.
\q1 “You Israelites will obtain the goods that the Egyptians work to produce
\q2 and the products that the people of Cush and the tall Sabean people sell.
\q2 Those things will be yours.
\q2 You will take those people captive, and they will become your slaves.
\q1 They will bow down to you and plead with you,
\q2 ‘You God is the only true God,
\q2 and he is surely helping you, {so let us be part of your community}.’”
\b
\q1 \v 15 Surely you, the God of the Israelites, the one who saves them,
\q2 are a God who works in ways that people can not see.
\q1 \v 16 All those who make idols will feel shame and disgrace.
\q2 They will walk away in disgrace together.
\q1 \v 17 Yahweh will save the Israelites,
\q2 and they will always be safe.
\q1 The Israelites will not feel shame or disgrace
\q2 at any time in the future.
\b
\q1 \v 18 Yahweh is God.
\q1 He is the one who created the sky
\q2 and formed the earth and set it in place.
\q1 He did not create the earth to remain empty.
\q2 He formed it so that people would live on it.
\q1 This is what he says:
\q2 “I am Yahweh, and I am the only true God.
\q1 \v 19 I have spoken openly,
\q2 in a way that has not been difficult to discover.
\q1 I did not tell the descendants of Jacob
\q2 that they would have to search for me
\q2 in a place where nothing seemed to be.
\q2 I, Yahweh, clearly state what is right.
\b
\q1 \v 20 Some of the survivors from the nations {that the Babylonians destroyed}
\q2 carry around their wooden idols
\q1 and pray to gods that can not save anyone.
\q2 They are very foolish.
\q1 Suppose they had to gather
\q2 and come and appear together in court before Yahweh.
\q1 \v 21 Suppose the court case was about who announced long ago what would happen.
\q2 Suppose those survivors could discuss together what to say,
\q2 and suppose they tried to make a case and present evidence for it.
\q1 It would become clear that I, Yahweh, was the one who did that.
\q2 I am the only true God.
\q1 I am a God who does what is right and saves people.
\q2 None of the foreign gods are real.
\b
\q1 \v 22 People from throughout the world.
\q2 come to me, and I will save you.
\q2 Come to me because I am the only true God.
\q1 \v 23 I have sworn a solemn oath in my own name.
\q2 What I have said is true,
\q2 and I will never take it back:
\q1 “All people will bow down to me {as their ruler},
\q2 and they will solemnly promise to be loyal to me.
\q1 \v 24 People will say about me,
\q2 ‘Only Yahweh does what is right.
\q2 He is the one who gives us strength.’”
\b
\q1 Some people have been hostile to Yahweh.
\q2 This will now humiliate them,
\q2 and they will have to come and submit to him.
\q1 \v 25 Yahweh will show that the Israelite people are right,
\q2 and they will praise him for what he has done.
\c 46
\q1 \v 1 Statues that represent Bel and Nebo
\q2 are lying face down.
\q1 People used to carry those statues in processions,
\q2 but now they have loaded them onto the backs of pack animals.
\q2 They are so heavy that it exhausts the animals to carry them.
\q1 \v 2 All those statues are lying face down.
\q2 The gods that they represent can not keep their enemies from capturing them.
\q2 Those enemies take them away as prizes.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Yahweh says, “Listen to me, you surviving Israelites,
\q2 who are descendants of Jacob.
\q1 I have taken care of you ever since you were born.
\q1 \v 4 I will continue to be the one who takes care of you
\q2 even when you become old.
\q1 I am the one who made you,
\q2 and I will rescue you and take very good care of you.
\b
\q1 \v 5 There is no god to whom you can compare me,
\q2 because none of them are like me or equal to me.
\q1 \v 6 When people want to have an idol that represents a god,
\q2 they pay a metalworker to make one.
\q1 They pour out so much gold from their bags for him to use
\q2 that it seems as if they do not consider gold to be valuable.
\q2 They also weigh out {much} silver.
\q2 After he makes it, they bow down and worship it.
\q1 \v 7 They pick it up and carry it on their shoulders
\q2 and put it in a shrine. It stays there and never moves.
\q1 Then people pray to it, but it does not do what they ask.
\q2 It does not save anyone who is in trouble.
\b
\q1 \v 8 You Israelites have been worshiping other gods instead of me.
\q2 You have forgotten something that you need to remind yourselves about.
\q2 Then you will be able to do what you should do.
\q1 \v 9 Remember the things that I did long ago.
\q2 Only I am God; there is no other.
\q2 Yes, I am God, and no one is like me.
\q1 \v 10 From the very beginning, I say what will happen in the end.
\q2 I announce what is going to happen before it happens.
\q1 I say, ‘What I plan will certainly happen;
\q2 I will do everything I want to do.’
\q1 \v 11 I have summoned a man to come from the east
\q2 who will swoop down on his enemies like a hawk.
\q2 I am bringing him from a distant land to carry out my plan.
\q1 I have said what will happen, and I will make it happen.
\q2 I have planned it, and I will do it.
\q1 \v 12 Listen to me, you stubborn people
\q2 who are doing things that are very wrong!
\q1 \v 13 I am going to save you and make things right.
\q2 I will do this soon, without delaying.
\q1 I will rescue the people of Jerusalem,
\q2 and I will make the Israelites honorable again.”
\c 47
\q1 \v 1 Yahweh says, “You Babylonian people will mourn greatly
\q2 because you will no longer rule other countries.
\q1 You will be like a queen who once sat on a throne
\q2 but who is now sitting on the ground.
\q1 It will be as if people once said that she was very dainty
\q2 but then stopped saying that about her.
\q1 \v 2 It will be as if that former queen had to take millstones
\q2 and grind flour like a slave.
\q2 It will be as if she had to remove her veil, take off her robes,
\q2 and lift up her skirts so that she could wade through rivers.
\q1 \v 3 Your enemies will strip you Babylonians naked {to lead you away as captives},
\q2 and you will feel great shame because they have exposed your bodies.
\q1 I will punish you,
\q2 and I will not spare anyone.”
\b
\q1 \v 4 Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 is the name of the one who is rescuing us.
\q2 He is the holy God of us Israelites.
\b
\q1 \v 5 Yahweh says, “Sit down and be silent, you Babylonians,
\q2 and then go into captivity.
\q1 No one will ever again call you
\q2 “The Kingdom that Rules Other Kingdoms.”
\q1 \v 6 I was angry with my people,
\q2 and I treated them as if they were not special.
\q1 I allowed you to capture them.
\q1 But you did not treat them with any kindness.
\q2 You even treated old people very harshly.
\q1 \v 7 You said, ‘I will always rule other kingdoms.’
\q2 But you did not think carefully about your situation.
\q2 You did not consider what would finally happen to you.
\b
\q1 \v 8 So now listen to this, you Babylonians who love luxury
\q2 and think that you are safe.
\q1 You say to yourself that you are like God
\q2 and that no one is equal to you.
\q1 You say that your gods will always protect you
\q2 and that you will never lose your people.
\q1 \v 9 But both of those things will happen to you very suddenly.
\q2 Your gods will fail to protect you,
\q2 and you will lose your people.
\q1 This will happen to you completely,
\q2 even though you practice many kinds of magic
\q2 and perform spells that you think are very powerful.
\q1 \v 10 You rely on doing things that are wrong,
\q2 and you believe that no one is able to judge and punish you for doing them.
\q1 You believe that you know how to do things magically.
\q2 But because you are wrong about this,
\q1 you say to yourself,
\q2 ‘I am like God, and no one is equal to me.’
\q1 \v 11 But terrible things will happen to you,
\q2 and you will not recognize it when they begin to happen.
\q1 Enemies will come to destroy you,
\q2 and you will not be able to appease them.
\q1 They will destroy you suddenly,
\q2 before you even realize what they are going to do.
\b
\q1 \v 12 You can go ahead and keep performing spells
\q2 and practicing many kinds of magic.
\q2 You have been working hard at that for a long time.
\q1 But this will not help you.
\q2 You will not be able to scare your enemies away.
\q1 \v 13 You have many people who advise you.
\q2 They group the stars into constellations {that they believe influence people}.
\q1 They consider what stars are in the sky at a certain time,
\q2 and they tell you what you should do during that time.
\q1 But those people have only worn you out.
\q2 They will not be able to save you from what is going to happen to you now.
\q1 \v 14 Listen! Your enemies will destroy those advisors
\q2 the way fire burns up straw.
\q1 They will not be able to save themselves
\q2 when those enemies destroy them as if they were burning them up.
\q1 This will not be like a home fire that produces coals that warm people
\q2 and lights the area in front of it.
\q1 \v 15 For a long time, you have worked hard to rely on magicians.
\q2 But they will not help you at all.
\q1 It will be as if they all walk away from you.
\q2 None of them will be able to save you.”
\c 48
\q1 \v 1 Yahweh says, “Listen to this, you descendants of Jacob,
\q2 whom people call Israelites,
\q2 who come from the tribe of Judah.
\q1 You appeal to me, Yahweh, to guarantee your oaths,
\q2 and you pray to me as the God of Israel,
\q2 but you do not do that sincerely or honestly.
\q1 \v 2 You {merely} identify yourselves as people from the holy city of Jerusalem,
\q2 and you claim to rely on me, the God of Israel.
\q2 I am Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Long ago I announced the things that would happen.
\q2 I proclaimed them, and I made sure everyone heard them.
\q1 Then, when it was the right time, I acted, and they happened.
\q1 \v 4 I did this because I knew that you are stubborn.
\q2 You refuse to change what you think or what you do.
\q1 \v 5 That is why I told you those things long ago.
\q2 I announced them to you before they happened.
\q1 That way you could not say that your idol did them
\q2 or that your wooden or metal image commanded them to happen.
\b
\q1 \v 6 You have heard what I predicted.
\q2 Consider all that has happened.
\q1 You should certainly acknowledge that what I announced did happen!
\q1 Now I am telling you about new things that will happen.
\q2 These are things that I have kept secret, so you do not know about them.
\q1 \v 7 I am making them happen now. They did not begin long ago,
\q2 You have not heard about them before today,
\q2 so you can not say, ‘Listen, I already knew about those things.’
\b
\q1 \v 8 No, you certainly did not hear about them, and you did not know about them.
\q2 You were not paying attention in the past.
\q1 I did not tell you, because I knew that you would certainly be disloyal to me.
\q2 As soon as you were born, it was clear that you would rebel.
\b
\q1 \v 9 However, for the sake of my own reputation, I will not become angry with you.
\q2 People will praise me because I restrained myself
\q2 and did not destroy you completely.
\q1 \v 10 Listen! I have purified you, but not with actual fire,
\q2 such as people use to purify silver.
\q1 Instead, I have allowed your enemies to afflict you,
\q2 and this has shown your true character,
\q2 just as melting metal in a furnace shows whether it is pure.
\q1 \v 11 It is certainly for my own sake that I will act.
\q2 I will not allow people to think that I am like the gods that other nations worship!
\q2 I will not let anyone else receive the honor that only I deserve.”
\b
\q1 \v 12 “Listen to me, you Israelites, who are descendants of Jacob
\q2 and whom I have chosen as my people.
\q1 I am the only true God.
\q2 I have always existed, and I always will exist.
\q1 \v 13 Truly, by my great power, I created the earth and the sky.
\q2 They still obey my commands.
\b
\q1 \v 14 All of you, gather together and listen!
\q2 None of your idols ever announced these things.
\q1 I, Yahweh, have chosen a man to do what I want against the Babylonians.
\q2 Through him, I will use my power against them.
\q1 \v 15 I myself have spoken, and I have called him personally.
\q2 I have brought him here {to attack the Babylonians},
\q2 and he will succeed in what he is doing.
\p
\v 16 Come close to me and listen to this:
\q1 From the very beginning, I have spoken openly.
\q2 From the time these things began to happen, I have been making them happen.”
\q1 Now Yahweh, the Lord, has given me his Spirit and sent me {to you Israelites}.
\b
\q1 \v 17 Yahweh is the holy God of you Israelites.
\q2 He is rescuing you. This is what he says:
\q1 “I am Yahweh your God.
\q2 I am telling you what will help you.
\q2 I am guiding you to do what you should do.
\q1 \v 18 I wish that you had obeyed my commands!
\q2 If you had done that, things would have gone well for you continually.
\q2 You would have kept doing what is right,
\q2 just as the waves of the sea keep breaking on the shore.
\q1 \v 19 Your descendants would have been very numerous,
\q2 like the grains of sand on the seashore.
\q1 I would not have destroyed them,
\q2 and then their family line would have continued in my presence.”
\b
\q1 \v 20 Leave Babylon!
\q2 Yes, flee that land that the Chaldeans rule!
\q1 Celebrate as you announce this so that people know about it.
\q2 Proclaim the news to people throughout the world.
\q1 Say, “Yahweh has rescued his servants, the descendants of Jacob.”
\q1 \v 21 Yahweh led them through the desert, but they did not become thirsty.
\q1 He split open a rock
\q2 and caused water to gush out for them to drink.
\b
\q1 \v 22 But Yahweh says, “Things will never go well for wicked people.”
\c 49
\q1 \v 1 People throughout the world, listen carefully to what I say!
\q2 Yahweh appointed me {to be a prophet} before I was born.
\q1 \v 2 He equipped me to say things that would convict people,
\q2 just as a sharp sword cuts through things.
\q2 He kept me safe by his power {while he was doing that}.
\q1 He prepared me to deliver his message,
\q2 just as an arrow whose shaft someone has polished flies straight and fast.
\q1 He has kept me ready for just the right time
\q2 like an arrow in a quiver.
\q1 \v 3 He said to me, “You are my servant.
\q2 You represent the people of Israel.
\q2 Through you, I will show how glorious I am.”
\q1 \v 4 But for my part, I said, “I have worked hard, but I have not accomplished anything.
\q2 Indeed, I have used up my strength, but I have not achieved anything.
\q1 Nevertheless, Yahweh will do what is right for me.
\q2 My God will give me what I deserve.”
\b
\q1 \v 5 Even as I was forming inside my mother’s body,
\q2 Yahweh was preparing me to serve him
\q2 by leading the people of Israel to worship him again.
\q1 Yahweh is proud of me for doing that.
\q2 He is my God, and he makes me strong.
\q2 This is what he now says to me.
\q1 \v 6 He says, “I have much more for you to do as my servant
\q2 beyond restoring the surviving Israelites, the descendants of Jacob.
\q1 I will also make you someone who brings the truth about me to other nations.
\q2 Then I will save people throughout the world.”
\b
\q1 \v 7 Yahweh is rescuing the Israelites.
\q2 He is their holy God.
\q1 The Israelites do not like his prophet.
\q2 They do not care whether he lives or dies.
\q2 Foreign rulers have made him their subject.
\q1 But this is what Yahweh says to his prophet:
\q1 “When kings see you, they will stand up {to honor you}.
\q2 When princes see you, they will bow down to you.
\q1 They will do this because I, Yahweh, will keep my promises to you.
\q2 I am the holy God of the Israelites, and I have chosen you.”
\p
\v 8 Yahweh also says this:
\q1 “At the right time, I will act on your behalf.
\q2 Yes, when it is time for me to save you, I will help you.
\q1 I will keep you alive,
\q2 and through you I will fulfill my agreement with my people.
\q2 I will restore the land
\q1 and give my people back the territory that others have made desolate.
\q1 \v 9 You will say to those whom your enemies have captured, ‘You are free to leave!’
\q2 You will say to those who are in dark prisons, ‘Come out into the light!’
\q1 {As they return home,} they will find what they need along the roads,
\q2 They will even find food on bare hilltops.
\q1 \v 10 They will not be hungry or thirsty,
\q2 and the burning heat of the sun will not harm them.
\q1 That is because I, Yahweh, will treat them kindly
\q2 and lead them to springs of water.
\q1 \v 11 To make a road for them,
\q2 I will flatten the mountains and fill in the valleys.
\q1 \v 12 People will see exiles returning from distant countries.
\q2 They will see them coming from the north and from the west
\q2 and from the land of Sinim.”
\b
\q1 \v 13 Everything throughout the creation
\q2 should shout to celebrate this!
\q2 {If they could,} the mountains should sing joyfully.
\q1 They should do this because Yahweh will treat his people kindly.
\q2 He will be merciful to those who are suffering.
\b
\q1 \v 14 However, the people of Jerusalem say,
\q1 “Yahweh has abandoned us;
\q2 yes, he has forgotten about us.”
\b
\q1 \v 15 {Yahweh replies,} “A mother would not forget the infant she was nursing.
\q2 She would not be harsh to a child to whom she gave birth.
\q1 But even if a mother could do that,
\q2 for my part, I would never forget you!
\q1 \v 16 Listen! I have written the name of your city on my palms
\q2 {so that I will always remember you}.
\q2 I am always thinking about you people of that city.
\q1 \v 17 Those who tore your city down and ruined it are leaving,
\q2 and your fellow citizens are hurrying back to you.
\q1 \v 18 You will look around and see all the people
\q2 who have come together and who are returning to you.
\q1 I, Yahweh, swear by myself
\q2 that those people will make your city a wonderful place, as if they are jewels that your city is wearing.
\q1 You will keep them with you, just as a bride securely ties on her jewels.
\q1 \v 19 Your enemies destroyed your land,
\q2 and now there are many areas in it where no one lives.
\q2 But soon those enemies will be gone,
\q2 and there will not be enough room in the land
\q2 for everyone who wants to live there.
\q1 \v 20 Soon you will hear Israelites who were born in exile and who have returned say,
\q2 ‘This place is not big enough for all of us;
\q2 give us more land on which we can live!’
\q1 \v 21 Then you will think to yourselves,
\q2 ‘How did we get all these people?
\q1 Enemies took away most of our people as prisoners.
\q2 Our city was like a woman whose children had died and who was not having more children.
\q2 So we do not know who raised these children!
\q1 Indeed, our city was like a woman whose husband had died.
\q2 So we do not know where they came from!’”
\p
\v 22 This is what Yahweh, the Lord, says:
\q1 “Listen! I will signal to the people of other nations
\q2 what I want them to do.
\q1 Then they will bring your exiled people back to you
\q2 the way people carry children on their shoulders or have them sit on their laps as they ride an animal.
\q1 \v 23 Kings will take care of your children as if they were their fathers.
\q2 Their princesses will nurse them as if they were their mothers.
\q1 They will bow down low to the ground in front of you
\q2 to show you great honor.
\q1 Then you will know that I, Yahweh, am the only true God.
\q2 No one will feel shame because he trusted in me.”
\b
\q1 \v 24 People do not take valuable possessions away
\q2 from a mighty soldier who has captured them.
\q1 People do not take captives away
\q2 from a tyrant who has captured them.
\p
\v 25 But this is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “I will free even captives that a mighty soldier is holding.
\q2 I will take back what a tyrant has seized.
\q1 I myself will fight against those who are fighting against you,
\q2 and I myself will rescue your people.
\q1 \v 26 I will cause your enemies to destroy one another.
\q2 They will kill each other with great violence.
\q1 Then people throughout the world will know
\q2 that I, Yahweh, the Mighty God of the descendants of Jacob,
\q2 am the one who is saving and rescuing you.”
\c 50
\p
\v 1 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “I did not fail to keep my covenant with you Israelites,
\q2 as if you were children whose mother I had divorced
\q2 or whom I gave to someone to pay a debt.
\q1 No, I allowed your enemies to take you into exile away from your land
\q2 because of the many wrong things you did.
\q1 \v 2 I came to rescue you, but no one met with me to represent you.
\q2 I called to you, but no one responded.
\q1 I certainly have the power to rescue you.
\q2 Consider this: I am able to command the sea to become dry!
\q1 I can also cause rivers to become deserts,
\q2 so that the fish in them die from thirst and rot in the dry air.
\q1 \v 3 I can make the sky become dark
\q2 by covering it with clouds,
\q2 as if it were wearing thick cloth.”
\b
\q1 \v 4 Yahweh, the Lord, has taught me{, his prophet,} how to speak for him,
\q2 so that I know what to say to encourage people who are weary.
\q1 Every single morning he wakes me up,
\q2 and he enables me to understand what he is teaching me.
\q1 \v 5 Yahweh, the Lord, has enabled me to understand
\q2 what he wants me to do,
\q2 and I have not disobeyed him
\q2 and done something else.
\q1 \v 6 I endured it when people beat me on my back
\q2 and pulled out the hair of my beard.
\q1 I did not turn my face away from them
\q2 when they spat on me to shame me.
\q1 \v 7 Because Yahweh, the Lord, helps me,
\q2 this does not disgrace me.
\q1 So I have firmly resolved to endure these difficulties,
\q2 knowing that they will not humiliate me.
\q1 \v 8 Anyone who wants to accuse me of doing wrong
\q2 may go to trial with me.
\q2 Yahweh is right here to defend me.
\q1 Anyone who thinks he can win a case against me
\q2 may come and confront me.
\q1 \v 9 Listen! Yahweh, the Lord, will defend me himself,
\q2 so no one will declare that I am guilty.
\q1 Listen! The people who accuse me will all go away,
\q2 just like clothing that has worn out or that moths have eaten.
\b
\q1 \v 10 If you respect Yahweh
\q2 and do what his servant tells you to do,
\q1 even if you do not understand why you are in a difficult situation,
\q2 depend on your God.
\q2 You can trust Yahweh because of who he is.
\q1 \v 11 But some people decide for themselves what they should do.
\q2 If they do that instead of what Yahweh wants,
\q1 Yahweh will punish them
\q2 by making them die painfully.
\c 51
\q1 \v 1 {Yahweh says,} “You people who want to do what is right
\q2 and want to obey me, listen to me!
\q1 Consider the man from whom you came,
\q2 just as people cut a stone block from a cliff
\q2 or dig it out of a quarry.
\q1 \v 2 Think about your ancestor Abraham
\q2 and {his wife} Sarah, from whom all of you descend.
\q2 When I first spoke to Abraham,
\q1 he had no children.
\q2 But I blessed him and enabled him to have many descendants.”
\q1 \v 3 Yahweh will encourage the people of {the city of Jerusalem on Mount} Zion.
\q2 He will bring people back to the places {in Judah} where no one now lives.
\q1 He will make her dry land as fertile as the Garden of Eden,
\q2 which Yahweh himself planted.
\q1 The people of Judah will celebrate happily.
\q2 They will thank Yahweh and sing praises to him.
\b
\q1 \v 4 {Yahweh says,} “Listen carefully to me,
\q2 you people of my nation!
\q1 Do that because I am going to proclaim my laws,
\q2 and then the people of other nations will recognize how to live right.
\q1 \v 5 I am about to act justly and save you.
\q2 I will use my power to make things right among the nations.
\q1 People throughout the world will wait for me to act.
\q2 They will confidently expect me to use my power.
\q1 \v 6 Look up at the sky
\q2 and look down at the earth,
\q1 because the sky will disappear, just as smoke clears,
\q2 and the earth will go away, like old clothing that falls apart.
\q2 Similarly, the people who live on the earth will die.
\q1 But I will always keep saving you.
\q2 I will always do what is right.
\q1 \v 7 You people who know what is right
\q2 and who earnestly want to obey my laws, listen to me!
\q1 Do not be afraid when people insult you.
\q2 Do not let their mocking discourage you.
\q1 \v 8 Recognize that God will destroy those people,
\q2 just as grubs that will grow into moths eat wool clothes.
\q1 But I will always do what is right,
\q2 and I will keep saving you and your descendants.”
\b
\q1 \v 9 Yahweh, act with your great power!
\q2 Do mighty things as you did long ago,
\q2 many generations before now!
\q1 You are the one who pushed back the sea to make the dry land.
\q1 It was as if you destroyed a chaos monster
\q2 that lived in the ocean.
\q1 \v 10 You are the one who dried up the Sea of Reeds,
\q2 whose waters are so deep.
\q1 You made a dry path through the midst of that sea
\q2 so that the people you rescued could cross it!
\q1 \v 11 In the same way, those whom Yahweh frees from Babylon
\q2 will return to Zion, singing joyfully.
\q1 They will always be happy.
\q2 They will celebrate gladly.
\q2 They will no longer be sad or grieving.
\b
\q1 \v 12 {Yahweh says,} “I am the one who comforts you.
\q1 So you are people who should not be afraid of other people.
\q2 Human beings are mortal, like grass that dries up and dies.
\q1 \v 13 So you should not have abandoned me, Yahweh, who created you.
\q2 I am the one who created the sky and put it in place.
\q2 I am the one who formed the earth.
\q1 So you should not live in constant fear every day
\q2 because your enemies are angry and want to destroy you.
\q2 Even though they are angry, they will not be able to hurt you.
\q1 \v 14 Those who have been captives will soon go free!
\q1 They will not die in prison,
\q2 and they will not go hungry.
\q1 \v 15 I am Yahweh your God;
\q2 I am the one who calms the sea when it becomes stormy.
\q2 I am Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies!
\q1 \v 16 I have told you what to say on my behalf,
\q2 and I have kept you safe with my power.
\q1 I have told you to tell the exiles from Judah,
\q2 ‘You are my people!’
\q1 When I restore them, it will be as if
\q2 I am creating the sky and the land once more.”
\q1 \v 17 You people from Jerusalem, realize what is happening and respond.
\q2 Yahweh was angry with you, and he punished you.
\q1 It was as if you drank an entire large bowl of strong wine
\q2 and became so drunk that you could no longer stand up.
\q1 \v 18 None of the people from Jerusalem
\q2 are being leaders of their community
\q2 and guiding its people about what to do.
\q1 \v 19 Two very bad things have happened to you.
\q2 Enemies have destroyed your land, ruining your cities,
\q1 and your people have died from hunger and in battle.
\q2 No one is comforting you.
\q1 \v 20 Your people have collapsed,
\q2 and they are lying helplessly in the streets,
\q2 like animals caught in traps.
\q1 This has happened because Yahweh, your God,
\q2 has been angry with them and has punished them.
\q1 \v 21 But now please listen to this, you people who have suffered greatly.
\q1 You are staggering around in confusion,
\q2 but not because you have drunk too much wine.
\q1 \v 22 Yahweh, your Lord and your God,
\q2 who defends his people, says this:
\q1 “Listen! I am no longer angry with you,
\q2 so I am going to end these punishments,
\q2 and I will not punish you again.
\q1 \v 23 Instead, I will punish those who tormented you.
\q2 Those people said to you,
\q2 ‘Lie face down on the ground so that we can walk on top of you!’
\q1 They treated you as if your back were a street
\q2 for them to walk on.”
\c 52
\q1 \v 1 You people of Zion, become alert!
\q2 Become strong again!
\q1 You people of the holy city of Jerusalem
\q2 should {stop mourning by wearing sackcloth and} wear beautiful clothes
\q2 because foreign enemies will not invade your city again.
\q1 \v 2 You people of Jerusalem should stop {mourning by} sitting on the ground.
\q2 Shake off the dust and sit {in an honorable seat}.
\q1 Take the chains off your necks,
\q2 you captive people of Zion!
\p
\v 3 Do that because Yahweh says this:
\q1 “Your enemies did not pay me anything when they took you as their slaves.
\q2 So I am not going to pay them any money when I set you free.”
\p
\v 4 Yahweh, the Lord, also says this:
\q1 “My people originally went to Egypt to live there{,
\q2 but the Egyptians enslaved them}.
\q2 Later, the Assyrians made them their slaves {without paying anything for them}.”
\q1 \v 5 Yahweh declares, “I should not be in a situation now
\q1 in which an enemy has taken my people away without paying anything for them.”
\q2 Yahweh declares, “{Because of this,} their captors mock me,
\q1 and they continually speak with contempt about my reputation.”
\q1 \v 6 So I am going to show my people what I am really like {by rescuing them}.
\q1 Then they will realize that I have done what I promised.
\q2 I am ready to act!”
\b
\q1 \v 7 How wonderful it is when a messenger comes over the mountains
\q2 and announces that God is going to save his people
\q2 and make them peaceful and prosperous again.
\q1 He tells the people of Zion, “Your God is ruling as king!”
\q1 \v 8 Listen to the watchmen of your city!
\q2 They are all shouting and singing joyfully,
\q1 because they see clearly
\q2 that Yahweh is bringing back the people of Zion.
\q1 \v 9 {If they could,} the ruins of Jerusalem should all sing joyfully,
\q1 because Yahweh has encouraged his people
\q2 and has rescued the exiles from that city.
\q1 \v 10 Yahweh will show his holy power in such a way
\q2 that all foreign nations will recognize it.
\q1 People throughout the world
\q2 will see our God saving his people.
\b
\q1 \v 11 You must certainly leave Babylon!
\q2 Do not touch anything that is ritually unclean.
\q1 You will be bringing back the articles that the Babylonians took from Yahweh’s temple.
\q2 So become ritually clean and depart from among the Babylonians.
\q1 \v 12 You will have the time to do that because
\q2 you will not need to leave quickly.
\q1 That is because Yahweh will be leading you,
\q2 and he, the God of you Israelites, will also protect you from behind.
\b
\q1 \v 13 Yahweh says, “Pay attention!
\q2 My servant will accomplish what I send him to do,
\q1 and I will honor him greatly.
\q1 \v 14 Many people will stare at him in shock
\q2 because his enemies will beat him so badly
\q2 that he will no longer look like a human being.
\q1 \v 15 But he will purify the people of many nations,
\q2 the way the priests sprinkled blood on objects to purify them.
\q2 Kings will be speechless because of him,
\q1 because they will have seen something that no one had ever told them about,
\q2 and they will have understood something that they had never heard of before.”
\c 53
\q1 \v 1 We challenge people to believe what we tell them {about God’s servant}.
\q2 We challenge people to recognize that Yahweh has acted powerfully through him.
\q1 \v 2 While God watched over him, his servant grew up.
\q2 He was vulnerable and in danger,
\q2 like a young plant growing from a root in dry ground.
\q1 He did not get people’s attention by being handsome or splendid.
\q2 No, his appearance did not attract people.
\q1 \v 3 People spoke of him with contempt, and they did not accept him.
\q2 He experienced much sadness and disappointment.
\q1 Because people regarded him with contempt and did not value him,
\q2 they did not pay attention to him.
\q1 \v 4 But the truth is that he experienced the suffering that we should have experienced.
\q2 He endured the pain that we deserved.
\q1 Yet we thought that God was punishing him.
\q2 We believed that God was making him to suffer {for his own sins}.
\q1 \v 5 He suffered injury because of the evil things that we did.
\q2 He died because of our sins.
\q1 Because of his punishment, now we can have peace.
\q2 By his wounds, God has healed us.
\q1 \v 6 We have all disobeyed God,
\q2 just as sheep stray from their shepherd.
\q2 Each of us has done what we wanted {instead of what God wanted}.
\q1 But Yahweh punished him instead of us for our sins.
\b
\q1 \v 7 People treated him harshly and made him suffer,
\q2 but he did not protest.
\q1 They led him away to kill him
\q2 the way people lead sheep away to slaughter them.
\q1 And just as a sheep remains silent when someone shears its wool,
\q2 he said nothing to defend himself.
\q1 \v 8 After an unfair trial,
\q2 the authorities executed him.
\q1 Because people killed him,
\q2 it did not seem that he would have any descendants,
\q2 He received the punishment that his people deserved
\q2 for the wrong things they did.
\q1 \v 9 People planned to bury his body where they buried wicked people.
\q2 But instead, a rich man buried him in his own tomb.
\q1 That is because he had not done anything violent
\q2 and he had not said anything deceptive.
\q1 \v 10 Yet Yahweh chose to make him suffer and die.
\q2 When he accepts his life as a restoring sacrifice,
\q2 then he will have many descendants.
\q2 He will live for a long time,
\q1 and he will accomplish what Yahweh wants him to do.
\q1 \v 11 After he has suffered, he will live again,
\q2 and he will have a full number of descendants.
\q1 {Yahweh says,} “Because of what he experiences,
\q2 my servant, who will have done what is right,
\q1 will make many people right with me,
\q2 because he will have received the punishment for their sins.
\q1 \v 12 As a result, I will give him the kind of reward
\q2 that a great soldier receives after a battle.
\q1 I will do that because he was willing
\q2 for people to consider him a criminal and execute him.
\q1 He took the punishment for the sins of many people,
\q2 and he prays for people who have sinned.”
\c 54
\q1 \v 1 Yahweh says, “You people of Jerusalem should start
\q2 singing joyfully and shouting loudly.
\q1 Your city is like a woman
\q2 who has never gone into labor and given birth to any children.
\q1 But soon it will be like a woman
\q2 whose husband once abandoned her but then took her back,
\q2 and she had more children than a woman who was always married.
\q1 \v 2 You are like people who live in a tent who need to enlarge it
\q2 to make room for more children.
\q1 They would use more material and longer ropes
\q2 to make a larger space to live in.
\q1 They would make sure that the stakes were strong enough
\q2 to hold this larger tent.
\q1 \v 3 Get ready, because the people of the city will spread out in every direction.
\q2 Their families will occupy territory that other nations now control,
\q2 and they will settle in cities in which no one now lives.
\b
\q1 \v 4 You have felt shame, as if you were a woman
\q2 {who did not have children} when she was young.
\q1 You have felt disgrace, as if you were a woman whose husband had died.
\q2 But you will not feel those things anymore.
\q1 So, since no one will disgrace you again, do not be afraid.
\q2 Since no one will humiliate you again, do not feel shame.
\q1 \v 5 This is because I, Yahweh, the commander of the heavenly armies,
\q2 the one who made you, will be like a husband to you.
\q1 I, the holy God of the Israelites, am the one who is rescuing you.
\q2 I am the God who rules the whole world.”
\q1 \v 6 Your God says, “Indeed, I, Yahweh, have seen
\q2 that you are like a wife whose husband left her.
\q1 You have been very sad, like a woman
\q2 whose husband married her when he was young
\q2 but then rejected her.
\q1 \v 7 I sent you away for a short time,
\q2 but now I will bring you back to me very tenderly.
\q1 \v 8 Because I was so angry with you,
\q2 I had nothing to do with you for a time.
\q2 But from now on, I will always treat you kindly and mercifully.”
\q1 That is what Yahweh says. He is the one who is rescuing you.
\b
\q1 \v 9 “I promise this because for me, this is like the flood in Noah’s time.
\q2 Then I swore that a flood would never cover the earth again.
\q1 In the same way, I now swear that I will not
\q2 be angry with you or punish you.
\q1 \v 10 You can be sure of this because even if the mountains and hills crumble,
\q2 I will always keep loving you faithfully.
\q1 I will never break my promise
\q2 to make things go well for you.”
\q2 That is what Yahweh says. He is the one who is treating you mercifully.
\b
\q1 \v 11 “You suffering people of Jerusalem, enemies have battered you,
\q2 and no one has helped you.
\q1 Listen! I am going to rebuild your city splendidly.
\q2 It will be as if the stones have turquoise settings
\q2 and as if the foundations are sapphires.
\q1 \v 12 It will be as if people have made the fortifications from rubies,
\q2 the gates from beryl, and the walls from precious stones.
\q1 \v 13 I myself will teach everyone who resides in the city,
\q2 and they will live very peacefully.
\q1 \v 14 People in the city will do what is right.
\q2 No one will treat you cruelly, so you will not have to be afraid.
\q2 Nothing harmful will attack you, so will not have to feel terror.
\q1 \v 15 Listen! If anyone attacks you,
\q2 it will not be because I sent them to do that.
\q2 You will defeat anyone who attacks you.
\b
\q1 \v 16 Think about this: I am the one who created metalworkers
\q2 who blow on coals to make hot fires
\q1 so they can produce weapons that will be effective.
\q2 I am also the one who allows armies to destroy things.
\q1 \v 17 No weapon that anyone makes to use against you will be effective.
\q2 If anyone speaks against you to accuse you,
\q2 you will prove that they are wrong.
\q1 This is what I, Yahweh, do for my servants.
\q2 I make them victorious.”
\q2 This is what Yahweh declares.
\c 55
\q1 \v 1 “Listen! Anyone who is thirsty may come and drink water.
\q2 {Anyone who is hungry} may come and eat
\q2 even if he has no money to pay for the food.
\q1 Yes, come and drink milk or wine, even if you have no money,
\q2 because it does not cost anything.
\q1 \v 2 You should not pay money for things that do not nourish you.
\q2 You should not work hard to get things that do not satisfy you.
\q1 Pay careful attention to what I say.
\q2 Get things that are good for you,
\q2 and that will richly satisfy you deep inside.
\q1 \v 3 Pay attention to me and come to me.
\q2 Do what I say, and you will live.
\q1 I will make an agreement with you that will last forever.
\q2 I will treat you kindly, just as surely as I treated King David kindly.
\q1 \v 4 Consider how I used him to show the people of other nations what I am like.
\q2 I made him a ruler who governed other nations.
\q1 \v 5 In the same way, you will call to the people of nations
\q2 you have not heard about before.
\q1 Those people will have not heard about you either.
\q2 But they will hurry to you
\q1 to find out about Yahweh your God. He is the holy God of you Israelites.
\q2 People will come to you because he will make you glorious.”
\b
\q1 \v 6 Worship Yahweh now while he will accept your worship.
\q2 Pray to him now while he is approaching you.
\q1 \v 7 Wicked people should stop doing evil things.
\q2 Sinful people should stop thinking evil thoughts.
\q1 They should repent and ask Yahweh, our God, to forgive them,
\q2 because will be merciful to them
\q2 and he will forgive them even if they have done very bad things.
\b
\q1 \v 8 Yahweh declares, “Recognize that I do not think the way you think,
\q2 and I do not do things the way you do things.
\q1 \v 9 Recognize that just as the sky is far above the earth,
\q2 so the things I do are far greater than the things you do.
\q2 The way I think is far beyond the way you think.
\q1 \v 10 Consider how rain and snow come down from the sky
\q2 and do not go back up again
\q1 without soaking the ground
\q2 so that plants sprout and grow.
\q1 Then the earth produces seeds that farmers plant,
\q2 and they grow grain that people use to make bread to eat.
\q1 \v 11 The command that I have spoken will be like that.
\q2 I will not discover later that it did not make happen what I want to happen.
\q1 Instead, it will make happen
\q2 the things about which I gave the command.
\q1 \v 12 Indeed, you will leave Babylon rejoicing.
\q2 I will lead you out of there without any trouble.
\q1 It will be as if the mountains and hills celebrate as you pass by.
\q2 It will be as if all the trees in the countryside clap their hands.
\q1 \v 13 Where thornbushes and briars once grew,
\q2 cypress trees and myrtle trees will grow instead.
\q1 People will honor Yahweh because of this.
\q2 It will always remind people that he does what he promises.”
\c 56
\p
\v 1 Yahweh says,
\q2 “Soon I will come to save you
\q2 and show that I act righteously.
\q1 So treat people fairly and do what is right.
\q1 \v 2 I want you to treat Sabbath days as special
\q2 and make sure that you do not do anything wrong.
\q1 Everyone who does those things consistently
\q2 will have a good life.
\b
\q1 \v 3 Foreigners who want to worship me should not say,
\q2 ‘Yahweh will certainly not let me belong to his people.’
\q1 Eunuchs should not say, ‘{Yahweh will not accept me}
\q2 because I cannot have any children.’
\q1 \v 4 Instead, Yahweh says,
\q2 Suppose eunuchs treat Sabbath days as special.
\q1 Suppose they choose to do things that please me.
\q2 Suppose they honor the agreement I made with the Israelites.
\q1 \v 5 If they do that, I will welcome them into the courts of my temple.
\q2 Their reputation will make people honor them
\q2 more than having sons and daughters would have.
\q1 They will always have a good reputation.
\q2 No one will destroy it.
\q1 \v 6 Suppose foreigners want to worship me and obey me.
\q1 Suppose they devote themselves to me as my servants.
\q1 Suppose they treat Sabbath days as special by doing no work on those days.
\q2 Suppose they honor the agreement I made with the Israelites.
\q1 \v 7 Then I will bring them to Mount Zion,
\q2 where I am present in a special way.
\q1 I will allow them to come happily to my temple,
\q2 where people pray to me.
\q1 I will accept the sacrifices that they burn on my altar
\q2 and the other sacrifices they offer.
\q1 I will do that because I want people to know
\q2 that my temple is a place where the people of all nations can pray to me.”
\q1 \v 8 This is what Yahweh, the Lord, declares.
\q2 He is bringing back the people of Israel whom enemies took away.
\q1 {He says,} “I will bring even more people
\q2 to join the ones I have already brought back.”
\b
\q1 \v 9 Troubles of many kinds
\q2 should cause difficulty for the Israelites
\q2 {so that they will realize they are doing things that are wrong}.
\q1 \v 10 None of the leaders are aware of the dangers that could harm the people.
\q2 It is as if they are blind.
\q1 They are all unable to warn the people,
\q2 like dogs that can not bark
\q2 and instead want to lie down and sleep and dream.
\q1 \v 11 These leaders are greedy and continually want to get more wealth.
\q2 They are like shepherds who do not know how to care for their flocks,
\q1 Each one tries to do what benefits himself
\q2 and to make more money. They are all like that.
\q1 \v 12 {They say to each other,} “Come on, let us get some wine
\q2 and beer, and let us get drunk!
\q1 Tomorrow will be as good as today,
\q2 or even much better!”
\c 57
\q1 \v 1 People who do what is right and honor God’s agreement sometimes die,
\q2 but others do not think carefully about why that happens.
\q1 They do not realize that God takes good people away
\q2 so they will not have to experience bad things.
\q1 \v 2 When people who have lived in the right way die,
\q2 they lie peacefully in their graves.
\b
\q1 \v 3 {Yahweh says,} “But some of you practice sorcery.
\q2 You Israelites should be faithful to me,
\q2 but instead you worship idols.
\q1 Now listen to me!
\q1 \v 4 You are sinning against me,
\q2 but you are pretending that you are not.
\q1 That is just as if you are mocking me
\q2 and gaping at me and sticking your tongues out at me!
\q1 \v 5 You commit sexual immorality in oak groves
\q2 and under many great trees.
\q1 You even sacrifice your children to idols in stream beds,
\q2 below rocky cliffs.
\q1 \v 6 You select smooth stones from stream beds
\q2 and you set them up and worship them as your gods!
\q1 You also pour out wine as an offering to them,
\q2 and you bring grain as an offering to them.
\q1 I should not refrain from punishing you for doing these things!
\q1 \v 7 You go up to hilltop shrines to offer sacrifices to false gods.
\q2 You are being unfaithful to me when you do that.
\q1 \v 8 You also keep things in your own homes that you remind you of those gods.
\q1 Indeed, you abandon me and pledge your loyalty to false gods.
\q2 In that way, you are like a wife who is unfaithful to her husband,
\q1 who goes and has sexual relations with another man
\q2 and enjoys being with him.
\q1 \v 9 You bring olive oil and many perfumes as gifts to Molech.
\q2 You look throughout the world for further gods to worship.
\q2 You even deal with spirits of the place where dead people are.
\q1 \v 10 You wear yourselves out with these efforts,
\q2 but you do not say, ‘This is useless.’
\q1 You realize that you have a little more strength to keep going,
\q2 so you do not give up.
\b
\q1 \v 11 You seem to more afraid of some other god than of me!
\q2 That is why you do not worship me or even think about me
\q2 but pretend that you are still loyal to me.
\q1 I have not acted to punish sinful people for a long time,
\q2 and that seems to be why you do not fear me.
\q1 \v 12 But now I will describe the true character of what you have been doing,
\q2 which you claim is right,
\q2 and it will be clear that you deserve punishment.
\q1 \v 13 Go ahead and cry to your collection of idols for help,
\q2 but you will find that they do not save you.
\q2 They are so worthless that it is as if
\q2 the wind could blow them all away.
\q2 It is as if a single breath could carry them off.
\q1 However, those who trust in me will live in the land.
\q2 They will worship me on Mount Zion,
\q2 where I am present in a special way.”
\p
\v 14 Someone will say,
\q1 “Help the people be loyal to Yahweh again!
\q2 Remove every obstacle that keeps us Israelites from worshiping him!”
\q1 \v 15 Yahweh is greater than everything.
\q2 He has always existed, and his character is holy.
\q2 This is what he says.
\q1 “I dwell in a high, holy place.
\q2 But I also live with people who are humble and sorry for their sins.
\q1 I encourage people who are humble,
\q2 and I give hope to people who are sorry for their sins.
\q1 \v 16 I will not keep accusing my people,
\q2 and I will not keep being angry with them.
\q1 If I did that, it would discourage them.
\q2 They would feel that they could not please me.
\q2 Then the people I created would give up.
\q1 \v 17 People did wicked things in order to get money.
\q2 That made me angry, and I punished them.
\q2 Because I was angry, I withdrew from them.
\q1 But they kept wanting to do what I did not want them to do.
\q1 \v 18 I have seen what they have done,
\q2 but I will restore them,
\q2 and I will guide them.
\q1 I will comfort the Israelites,
\q2 especially those who are sorry for their sins.
\q1 \v 19 This will lead them to praise me.
\q2 I will make things go well for people throughout the community,”
\q2 Yahweh says. “I will restore my people.
\q1 \v 20 However, if people continue to be wicked,
\q1 their lives will be like the choppy sea. It never stops churning.
\q2 Its waves constantly pull up muddy dirt.”
\q1 \v 21 Our God says, “Things will not go well for wicked people.”
\c 58
\q1 \v 1 {Yahweh told me,} “Shout in a deep voice as loudly as you can!
\q2 Make a loud noise as people do with a ram’s horn!
\q1 Tell my people, the descendants of Jacob,
\q2 how they are sinning against me!
\q1 \v 2 They worship me every day.
\q2 They say that they want to know what I want them to do.
\q1 They act as though they are people who do what is right
\q2 and who keep obeying my laws.
\q1 They ask me to judge justly,
\q2 and they enjoy coming to my temple.
\q1 \v 3 {They say,} ‘We did something difficult by going without food.
\q2 So you should have done something for us in response.’
\b
\q1 {Tell them for me,} ‘That is because on days when you go without food,
\q1 you still pursue your own interests,
\q2 and you treat your workers cruelly to make them work hard for you.
\q1 \v 4 Indeed, you go without food {as a religious practice},
\q2 but at the same time, you also quarrel and argue.
\q2 You even punch people wickedly.
\q1 When you fast like that, you can not expect me in heaven
\q2 to do what you ask me to do.
\q1 \v 5 I do not want people to fast the way you do.
\q2 When you fast, you go without food.
\q1 You bow your heads like reeds.
\q2 You lie down on rough cloth and ashes.
\q1 But you should not call that fasting
\q2 or say that it pleases me, Yahweh.
\b
\q1 \v 6 No, this is the kind of fasting that I want:
\q1 I want you to stop treating people unjustly and to stop oppressing them.
\q1 I want you to restore those whose lives others have destroyed
\q2 and to stop everyone from oppressing others.
\q1 \v 7 I want you to share your food with hungry people
\q2 and bring into your houses poor people who have nowhere else to live.
\q1 When you see people who lack clothing,
\q2 I want you to provide clothes for them.
\q1 Help these people; they are your own kinsmen!
\q1 \v 8 If you do those things,
\q1 this difficult time will end,
\q2 just as night ends at sunrise.
\q1 You will rebuild your ruined cities,
\q2 just as young plants grow vigorously from the ground.
\q1 Your desire to do what is right will guide you,
\q2 and I, Yahweh, will protect you with my glorious presence.
\q1 \v 9 Then, when you pray to me,
\q2 I will do what you ask me to do.
\q1 When you cry for help,
\q2 I will assure you, “I am here to help you.”
\b
\q1 Stop oppressing people.
\q2 Stop accusing people falsely,
\q2 and stop saying wicked things about people.
\q1 \v 10 Give generously to hungry people,
\q2 and provide poor people with what they need.
\q1 Then this time of difficulties will end.
\q1 It will feel like a bright, sunny day
\q2 instead of a dark, gloomy one.
\q1 \v 11 Yahweh will always guide you.
\q2 He will provide what you need, wherever you are.
\q1 He will make you healthy,
\q2 like plants that have all the water they need.
\q1 You will be like a spring
\q2 whose waters always keep flowing.
\q1 \v 12 Some of you will rebuild the cities that others destroyed long ago.
\q2 They will rebuild them on their original foundations.
\q1 Others will know you as people who repair their walls
\q2 and restore places for people to live in.
\b
\q1 \v 13 Treat the Sabbath as a special day.
\q2 I have made it holy, so do not do whatever you want on that day.
\q1 Be eager to respect the Sabbath.
\q2 Honor it because I, Yahweh, have made it holy.
\q2 Honor it by not pursuing your own interests,
\q1 by not just doing what you enjoy, and by not talking idly.
\q1 \v 14 If you do those things,
\q2 you will be happy as you serve me, Yahweh.
\q1 I will make you very prosperous,
\q2 and I will bless you, as I promised to your ancestor Jacob.’”
\q1 These things will surely happen, because Yahweh has said that they will happen.
\c 59
\q1 \v 1 Listen! Yahweh is powerful enough to save you.
\q2 He can hear you when you call for help.
\q1 \v 2 Rather, the wrong things you have done have separated you from your God.
\q1 Your sins have led him to disregard you
\q2 and not do what you ask.
\q1 \v 3 You have done wicked things,
\q2 including attacking people violently.
\q1 You say bad things that are not true.
\q1 \v 4 People do not bring others to court honestly,
\q2 and they do not plead their own cases fairly.
\q1 They depend on being deceptive, and they tell lies.
\q2 They plan to cause trouble, and then they carry out their evil plans.
\q1 \v 5 They make bad things happen,
\q2 as if they were hatching snakes from eggs.
\q1 If people eat those eggs, they will die,
\q2 If they crack open their shells, a poisonous snake will come out.
\q1 It is as if those wicked people are spiders
\q2 that spin webs to catch other creatures.
\q1 \v 6 They do not produce useful things,
\q2 just as people can not make clothing from spider webs.
\q1 That is because what they do is wicked.
\q2 They even attack other people violently.
\q1 \v 7 They are eager to do things that are wrong.
\q2 They rush to murder innocent people.
\q1 They think of wicked things to do.
\q2 Wherever they go, they destroy things and make people suffer.
\q1 \v 8 They do not know how to live peacefully
\q2 and they do not treat others fairly.
\q1 They behave dishonestly.
\q2 Those who live as they do will not have peaceful lives.
\b
\q1 \v 9 Because of this, Yahweh is not delivering us.
\q2 He is not rescuing us from our troubles.
\q1 We hope that things will go well for us, but instead things go badly.
\q2 We hope for relief, but instead we experience trouble.
\q1 \v 10 We do not know what we should do,
\q2 as if we are blind people who must feel their way along a wall.
\q1 We should know what to do, as people can see clearly in daylight,
\q2 but instead we make mistakes, as people stumble in dim light.
\q1 Compared with strong people,
\q2 we are so weak that it is as if we were dead.
\q1 \v 11 We all grumble as if we were bears,
\q2 and we moan as if we were doves.
\q1 We wait for Yahweh to deliver us, but he does not.
\q2 We long for him to rescue us, but he does not.
\b
\q1 \v 12 We know that we have sinned many times against Yahweh.
\q2 What we have done wrong shows that we are guilty.
\q1 Yes, we are aware that we have sinned.
\q2 We know what we have done wrong.
\q1 \v 13 We have rebelled against Yahweh and rejected him.
\q2 We have stopped obeying our God.
\q1 We made plans to disobey him by oppressing others.
\q2 We carefully thought of untrue things to say, and then we said them.
\q1 \v 14 Judges do not decide cases fairly,
\q2 and people do not do what is right.
\q1 They do not tell the truth in the public places where people gather.
\q2 People are not able to speak honestly.
\q1 \v 15 People no longer tell the truth.
\q2 They take advantage of others who are honest.
\b
\q1 Yahweh saw this, and it displeased him
\q2 that no one was acting fairly.
\q1 \v 16 It discouraged him when he saw
\q2 that no one was trying to help others.
\q1 So he used his own power to rescue his people.
\q2 He always does what is right, and that strengthened him.
\q1 \v 17 He does what is right,
\q2 and that was like armor for him.
\q2 He saves people, and that was like a helmet for his head.
\q1 He punishes people for doing wrong, and that was like a garment for him.
\q2 He wants people to worship only him, and that was like a robe for him.
\q1 \v 18 He will punish people as they deserve for what they have done.
\q2 He will be angry with those who oppose him,
\q2 and he will treat those enemies as they deserve.
\q2 Throughout the world, he will punish people as they deserve.
\q1 \v 19 Then people throughout the world
\q2 will respect Yahweh because he is great and glorious.
\q1 That is because he will come to punish people powerfully and quickly,
\q2 the way water rushes in a narrow stream when a strong wind pushes it along.
\q1 \v 20 Yahweh declares, “I will come to Mount Zion
\q2 and rescue those Israelites who stop sinning.”
\p
\v 21 Yahweh says {to his people}, “This is the agreement that I am making with you. I will never take away from you my Spirit, who is with you, and the message that I have given you to speak. You and your descendants and their descendants will always be declaring my message.” Yahweh says, “This will be true from now on and forever.”
\c 60
\q1 \v 1 {Yahweh says,} “You people of Jerusalem,
\q2 be very happy, as if you were the sun shining brightly.
\q2 I, Yahweh, am about to do glorious things for you.
\q1 \v 2 It will be as if it is still a dark night
\q2 for the people of other nations,
\q1 but I, Yahweh, will make it as if
\q2 it is a bright day for you.
\q2 What I do for you will show that I am glorious.
\q1 \v 3 The rulers and people of other nations will come to you
\q2 to find out what is happening,
\q2 because you will be like a bright sunrise.
\q1 \v 4 Look around and see how the people whom your enemies took away
\q2 are gathering together in order to return.
\q1 Your sons will come from distant countries,
\q2 and people will carry your little daughters back home on their hips.
\q1 \v 5 When you see this happening, you will beam.
\q2 You will be so happy that you will tremble inside.
\q1 This is because people of other nations
\q2 will bring treasures to you.
\q2 They will even bring it to you across the sea in ships.
\q1 \v 6 There will be so many camels {carrying treasure to you}
\q2 that it will seem as if the camels fill your land.
\q2 These will be young camels from the regions of Midian and Ephah.
\q1 People will also come from Sheba
\q2 bringing gold and frankincense.
\q2 They will say how great I, Yahweh, am.
\q1 \v 7 People from Kedar will bring you flocks of sheep and goats.
\q2 The people of Nebaioth will bring you rams for you to use.
\q1 When you sacrifice them on my altar,
\q1 I will gladly accept them as offerings.
\q2 That will honor my glorious temple.
\q1 \v 8 I will tell you what those vessels are that are moving so quickly,
\q2 like clouds sweeping across the sky
\q2 or like doves flying to their nests.
\q1 \v 9 They are fast ships that can sail long distances.
\q2 They are the first ones to arrive bringing back the people whom your enemies took away.
\q1 People throughout the world were ready to send them back
\q2 when I commanded them to do that.
\q1 They are sending gold and silver back with them.
\q1 I, Yahweh, the holy God whom you Israelites worship,
\q2 have honored you, and those people are doing this
\q2 because they respect me.
\q1 \v 10 Foreigners will rebuild the walls of your cities,
\q2 and their kings will help you.
\q1 That is because I am pleased with you, and so I will now treat you favorably,
\q2 even though I punished you before because I was angry with you.
\q1 \v 11 The gates of your cities will stay open all of the time.
\q2 That will allow people from many nations to bring you valuable things.
\q2 Their kings will come and submit to you.
\q1 \v 12 You will certainly defeat and conquer
\q2 any nation or kingdom whose people refuse to serve you.
\q1 \v 13 People will bring you the finest timber from Lebanon,
\q2 including cypress trees, plane trees, and box trees.
\q1 You will be able to use that wood to make my temple beautiful.
\q2 In that way, I will make glorious the place where I am present in a special way.
\q1 \v 14 People who belong to the nations that treated you cruelly
\q2 will come and bow down humbly before you.
\q1 Those who hated you will lie {submissively} face down in front of you.
\q1 They will call Jerusalem ‘Yahweh’s City.’
\q2 They will call it ‘Zion, Where Israel’s Holy God Is.’
\q1 \v 15 Formerly, people hated Jerusalem and did not want to live there.
\q2 People did not even pass through the city.
\q1 But now I will make the city a place in which people will always be proud to live.
\q2 People of future generations will be happy to live there.
\q1 \v 16 You will become rich from the tribute that the kings of other nations pay you,
\q2 just as a baby becomes strong from drinking its mother’s milk.
\q1 Then you will recognize that I, Yahweh,
\q2 have saved you by rescuing you from your enemies.
\q1 You will recognize that I,
\q2 the God whom you descendants of Jacob worship, am mighty.
\q1 \v 17 I will give you gold instead of bronze
\q2 and silver instead of iron.
\q1 I will give you bronze instead of wood
\q2 and iron instead of stones.
\q1 I will cause your leaders to govern peacefully
\q2 and rule you fairly.
\q1 \v 18 People in your land will no longer act violently.
\q2 Within your territory, people will not destroy things and make people suffer.
\q1 You will be safe within the walls of your city,
\q2 and people who come through your gates will praise me.
\q1 \v 19 It will be as if you no longer need the sun to give you light during the day
\q2 or the moon to shine on you at night.
\q1 That is because I, Yahweh, will always make you glorious.
\q2 Yes, I, your God, will make you splendid.
\q1 \v 20 It will be as if the sun never sets and the moon is always full.
\q2 That is because I, Yahweh, will always make you glorious.
\q1 You will not grieve anymore.
\q1 \v 21 All of your people will do what is right.
\q2 They will always live in the land.
\q1 I myself have established them there.
\q2 Because I have done this, people will glorify me.
\q1 \v 22 A little group of your people will become very large.
\q2 A small tribe will grow to become a powerful nation.
\q1 When it is the right time,
\q2 I, Yahweh, will make this happen quickly.”
\c 61
\q1 \v 1 Because Yahweh, the Lord, has chosen me for this task,
\q2 his Spirit is directing me.
\q1 He wants me to tell good news to people who are poor,
\q2 to encourage people who are very sad,
\q1 and to announce that he will set free
\q2 people whom their enemies have captured.
\q1 \v 2 He has sent me to announce that Yahweh will now act kindly toward his people
\q2 but that he, our God, will punish their enemies.
\q2 He is going to comfort people who have been grieving.
\q1 \v 3 This is what he will do for the people of Jerusalem who have been grieving.
\q1 They have been putting ashes on their heads {to express their grief},
\q2 but he will give them beautiful headdresses to wear instead.
\q1 Since they will not be sad anymore,
\q2 he will have them put oil on their hair as people do when they celebrate.
\q2 He will enable them to praise him instead of feeling despair.
\q1 People will acknowledge that Yahweh has restored them to their land
\q2 and made them great and strong like oak trees.
\q2 This will glorify him.
\q1 \v 4 They will rebuild the cities that have been in ruins for a long time.
\q2 Their enemies destroyed those cities,
\q2 and no one has lived in them for many generations.
\q1 \v 5 Foreigners will serve you by caring for your flocks.
\q2 People from other countries will plow your fields and tend your vineyards.
\q1 \v 6 People will say that you are like priests who serve Yahweh, our God.
\q1 You will enjoy valuable things that come from other nations.
\q2 You will be proud of the wealth you get from them.
\q1 \v 7 You will no longer feel shame,
\q2 because you will have twice as many good things as you had before.
\q1 You will no longer feel disgrace.
\q2 Instead, you will shout joyfully about what you have received.
\q2 You will possess twice as much as before in your own land,
\q2 and you will always be happy.
\b
\q1 \v 8 “I, Yahweh, love it when people judge fairly,
\q2 but I hate it when people rob others and act unjustly.
\q1 I will reward my people for being faithful to me.
\q2 I will make a permanent agreement with them.
\q1 \v 9 Their descendants will do things that make them famous.
\q2 People of other nations will know about them.
\q1 Those who see them will recognize
\q2 that they are a people whom I, Yahweh, have blessed.”
\b
\q1 \v 10 I am very happy because of what Yahweh has done!
\q2 Yes, I am very glad about what my God has done.
\q1 He has saved me by delivering me from my enemies.
\q2 It is as if he has given me new clothes to wear,
\q2 just as a bridegroom puts on a fine turban
\q2 and a bride wears her jewelry.
\q1 \v 11 Just as seeds grow from the ground
\q2 and things that people plant in gardens grow there,
\q1 so Yahweh, the Lord, will deliver his people
\q2 as the people of other nations are watching,
\q2 and this will make people praise him.
\c 62
\q1 \v 1 Because I care deeply about you people who live in
\q2 the city of Jerusalem on Mount Zion,
\q1 I will keep praying for you
\q2 until God delivers you in a way that shows clearly
\q1 that he is the one who has saved you.
\q1 \v 2 The people of other nations will recognize that God has delivered you,
\q2 and their kings will recognize that he has made your city glorious.
\q1 Yahweh himself will give the city a new name,
\q2 and that is what people will call it.
\q1 \v 3 Yahweh your God will proudly show what you have become,
\q2 as a king might hold out his beautiful royal crown for people to admire.
\q1 \v 4 People will no longer call you “Abandoned,”
\q2 and they will no longer call your land “Desolate.”
\q1 Instead, they will call you “Hephzibah,”
\q2 and they will call your land “Beulah.”
\q1 That is because you will please Yahweh,
\q2 and your land will belong to him,
\q2 just as a wife belongs to her husband.
\q1 \v 5 The people who are born in the land
\q2 will commit themselves to caring for it and defending it,
\q2 just as a young man commits himself to the young woman he marries.
\q1 Your God will be very happy about you,
\q2 just as a bridegroom is happy about his bride.
\q1 \v 6 You people of Jerusalem, I have appointed people to pray for you.
\q2 They will pray for you continually.
\q1 You who are to remind Yahweh {about what he has promised to do},
\q2 keep praying
\q1 \v 7 and keep reminding Yahweh
\q2 until he restores Jerusalem
\q1 and makes people throughout the earth
\q2 admire the city.
\q1 \v 8 Yahweh has made a solemn promise to the people of Jerusalem,
\q2 and he has guaranteed it by his own great power:
\q1 “I will never again let your enemies take away your grain and eat it,
\q2 and foreigners will never again drink up the new wine
\q1 that you have worked hard to produce.
\q1 \v 9 Instead, you who harvest the grain will eat it yourselves,
\q2 and you will thank me, Yahweh, as you eat it.
\q1 You who gather the grapes will drink the wine yourselves
\q2 in the courtyards of my temple.”
\q1 \v 10 Go out through the city gates right away!
\q2 Prepare a road on which the people can return!
\q1 Build up the highway well,
\q2 and clear away the stones!
\q1 Raise a signal flag so that the people of other nations
\q2 will know the way to Jerusalem!
\q1 \v 11 Listen! Yahweh has proclaimed this message to people throughout the earth:
\q1 “Tell the people of Jerusalem,
\q2 ‘Pay attention! The one who saves you is coming!
\q2 He is bringing with him the people he has taken from your enemies.’”
\q1 \v 12 People will call them “the holy people,”
\q2 “the ones whom Yahweh has rescued.”
\q1 People will call Jerusalem “The city that God cares for,”
\q2 “the city that God will always protect.”
\c 63
\q1 \v 1 {Someone asks,} “Who is this person coming from the city of Bozrah in Edom
\q2 whose clothes are red with blood stains?
\q1 Who is this person wearing such splendid clothing
\q2 who is marching forward with great strength?”
\q1 {One replies,} “I am Yahweh! I declare what is right,
\q2 and I have the power to save people.”
\q1 \v 2 {Someone asks,} “Why are your clothes so red?
\q2 You look as if you have been crushing grapes in a winepress.”
\q1 \v 3 {One replies,} “I have crushed enemy nations as if they were grapes,
\q2 and I did it alone. No one else helped me.
\q1 Because I was very angry, I stomped on them and destroyed them.
\q2 Their blood splashed onto my clothes,
\q1 and it stained everything I was wearing.
\q1 \v 4 I did this because I had planned to punish them at a certain time.
\q2 It was time for me to rescue my people.
\q1 \v 5 I looked around, but no one was there to help.
\q2 It shocked me that no one would support me.
\q1 So I used my own power to win the victory.
\q2 My fierce anger motivated me.
\q1 \v 6 Because I was very angry, I destroyed those enemy nations.
\q2 I made them stagger helplessly,
\q2 and their blood poured out onto the ground.”
\q1 \v 7 I will tell about how Yahweh loves us faithfully.
\q2 I will praise him for everything he has done for us.
\q1 Yahweh has been very good to the people of Israel
\q2 because he faithfully loves us so much.
\q2 So he has treated us mercifully.
\q1 \v 8 Yahweh said, “Certainly these are my people.
\q2 They are my spiritual children, so they will not betray me.”
\q2 So he saved them.
\q1 \v 9 When they suffered, he shared in their distress.
\q2 He sent an angel representing him, and that angel saved them.
\q1 Because he loved them and was kind to them,
\q2 he rescued them.
\q1 He strengthened them and supported them
\q2 throughout that time long ago.
\q1 \v 10 But for their part, they rebelled against him.
\q2 They made his Holy Spirit very sad.
\q1 So he acted as if he were their enemy;
\q2 he himself fought against them.
\b
\q1 \v 11 Then his people remembered what had happened long before, in the time of Moses.
\q2 {They cried out,} “We need the help of the one
\q2 who brought our ancestors through the Sea of Reeds
\q1 with Moses, Aaron, and Miriam as their leaders!
\q2 We need the help of the one who sent
\q2 his Holy Spirit to be with them!
\q1 \v 12 He is the one who worked alongside Moses by his great power.
\q2 He is the one who made a dry path for them through the Sea of Reeds.
\q2 In that way, he gained a lasting reputation.
\q1 \v 13 He is the one who led them through the depths of the sea.
\q2 They did not stumble as they walked through it,
\q2 just as horses do not stumble when they run across open country.
\q1 \v 14 Just as cattle go down into a valley to find pasture,
\q2 Yahweh’s Spirit gave them a place to rest.
\q1 That is how you led your people, Yahweh,
\q2 and gained a glorious reputation for yourself.
\q1 \v 15 Yahweh, please look down from your holy and glorious home in heaven
\q2 and consider our situation.
\q1 We need you to be eager to help us and to act powerfully on our behalf.
\q2 But you no longer seem to feel sorry for us and want to help us.
\q1 \v 16 You are the true ancestor of our nation.
\q2 Even if Abraham and Jacob did not acknowledge us
\q2 as their descendants,
\q1 you, Yahweh, would be our ancestor.
\q2 People have always recognized you as the one who rescues us.
\q1 \v 17 Yahweh, we do not understand why you have allowed us to stop obeying you.
\q2 We do not understand why you have allowed us to become unwilling to respect you.
\q1 Please help us again,
\q2 since we are people who serve you.
\q2 We are the tribes that belong to you.
\q1 \v 18 We people who belong to you
\q2 lived in the land you gave us for only a short time.
\q2 Then our enemies came and destroyed your temple.
\q1 \v 19 We have become like people whom you never ruled.
\q2 It is as if we never belonged to you.
\c 64
\q1 \v 1 “Yahweh, we wish that you would come down from heaven
\q2 and make mountains shake when you arrive!
\q1 \v 2 If you made your enemies realize who you are,
\q1 the people of other nations would shake with fear in your presence,
\q2 just as brushwood ignites or water boils when fire heats it.
\q1 \v 3 In the past, you did amazing things that we could not have imagined.
\q2 You came down from heaven and the mountains shook because you were there.
\q1 \v 4 You act to help those who trust you.
\q2 You are the only God that anyone has ever seen {who does this}.
\q2 From ancient times, no one has ever heard about
\q2 {anyone else doing this}.
\q1 \v 5 You welcome those who are glad to do what is right.
\q2 Those people are careful to live as you want.
\q1 However, you became angry because we continually sinned
\q2 by not obeying your laws. We do not deserve to have you save us.
\q1 \v 6 We have all become like people who are not acceptable to you.
\q2 The things we do that we think are good
\q2 are actually worthless.
\q1 We have all become as weak as a dying leaf,
\q2 and our sins have separated us from you,
\q2 as if they were a wind that had blown us far away from you.
\q1 \v 7 Our people do not pray to you,
\q2 and they do not strive to remain loyal to you.
\q1 So you no longer help us.
\q2 Instead, you let the wrong things we do destroy us.
\q1 \v 8 And yet, Yahweh, you are our father.
\q2 You made every one of us,
\q2 as if we were clay and you were a potter.
\q1 \v 9 Yahweh, please do not be so very angry with us.
\q2 Please do not keep thinking about the wrong things we have done.
\q2 We are all your people, so please pay attention to us.
\q1 \v 10 Few people now live in the cities in the land that is special to you.
\q2 Enemies have even destroyed the city of Jerusalem on Mount Zion
\q2 so that few people live there.
\q1 \v 11 Our ancestors praised you in a beautiful sacred temple.
\q2 But now enemies have burned that temple down,
\q2 and they have destroyed all the things that we treasured there.
\q1 \v 12 Since this has happened, Yahweh,
\q2 please do not hold yourself back from helping us any longer!
\q2 Please do something so that we stop suffering so terribly!”
\c 65
\p
\v 1 {This is what Yahweh replied:}
\q1 “I was ready to respond to my people,
\q2 but they did not ask me to help them.
\q2 I kept telling them that I was available,
\q2 but they were a nation that did not pray to me.
\q1 \v 2 I kept trying to encourage
\q2 people who were rebelling against me {to obey me again}.
\q1 But they kept doing evil things.
\q2 They did whatever they wanted to do.
\q1 \v 3 Those people kept making me angry blatantly.
\q2 They offered sacrifices {to idols} in gardens,
\q1 and they made brick altars on which to burn incense.
\q1 \v 4 They sat among graves
\q2 and spent the night in caves
\q1 {trying to talk with the spirits of dead people}.
\q2 They ate the meat of pigs,
\q1 and their pots were full of broth from other unclean meats.
\q1 \v 5 But they still said to others,
\q2 ‘Stay away from me and do not touch me,
\q1 because I am holier than you {and you would make me unclean}.’
\q1 Those people continually made me angry,
\q2 the way smoke irritates a person’s nose.
\q1 \v 6 Pay attention! I have recorded what they have done.
\q2 I will certainly act. Indeed, I will punish them,
\q1 and I will do that just as they deserve
\q1 \v 7 for what they and their ancestors both did wrong,”
\q2 says Yahweh.
\q1 “They mocked me by burning incense {to their idols}
\q2 on the mountains and the hills.
\q1 So I will punish them fully as they deserve
\q2 for these sins that they have committed.”
\b
\q1 \v 8 Yahweh also said,
\q1 “When someone finds that there is still some good juice in a cluster of grapes,
\q2 he says, ‘Do not throw it away,
\q2 because there is still something good in it.’
\q1 In the same way, I will not destroy all of my people,
\q2 because some of them still serve me.
\q1 \v 9 Instead, I bring back some of the descendants of Jacob
\q2 who are from the tribe of Judah,
\q2 and they will once again possess my mountainous land.
\q1 The people whom I have chosen will possess that land.
\q2 Yes, those who serve me will live there.
\q1 \v 10 Some of my people have remained loyal to me.
\q2 The Plain of Sharon will become pastureland for their flocks,
\q2 and the Valley of Achor will become a resting place for their herds.
\q1 \v 11 But it will be different for you who have abandoned me, Yahweh,
\q2 who no longer worship me, the one whose temple is on Mount Zion.
\q1 Instead, you set out food on tables for the god Fortune,
\q2 and you fill bowls with mixed wine for the god Destiny.
\q1 \v 12 So I will make you die violently.
\q2 You will all fall to the ground when someone slaughters you.
\q1 I will do this because you did not obey me
\q2 when I spoke to you and gave you instructions.
\q1 Instead, you did things that I consider evil,
\q2 and you chose to do things that do not please me.”
\b
\q1 \v 13 Therefore, this is what Yahweh, the Lord, says:
\q1 “Listen! Those who serve me will have food to eat,
\q2 but you will go hungry.
\q1 Those who serve me will have wine to drink,
\q2 but you will go thirsty.
\q1 Those who serve me will celebrate,
\q2 but you will feel shame.
\q1 \v 14 Those who serve me will shout joyfully because they feel so happy,
\q2 but you will cry out because you feel so badly.
\q2 You will wail because your sufferings will make you feel despair.
\q1 \v 15 After you die, the people whom I have chosen
\q2 will curse others by saying they wish they were like you.
\q1 I, Yahweh, the Lord, will kill you,
\q2 but I will give a new name to those who serve me.
\q1 \v 16 People will forget the troubles that happened before.
\q2 That is because I will consider that they never happened at all.
\q1 Then anyone in the land who asks for a blessing
\q2 will ask me, the God who is faithful, to bless him.
\q1 Anyone who makes a solemn promise in the land
\q2 will swear by me, the God who is faithful.
\q1 \v 17 Pay attention! It is as if I am about to create
\q2 a new sky and a new earth.
\q1 You will not remember the things that happened before,
\q2 and you will not feel badly about them.
\q1 \v 18 Instead, be glad and celebrate forever
\q2 because of what I am about to create,
\q1 because I am going to make Jerusalem a place of celebration.
\q2 I will make the people who live there happy.
\q1 \v 19 I will celebrate because of Jerusalem,
\q2 and I will be glad because of my people.
\q1 The people of that city will not weep
\q2 or cry out in distress anymore.
\b
\q1 \v 20 No baby will die there anymore after living for only a few days.
\q2 People will live a full lifetime.
\q1 If someone is 100 years old when he dies,
\q2 people will feel that he died as a young man.
\q2 If someone dies before he is 100 years old,
\q2 people will conclude that God punished him for his sins.
\q1 \v 21 My people will build houses and then live in them.
\q2 They will plant vineyards and then eat the grapes from those vineyards.
\q1 \v 22 Someone else will not live in the houses that they build.
\q2 Someone else will not eat the crops that grow from what they plant.
\q1 Instead, my people will live for a long time, just as trees live for a long time.
\q2 They will live so long that they will wear out the things they make for themselves.
\q1 \v 23 They will not work hard and get nothing in return.
\q2 Their children will not die from sudden disasters.
\q1 That is because they are the descendants of
\q2 people whom Yahweh has blessed,
\q2 and so are their children.
\q1 \v 24 Then I will start to help them even before they ask me for help.
\q2 Even while they are still praying, I will do what they request.
\q1 \v 25 Wolves and lambs will eat grass together peacefully;
\q2 and lions will eat straw as oxen do.
\q2 But snakes will eat only dust.
\q1 No one will harm anyone or destroy anything
\q2 anywhere on the mountain where I am present in a special way,”
\q2 says Yahweh.
\c 66
\p
\v 1 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 “I rule over everything in heaven,
\q2 and I have authority over the whole earth.
\q1 You could never build a temple
\q2 that would be great enough for me to live in!
\q1 \v 2 By my power, I made everything in heaven and on earth.
\q2 That is why those things exist.”
\q2 This is what Yahweh declares.
\b
\q1 I pay attention to people who are genuinely humble,
\q2 and who carefully obey my commands.
\q1 \v 3 {Because you continue to sin even though you worship me,}
\q1 when someone slaughters an ox {as a sacrifice},
\q2 that is as bad as if he had killed someone.
\q1 When someone sacrifices a lamb to me,
\q2 that is as bad as if he had killed a dog {to sacrifice to me}.
\q1 When someone brings a grain offering to me,
\q2 that is as bad as if he had offered pig’s blood to me.
\q1 When someone burns incense to me,
\q2 that is as bad as if he had worshiped an idol.
\q1 These people have chosen to commit sins.
\q2 They enjoy doing those wrong things.
\q1 \v 4 So I will choose to punish them,
\q2 I will make things happen to them that terrify them.
\q1 I will do that because when I warned them not to keep sinning,
\q2 they did not respond by repenting.
\q1 Instead, they kept doing what I consider evil.
\q2 Yes, they chose to do what I hate.”
\q1 \v 5 Listen to what Yahweh says,
\q2 you people who carefully obey his commands:
\q1 “Some of your own relatives hate you and shun you
\q2 because you belong to me.
\q1 They mock you by saying,
\q2 ‘Yahweh should show how great he is!
\q2 We want to see him make you happy!’
\q1 But those people are going to feel shame for saying that.”
\q1 \v 6 Listen! There is a loud noise coming from the city!
\q2 A noise is coming from the temple!
\q1 It is the sound of Yahweh
\q2 punishing his enemies for what they did!
\b
\q1 \v 7 The city of Jerusalem is like a woman
\q2 who had a baby boy
\q2 without having any pains or going into labor.
\q1 \v 8 This is something that no one has seen or heard of before.
\q1 A whole nation has appeared in a very short time!
\q1 There are many people in Judah once again,
\q2 as if Zion had given birth to them all at once.
\q1 \v 9 Yahweh, our God, says,
\q2 “It was as if Zion was a woman whom I made ready to have a baby.
\q2 I certainly would not have stopped her from having that baby!”
\q1 \v 10 Everyone who loves Jerusalem,
\q2 rejoice greatly together with the people of that city!
\q2 Everyone who was sad because of what had happened to that city,
\q2 celebrate with its people now!
\q1 \v 11 You may enjoy everything that Jerusalem provides.
\q2 That will satisfy you completely.
\q1 Yes, you may take great pleasure
\q2 in all the wonderful things the city has to offer.
\p
\v 12 That is true because Yahweh says this:
\q1 “I will make Jerusalem prosper continually.
\q2 I will have the people of other nations bring their wealth
\q2 to the city in great quantities.
\q1 I will take care of you
\q2 the way a mother lovingly holds her baby on her hip
\q2 and bounces her child on her knees.
\q1 \v 13 I will comfort you people of Jerusalem
\q2 the way a mother comforts her child.”
\b
\q1 \v 14 When you see these things happen,
\q2 you will be very happy,
\q1 and you will become healthy and strong again.
\q1 Yahweh will use his power to help those who serve him,
\q2 but he will punish his enemies.
\q1 \v 15 Pay attention! Yahweh will destroy his enemies as if he were burning them up.
\q2 It will be as if he commands chariots
\q2 that sweep them away like a windstorm.
\q1 He will punish them for doing things that made him so angry,
\q2 and it will be as if he were burning them up.
\q1 \v 16 Yes, it will be as if Yahweh is punishing his enemies by burning them up
\q2 or by using a sword to kill them.
\q2 Yahweh will kill many people.
\p
\v 17 Yahweh declares, “Some of you perform rituals to make yourselves acceptable for worship, and then you go into gardens to worship idols. You follow your leader into those places. You eat the meat of pigs, mice, and other things that I have forbidden you to eat. So I will destroy all of you for doing those things.”
\p
\v 18 {Yahweh also says,} “I know what these people are doing and what they are thinking. Very soon I will gather together people from many different nations who speak many different languages. They will come and see how glorious I am.
\p
\v 19 I will do a sign among them, and I will send some of them as eyewitnesses to distant countries. I will send them to Tarshish, Pul, Lud (whose people are great archers), Tubal, Javan, and distant lands by the sea. The people of those nations have not heard about me and have not seen how glorious I am. The people I send out will tell them how glorious I am.
\v 20 Then they will send back all of your fellow Israelites from every nation to which their enemies took them. It will be as if they are sending an offering to me, Yahweh. They will send them on horses, in chariots, in wagons, on mules, and on camels to the city of Jerusalem. I am present there in a special way on Mount Zion.” Yahweh says, “They will return your relatives just as the people of Israel bring grain offerings to my temple in clean containers.
\v 21 Moreover, I will choose some of them to serve as priests and Levites,” says Yahweh.
\b
\q1 \v 22 “The new heavens and the new earth that I am making
\q2 will last forever in my presence,” declares Yahweh.
\q1 “In the same way, you will always have living descendants,
\q2 and you will always be a distinct nation.
\q1 \v 23 Yahweh says, “On each new moon and on each Sabbath,
\q2 people from throughout the world will come and worship me at my temple,”
\q1 \v 24 When they leave Jerusalem, they will see
\q2 the dead bodies of the people who rebelled against me.
\q1 Maggots will keep eating their bodies,
\q2 and fire will keep burning them up.
\q2 Everyone who sees them will feel disgusted.” 